(window.__DP=window.__DP||[])[1]=" indications rather than long-term first-line HMB control.\",\"NSAIDs reduce prostaglandin-mediated bleeding and dysmenorrhea but are generally less effective for blood-loss reduction than the LNG-IUS.\",\"The LNG-IUS markedly reduces endometrial proliferation and menstrual blood loss and is first-line for many patients with HMB and no major cavity-distorting pathology.\",\"Tranexamic acid reduces fibrinolysis and menstrual blood loss but does not provide the sustained local endometrial suppression of an LNG-IUS.\",\"Combined hormonal contraception can reduce bleeding, but obesity and age may increase thrombotic risk and the LNG-IUS is generally more effective for HMB.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding management is guided by structural pathology, symptoms, fertility goals, and treatment contraindications.\",\"In a patient with no major intracavitary pathology who wants effective long-term treatment, the LNG-IUS is a preferred first-line option and also treats dysmenorrhea.\",\"Continuous local levonorgestrel causes endometrial decidualization and atrophy, substantially reducing menstrual blood loss while limiting systemic exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"The LNG-IUS is a highly effective first-line treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding when the uterine cavity is suitable.\",\"pr\":\"Bleeding can be irregular during the first months after LNG-IUS insertion before the longer-term reduction in menstrual loss becomes evident.\",\"tp\":\"Before insertion, exclude pregnancy and investigate any endometrial-risk features or unexplained bleeding that require sampling.\",\"cn\":\"The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) is the preferred first-line medical treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) without major cavity distortion.\",\"sl\":\"First-line medical options for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) are the levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS), tranexamic acid, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and combined hormonal contraception. The Mirena intrauterine system (IUS) reduces blood loss by up to 90% and about 30% are amenorrhoeic at 12 months.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[17,27]],[\"L33_0\",[62]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F085\"},{\"id\":\"F086\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hyperemesis and gastrointestinal disease\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding gastrointestinal disease in pregnancy, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is more common in first pregnancies\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is characterized by only mild vomiting, less than 5% weight loss, and no dehydration\",\"Gastroesophageal reflux symptoms are common during pregnancy\",\"Pregnancy may reduce the frequency of peptic ulcer disease symptoms\",\"Aspiration risk is increased during labor and obstetric anesthesia\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum (HG) is associated with several factors including nulliparity and multiple gestation, although recurrence can occur in later pregnancies.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum involves severe persistent nausea/vomiting with clinically important dehydration, nutritional compromise, or weight loss; mild symptoms do not define it.\",\"Reduced lower-esophageal sphincter tone and mechanical pressure make gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD) very common as gestation advances.\",\"Pregnancy can be relatively protective against peptic ulcer disease, partly through changes in gastric secretion and mucosal physiology.\",\"Pregnancy and labor increase risk of regurgitation and acid aspiration, especially with general anesthesia and delayed gastric emptying.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy causes major gastrointestinal physiologic changes, including reduced lower-esophageal sphincter tone and altered gastric motility.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is at the severe end of the nausea-vomiting spectrum and can cause dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, nutritional deficiency, and significant weight loss.\",\"Severe vomiting produces volume depletion and starvation, so treatment emphasizes rehydration, thiamine when indicated, antiemetics, and correction of metabolic abnormalities.\"],\"tk\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is a clinically significant vomiting disorder, not mild nausea with trivial weight change.\",\"pr\":\"Give thiamine before dextrose in prolonged severe vomiting to reduce the risk of Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Check weight trend, hydration, electrolytes, renal function, and ketone/nutritional status when vomiting is persistent or severe.\",\"cn\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe vomiting with dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, ketonuria and weight loss over 5%, not mild vomiting.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is a severe form of nausea and vomiting with intractable vomiting, dehydration, alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia and weight loss usually more than 5% of pre-pregnant weight; it affects 0.5-2% of pregnancies.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F086\"},{\"id\":\"F087\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate toxicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is least consistent with magnesium sulfate toxicity?\",\"o\":[\"Epigastric tenderness\",\"Cardiac conduction failure or arrest\",\"Loss of patellar reflexes\",\"Respiratory depression or failure\",\"Slurred speech and marked neuromuscular weakness\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Epigastric or right-upper-quadrant tenderness suggests hepatic involvement in severe pre-eclampsia rather than magnesium toxicity.\",\"Severe hypermagnesemia can progress to conduction abnormalities and cardiac arrest.\",\"Loss of deep-tendon reflexes is an early clinical sign of rising magnesium concentration.\",\"Higher magnesium levels impair neuromuscular transmission and can cause respiratory depression.\",\"Excess magnesium can produce central and neuromuscular depression, causing weakness and dysarthria.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate requires toxicity surveillance because accumulation can occur when renal clearance is impaired or dosing is excessive.\",\"Early toxicity causes loss of deep-tendon reflexes, followed by respiratory depression and, at very high levels, cardiac conduction failure.\",\"Magnesium suppresses neuromuscular transmission, which is why respiratory rate, reflexes, and urine output are monitored during therapy.\"],\"tk\":\"Loss of reflexes and respiratory depression are classic magnesium-toxicity findings; epigastric pain instead suggests severe pre-eclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Calcium gluconate is used as the antidote for clinically significant magnesium toxicity.\",\"tp\":\"Before each maintenance dose or during infusion, check respiratory rate, patellar reflexes, and urine output, especially if renal function is impaired.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium toxicity causes loss of patellar reflexes, respiratory depression and cardiac arrest; epigastric pain indicates severe pre-eclampsia instead.\",\"sl\":\"Monitor hourly for magnesium toxicity: loss of patellar reflexes (8-10 mEq/L), respiratory depression or arrest (12 mEq/L), mental status changes (12 mEq/L) followed by electrocardiogram (ECG) changes and arrhythmias. Treat with 1 g calcium gluconate intravenously (IV) over 3 minutes.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[47,37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F087\"},{\"id\":\"F088\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Hypothyroidism monitoring in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a pregnant patient with treated primary hypothyroidism, which laboratory value is most important for adjusting levothyroxine dosage?\",\"o\":[\"Triiodothyronine resin uptake\",\"Thyroid-binding globulin\",\"Long-acting thyroid stimulator\",\"Total free thyroxine without TSH context\",\"Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"T3 resin uptake is an older indirect test affected by thyroid-binding proteins and is not standard dose monitoring in pregnancy.\",\"Pregnancy raises thyroid-binding globulin through estrogen effects, but TBG concentration does not guide levothyroxine dosing.\",\"LATS refers historically to TSH-receptor-stimulating immunoglobulins associated with Graves disease, not levothyroxine dose titration.\",\"Free T4 can supplement assessment, but isolated measurement does not replace TSH-guided monitoring in primary hypothyroidism.\",\"TSH is the principal marker used to titrate levothyroxine, interpreted against pregnancy-specific thyroid targets.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases thyroid hormone requirements and alters binding proteins, making planned biochemical monitoring essential in known hypothyroidism.\",\"TSH is the main marker for levothyroxine adjustment, with free T4 used when additional biochemical context is needed.\",\"Adequate maternal thyroxine is especially important early in gestation because the fetus depends on maternal T4 before its own thyroid function is established.\"],\"tk\":\"Levothyroxine dosage in primary hypothyroidism is adjusted primarily using pregnancy-appropriate TSH targets.\",\"pr\":\"Women already taking levothyroxine often require an early dose increase after pregnancy is confirmed.\",\"tp\":\"Check thyroid function early after conception and at regular intervals after dose changes rather than waiting for symptoms.\",\"cn\":\"Levothyroxine dose in pregnant women with hypothyroidism is titrated to thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) using trimester-specific ranges.\",\"sl\":\"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) doubles, so total T4 and T3 rise about 50%; use free hormones and trimester-specific ranges. Hypothyroid women need about 25-50% more levothyroxine from early pregnancy. Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) falls in the 1st trimester; use trimester ranges.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[49,58]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F088\"},{\"id\":\"F089\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Antihypertensive medication safety\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is considered compatible with pregnancy when clinically indicated?\",\"o\":[\"High-dose vitamin A\",\"Live rubella vaccine\",\"Warfarin\",\"Methyldopa\",\"Tetracycline\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Excess preformed vitamin A has retinoid teratogenicity, particularly during organogenesis.\",\"The live attenuated rubella vaccine is avoided during pregnancy and given postpartum to susceptible patients.\",\"Warfarin crosses the placenta and can cause fetal warfarin syndrome and fetal hemorrhage.\",\"Methyldopa has a long history of use in pregnancy and lowers blood pressure through central alpha2-adrenergic agonism.\",\"Tetracyclines can affect fetal bone and tooth development and are generally avoided in pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Medication selection in pregnancy balances maternal benefit against placental transfer and fetal toxicity.\",\"Methyldopa is a pregnancy-compatible antihypertensive, while warfarin, live rubella vaccine, tetracyclines, and retinoid-level vitamin A exposures have important fetal concerns.\",\"The placenta permits transfer of many small molecules; low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) and unfractionated heparin are notable anticoagulant exceptions because they do not cross it.\"],\"tk\":\"Methyldopa is an established antihypertensive option during pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"After delivery, antihypertensive selection can change because fetal exposure through the placenta is no longer relevant, although breastfeeding safety still matters.\",\"tp\":\"Review every medication at the first antenatal visit, including vaccines, supplements, and over-the-counter drugs.\",\"cn\":\"Methyldopa is a pregnancy-compatible antihypertensive; warfarin, tetracycline, live vaccines and high-dose vitamin A are avoided.\",\"sl\":\"Antenatal antihypertensives: labetalol, nifedipine and methyldopa (centrally acting alpha-2 agonist; may cause tiredness, headache, depression, occasionally hepatitis). Warfarin crosses the placenta and causes warfarin embryopathy (nasal hypoplasia, skeletal abnormalities).\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[11]],[\"L11_0\",[26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F089\"},{\"id\":\"F090\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Menopausal hormone therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding menopausal hormone therapy, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Oral hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is associated with more gallbladder disease than non-use\",\"Transdermal HRT is useful when venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk makes oral estrogen less desirable\",\"Transdermal systemic HRT is preferred when the only symptom is isolated vaginal dryness\",\"Transdermal HRT avoids hepatic first-pass metabolism\",\"Systemic HRT reduces fragility-fracture risk while treatment continues\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oral estrogen increases hepatic exposure and is associated with increased gallbladder disease through biliary cholesterol effects.\",\"Transdermal estrogen avoids first-pass hepatic effects and has a more favorable venous thromboembolism risk profile than oral estrogen.\",\"For isolated genitourinary symptoms, local vaginal estrogen is preferred because it delivers low-dose treatment directly to affected tissues; systemic transdermal HRT is unnecessary.\",\"Skin delivery bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and first-pass hepatic metabolism, producing different effects on clotting proteins and triglycerides.\",\"Estrogen reduces bone resorption and lowers osteoporotic fracture risk during treatment.\"],\"e\":[\"Menopausal hormone therapy route selection should be matched to symptoms, comorbidities, and treatment goals.\",\"For isolated genitourinary syndrome of menopause, vaginal estrogen provides local therapy with minimal systemic exposure, whereas transdermal systemic HRT is considered for broader menopausal symptoms.\",\"Avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism makes transdermal estrogen attractive when venous thromboembolism (VTE) or metabolic risk influences route selection.\"],\"tk\":\"Local vaginal estrogen is preferred for isolated genitourinary menopausal symptoms rather than unnecessary systemic HRT.\",\"pr\":\"Transdermal estrogen generally has less effect on hepatic clotting-factor synthesis than oral estrogen.\",\"tp\":\"Ask whether symptoms are local, vasomotor, or both before choosing local versus systemic estrogen therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Isolated vaginal dryness is treated with low-dose vaginal estrogen, not systemic transdermal hormone replacement therapy (HRT).\",\"sl\":\"Vaginal estrogen is indicated for genitourinary syndrome (vaginal dryness, dyspareunia, urgency, recurrent cystitis) and needs no endometrial protection. Transdermal estrogen avoids the first-pass liver effect, with less gallbladder disease and coagulation effect; hormone replacement therapy (HRT) cuts vertebral and hip fracture by 25-50%.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[25,26,28,31]],[\"L34_1\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F090\"},{\"id\":\"F091\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Mechanical causes of obstructed labor\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is least likely to cause mechanical obstruction of labor?\",\"o\":[\"Bony pelvic tumor\",\"Umbilical hernia\",\"Lower-segment uterine fibroid\",\"Impacted ovarian cyst in the pouch of Douglas\",\"Pelvic kidney\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A large pelvic bone lesion can distort pelvic dimensions and create mechanical obstruction.\",\"An umbilical hernia lies in the abdominal wall and therefore does not narrow the maternal bony pelvis or birth canal.\",\"A large lower-segment fibroid can physically impede engagement or descent of the fetal presenting part.\",\"A persistent pelvic adnexal mass can occupy the birth canal and obstruct descent.\",\"An ectopic pelvic kidney can present as a fixed pelvic mass occupying the birth canal.\"],\"e\":[\"Obstructed labor is caused by mechanical failure of descent despite adequate contractions when a barrier compromises the birth canal.\",\"Maternal pelvic masses such as a lower-segment fibroid, impacted ovarian mass, pelvic kidney, or bony lesion can narrow the birth canal.\",\"An abdominal-wall defect such as an umbilical hernia lies outside the pelvic passage and therefore does not create cephalopelvic obstruction.\"],\"tk\":\"Mechanical obstruction of labor requires a lesion that compromises the maternal pelvis or birth canal.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent failure of descent despite adequate contractions should prompt reassessment of fetal position, pelvic adequacy, and occult pelvic masses.\",\"tp\":\"Before operative vaginal birth, confirm that there is no mechanical obstruction and that the presenting part is sufficiently low.\",\"cn\":\"Mechanical obstruction of labour requires a lesion in the pelvis or birth canal; an umbilical hernia does not obstruct.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD) include contracted or small pelvis and obstructive masses in the maternal pelvis; maternal factors for malpresentation include pelvic tumours and uterine fibroids.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[10,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F091\"},{\"id\":\"F092\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"True versus false labor\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"True labor is characterized by all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"Increasing contraction duration\",\"Regular contractions\",\"Progressive cervical dilatation\",\"Rupture of membranes must occur before contractions begin\",\"Increasing contraction intensity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The duration of effective uterine contractions generally lengthens with labor progression.\",\"True labor typically develops a regular, coordinated contraction pattern rather than irregular Braxton Hicks activity.\",\"True labor produces progressive cervical change, including dilatation and effacement.\",\"Membrane rupture can occur before, during, or after the onset of true labor and is not required to precede contractions.\",\"Contractions usually become stronger as active labor progresses.\"],\"e\":[\"True labor is defined by regular effective contractions that produce progressive cervical dilatation and effacement.\",\"Contractions become more coordinated, frequent, and intense, but rupture of membranes is not required before labor begins.\",\"Cervical change is the decisive physiologic consequence because coordinated myometrial activity generates the force needed for fetal descent.\"],\"tk\":\"Progressive cervical change distinguishes true labor from irregular nonprogressive contractions.\",\"pr\":\"Pain intensity alone cannot diagnose labor; repeated examination may be needed when contractions are present but cervical change is uncertain.\",\"tp\":\"Assess contraction pattern, cervical change, membrane status, and fetal wellbeing together rather than using membrane rupture as the defining criterion.\",\"cn\":\"True labour is regular, increasingly strong contractions causing progressive cervical change; rupture of membranes is not required.\",\"sl\":\"True labour: regular contractions that get closer and stronger with time. Labour is diagnosed when painful regular contractions lead to effacement and dilatation of the cervix, commonly accompanied by a show and/or rupture of membranes.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[23,30,31]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F092\"},{\"id\":\"F093\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a woman with a regular 22-day menstrual cycle, around which day is serum progesterone expected to peak?\",\"o\":[\"Day 14\",\"Day 16\",\"Day 21\",\"Day 7\",\"Day 15\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"This is approximately 6 days after predicted ovulation and may be rising, but the classic mid-luteal peak is about 7 days after ovulation.\",\"This is slightly later than the expected 7-day post-ovulation peak for a 22-day cycle.\",\"Day 21 is appropriate only as a fixed teaching point for a 28-day cycle; shorter cycles require timing relative to the next menses.\",\"Day 7 is near the predicted ovulation window in a 22-day cycle, before the corpus luteum reaches maximal progesterone output.\",\"Ovulation usually occurs about 14 days before the next menses; in a 22-day cycle that is near day 8, so mid-luteal progesterone peaks roughly 7 days later around day 15.\"],\"e\":[\"Progesterone is secreted by the corpus luteum after ovulation and peaks in the mid-luteal phase.\",\"Because the luteal phase is relatively constant at about 14 days, ovulation in a 22-day cycle is expected near day 8 and progesterone peaks about a week later.\",\"This is why mid-luteal progesterone should be timed approximately 7 days before the expected next period, not automatically on cycle day 21.\"],\"tk\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone is timed relative to ovulation or the next expected menses rather than by a fixed cycle day.\",\"pr\":\"A day-21 progesterone test is only appropriately timed for a typical 28-day cycle.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating ovulation, ask the patient’s usual cycle length before choosing the progesterone sampling date.\",\"cn\":\"In a 22-day cycle ovulation is near day 8 (cycle length minus 14), so progesterone peaks around day 15.\",\"sl\":\"Peak levels of progesterone are attained 7 days after ovulation; the luteal (progesterone) phase is fixed at 14 days while the follicular phase is variable.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[10,11]],[\"L33_1\",[2,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F026\"},{\"id\":\"F094\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate monitoring\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis in pre-eclampsia, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Magnesium toxicity cannot occur when baseline renal function is normal\",\"Severe headache and sustained clonus may precede eclampsia\",\"Magnesium sulfate is the preferred drug for prevention and treatment of eclamptic seizures\",\"Magnesium is cleared predominantly through the kidneys\",\"Respiratory rate, reflexes, and urine output should be monitored during treatment\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Toxicity can still occur with excess dosing, acute renal impairment, or medication error even when baseline renal function is normal.\",\"Neurologic symptoms such as severe headache, visual disturbance, and clonus indicate cerebral irritability and increased seizure risk.\",\"Randomized evidence supports magnesium sulfate because it lowers recurrent eclamptic seizure risk compared with alternative anticonvulsants.\",\"Magnesium is eliminated mainly by renal excretion, so oliguria increases toxicity risk.\",\"Bedside monitoring of respiration, deep-tendon reflexes, and urine output helps detect accumulation before severe toxicity.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate is the standard anticonvulsant for eclampsia prevention and treatment when clinically indicated.\",\"Because magnesium is cleared renally, toxicity risk increases with oliguria or renal impairment, but excessive administration can cause toxicity even with previously normal function.\",\"Progressive neuromuscular blockade causes loss of reflexes, respiratory depression, and eventually cardiac toxicity, making bedside monitoring essential.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal baseline renal function does not eliminate the possibility of magnesium sulfate toxicity.\",\"pr\":\"Loss of patellar reflexes generally precedes respiratory arrest and provides an early bedside warning sign.\",\"tp\":\"Reduce or stop magnesium and reassess urgently if urine output falls, reflexes disappear, or respiratory rate declines.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) has a narrow therapeutic window, so toxicity can occur even with normal renal function; monitor reflexes, respiration and levels.\",\"sl\":\"Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) has a narrow therapeutic window (4-6 mEq/L); check serum magnesium 4 hours after the loading dose then every 6 hours as needed, and monitor hourly for toxicity (loss of patellar reflexes, respiratory depression).\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[45,46,47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F094\"},{\"id\":\"F095\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Uterine rupture recognition\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a laboring patient at risk of uterine rupture, which finding is the most consistent early sign?\",\"o\":[\"Hematuria\",\"Vaginal bleeding\",\"Abnormal fetal heart-rate tracing\",\"Maternal cardiovascular collapse\",\"Sudden abdominal pain\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hematuria suggests bladder or urinary tract involvement and is not the typical initial manifestation.\",\"Bleeding may occur, but intra-abdominal hemorrhage can be concealed and vaginal loss may be modest.\",\"The most frequent early manifestation is fetal heart-rate abnormality, often sudden bradycardia or prolonged deceleration from acute interruption of uteroplacental perfusion.\",\"Shock is a late severe manifestation after major hemorrhage rather than the most consistent early sign.\",\"Pain can occur, but it may be masked by epidural analgesia and is not as consistently present as fetal heart-rate change.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine rupture is an obstetric emergency, especially in patients laboring with a scarred uterus.\",\"The most consistent early clue is an abnormal fetal heart-rate pattern, often abrupt bradycardia, before dramatic maternal signs appear.\",\"Rupture can abruptly reduce uteroplacental perfusion and cause fetal hypoxia while maternal bleeding may initially be concealed.\"],\"tk\":\"Sudden fetal heart-rate abnormality is the most consistent early sign of uterine rupture.\",\"pr\":\"Loss of fetal station can accompany rupture when the presenting part retracts from the pelvis.\",\"tp\":\"During trial of labour after caesarean (TOLAC), treat sudden persistent bradycardia or a prolonged deceleration as an emergency requiring immediate obstetric assessment.\",\"cn\":\"An abnormal fetal heart rate tracing (cardiotocography, CTG) is the most consistent early sign of uterine rupture.\",\"sl\":\"Symptoms of uterine rupture are often non-specific: abdominal pain between contractions, with or without bleeding, loss of station, inability to identify contractions, haematuria and blood-stained liquor, cardiotocography (CTG) abnormalities or lost fetal heart rate (FHR).\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[57]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F095\"},{\"id\":\"F096\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Preterm birth risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not a well-established risk factor for spontaneous preterm birth?\",\"o\":[\"Pyelonephritis\",\"Invasive intrauterine procedures such as amniocentesis\",\"Previous spontaneous preterm birth\",\"Multiple pregnancy\",\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. Pyelonephritis causes systemic inflammation and uterine irritability and is an established risk factor for spontaneous preterm birth.\",\"Wrong. Invasive intrauterine procedures such as amniocentesis carry a small risk of membrane rupture, infection and preterm delivery, so they are a recognized risk factor.\",\"Wrong. A previous spontaneous preterm birth is one of the strongest predictors of recurrent spontaneous preterm birth.\",\"Wrong. Multiple pregnancy causes uterine overdistension and is a major, well-established risk factor for preterm birth.\",\"Correct. Uncomplicated vulvovaginal candidiasis is not an established risk factor for spontaneous preterm birth, unlike bacterial vaginosis or urinary tract infection.\"],\"e\":[\"Preterm birth has multiple pathways, including cervical insufficiency, infection, uterine overdistension, hemorrhage, and recurrence after prior prematurity.\",\"Strong risk factors include previous preterm birth, multifetal pregnancy, and significant maternal infection.\",\"Simple Candida vaginitis does not produce the same established ascending-inflammatory pathway associated with bacterial vaginosis, chorioamnionitis, or pyelonephritis.\"],\"tk\":\"Risk factors for spontaneous preterm birth include prior preterm birth, uterine overdistension, and significant maternal infection.\",\"pr\":\"Bacterial vaginosis and untreated urinary infection have different obstetric implications from uncomplicated vulvovaginal candidiasis.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about prior gestational age at birth because recurrence prevention depends heavily on the phenotype of the previous preterm birth.\",\"cn\":\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis is not an established risk factor for spontaneous preterm birth, unlike previous preterm birth, multiple pregnancy and infection.\",\"sl\":\"History risk factors: previous preterm birth (PTB), previous preterm prelabour rupture of membranes, mid-trimester loss, uterine anomalies, multiple pregnancy, bleeding, cervical procedures, smoking. Infection/inflammation causes 20-40% of spontaneous preterm labour.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[9,13,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F096\"},{\"id\":\"F097\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":43,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Vulvar carcinoma epidemiology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding vulvar carcinoma, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Most invasive vulvar cancers occur in older women\",\"Squamous cell carcinoma is the predominant histologic type\",\"Persistent suspicious vulvar lesions should be biopsied\",\"Lichen sclerosus is the major pathway to vulvar carcinoma in women younger than 45 years\",\"Vulvar carcinoma accounts for a small minority of gynecologic cancers\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Incidence rises with age, and many cases occur in postmenopausal women, although HPV-related disease may present younger.\",\"Around 90% or more of vulvar cancers are squamous cell carcinomas.\",\"A lesion with ulceration, induration, color change, or persistent symptoms requires histologic diagnosis by biopsy.\",\"In younger patients, vulvar squamous neoplasia is more often associated with high-risk HPV and smoking; lichen sclerosus is linked mainly to the HPV-independent pathway in older women.\",\"Vulvar malignancy comprises only a few percent of gynecologic cancers.\"],\"e\":[\"Vulvar squamous carcinoma develops through two major biologic pathways, one HPV-associated and one HPV-independent.\",\"HPV-associated disease is more common in younger women and is linked to smoking and high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions, whereas HPV-independent disease often arises with lichen sclerosus in older women.\",\"Persistent epithelial injury and molecular alterations such as TP53 pathway abnormalities contribute to HPV-independent carcinogenesis.\"],\"tk\":\"Vulvar cancer in younger women is more strongly associated with HPV-related disease than with lichen sclerosus.\",\"pr\":\"Chronic vulvar pruritus in an older patient should not be treated indefinitely without examining for architectural change or neoplasia.\",\"tp\":\"Biopsy any persistent, ulcerated, pigmented, indurated, or otherwise suspicious vulvar lesion before definitive treatment.\",\"cn\":\"In women under 50, vulvar cancer is mainly human papillomavirus (HPV)-related; lichen sclerosus is the pathway in older women.\",\"sl\":\"Etiology: pre-existing dermatosis (lichen sclerosus, vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia [VIN]) and human papillomavirus (HPV), usually type 16, accounting for 80-90% under the age of 50. Vulvar cancer is rare, a disease of the older age group, majority squamous.\",\"s\":[[\"L43_0\",[3,2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F097\"},{\"id\":\"F098\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pre-eclampsia risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are recognized risk factors for pre-eclampsia except:\",\"o\":[\"Nulliparity\",\"Cigarette smoking\",\"Chronic hypertension\",\"Chronic kidney disease\",\"Type 2 diabetes mellitus\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Nulliparity is a moderate-risk factor reflecting a first placental implantation and maternal vascular adaptation.\",\"Smoking is not used as a pre-eclampsia risk factor in current prophylaxis algorithms despite its many serious fetal and maternal harms.\",\"Chronic hypertension is a high-risk condition for superimposed pre-eclampsia and placental dysfunction.\",\"Renal disease is a high-risk factor because of baseline vascular and renal dysfunction.\",\"Pregestational diabetes increases placental and endothelial dysfunction, raising pre-eclampsia risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Pre-eclampsia prevention begins with structured antenatal risk-factor assessment.\",\"High-risk conditions include chronic hypertension, kidney disease, and pregestational diabetes, while nulliparity is a moderate-risk factor.\",\"Smoking is profoundly harmful in pregnancy but is not used as a positive pre-eclampsia risk criterion in aspirin-prophylaxis algorithms.\"],\"tk\":\"Current pre-eclampsia risk algorithms include chronic hypertension, renal disease, diabetes, and nulliparity, but not smoking.\",\"pr\":\"Risk-factor lists should guide aspirin decisions, but smoking cessation remains essential because smoking increases fetal-growth restriction, placental abruption, and stillbirth risk.\",\"tp\":\"Do not misinterpret the absence of smoking from a pre-eclampsia risk list as evidence that smoking is safe in pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Cigarette smoking is not a pre-eclampsia risk factor; nulliparity, chronic hypertension, chronic kidney disease and diabetes are.\",\"sl\":\"High risk: chronic hypertension, pregestational diabetes, chronic kidney disease, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), antiphospholipid syndrome (APS), previous pre-eclampsia. Moderate risk: age over 40, body mass index (BMI) 35 or more, nulliparity, interpregnancy interval over 10 years, multiple gestation, family history. Smoking is not listed.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[32,34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F069\"},{\"id\":\"F099\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fatal complications of pre-eclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which complication is a major direct cause of maternal death in severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"Intracranial hemorrhage\",\"Congestive heart failure\",\"Fever\",\"Urinary tract infection\",\"Uremia alone\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Severe uncontrolled hypertension can rupture cerebral vessels, causing intracranial hemorrhage with rapid neurologic deterioration and maternal death.\",\"Cardiac failure and pulmonary edema can occur, but acute cerebrovascular hemorrhage is a particularly feared direct consequence of severe hypertension.\",\"Fever is not a characteristic fatal mechanism of hypertensive pregnancy disease and suggests infection or another process.\",\"Maternal infection may cause fever, leukocytosis, and sepsis-related organ dysfunction; this represents an infectious rather than hypertensive pathway.\",\"Renal failure can complicate severe disease, but isolated uremia usually evolves with progressive renal dysfunction and biochemical abnormalities rather than abrupt collapse.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe pre-eclampsia can cause catastrophic cerebrovascular and multiorgan complications, including stroke, pulmonary edema, hepatic injury, renal failure, DIC, abruption, and eclampsia.\",\"Persistent severe hypertension greatly increases the risk of intracranial hemorrhage, a major preventable cause of maternal death.\",\"Rapid treatment of severe-range blood pressure aims primarily to reduce catastrophic cerebral vascular injury while definitive management addresses the pregnancy itself.\"],\"tk\":\"Intracranial hemorrhage is a major fatal complication of uncontrolled severe hypertension in pre-eclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Seizure control with magnesium does not replace urgent antihypertensive treatment when blood pressure is severely elevated.\",\"tp\":\"Treat persistent severe-range hypertension promptly and reassess neurologic symptoms such as severe headache, visual change, or altered consciousness.\",\"cn\":\"Intracranial haemorrhage (cerebrovascular accident) from severe hypertension is a major cause of death in pre-eclampsia and eclampsia.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal complications of pre-eclampsia: renal failure, acute cardiac failure, pulmonary oedema, thrombocytopenia, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC), cerebrovascular accidents.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[50]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F099\"},{\"id\":\"F100\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Indications for IVF\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is generally not an immediate indication for in vitro fertilisation (IVF) because a specific medical treatment can often restore natural fertility first?\",\"o\":[\"Severe male factor infertility after counseling and sperm-retrieval planning\",\"Long-standing unexplained infertility meeting guideline criteria\",\"Azoospermia due to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism\",\"Bilateral tubal obstruction\",\"Hydrosalpinx after tubal occlusion or salpingectomy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Severe male-factor disease may require IVF with intracytoplasmic sperm injection when viable sperm are available.\",\"After an adequate period of unsuccessful expectant management, IVF may be offered for unexplained infertility according to access criteria.\",\"Men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism can often achieve spermatogenesis after gonadotropin or pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) therapy, so assisted reproduction may not be the initial step.\",\"When both tubes are blocked, IVF bypasses tubal transport and is an established treatment pathway.\",\"After appropriate management of hydrosalpinx, IVF can bypass the absent or nonfunctional fallopian tubes.\"],\"e\":[\"IVF is used when fertilization or gamete transport cannot be achieved adequately by simpler treatment, after the infertility mechanism has been defined.\",\"Bilateral tubal disease, severe male-factor infertility, and persistent unexplained infertility can justify IVF-based treatment after appropriate evaluation.\",\"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism is different because deficient follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)/luteinising hormone (LH) stimulation can often be replaced medically, restoring spermatogenesis before assisted conception is considered.\"],\"tk\":\"Treatable hypogonadotropic hypogonadism should receive etiologic fertility treatment before defaulting directly to IVF.\",\"pr\":\"In azoospermia, measure gonadotropins and testosterone before labeling the condition as irreversible testicular failure.\",\"tp\":\"Differentiate obstructive, primary testicular, and hypogonadotropic azoospermia because their fertility pathways are fundamentally different.\",\"cn\":\"Men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism should be offered gonadotrophin drugs, which can restore spermatogenesis without in vitro fertilisation (IVF).\",\"sl\":\"Male factor medical management: men with hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism should be offered gonadotrophin drugs. Salpingectomy or tubal disconnection for hydrosalpinges improves in vitro fertilisation (IVF) success; IVF is offered after 2 years of failure to conceive.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[23,30,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F100\"},{\"id\":\"F101\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Follicular phase variability\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During the menstrual cycle, one statement is false:\",\"o\":[\"In the follicular phase, estradiol and inhibin B levels increase.\",\"The luteinising hormone (LH) surge occurs approximately 36 hours before ovulation.\",\"The functional endometrium is shed down to the basalis layer during menstruation.\",\"The follicular phase is always 14 days.\",\"The optimal time to check serum progesterone is about 7 days after ovulation.\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Developing granulosa cells produce increasing estradiol and inhibin B during the follicular phase; inhibin B preferentially suppresses follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).\",\"The midcycle luteinising hormone (LH) surge begins roughly 34–36 hours before ovulation, while the LH peak is closer to 10–12 hours before follicular rupture.\",\"Menstruation sheds the stratum functionalis while the stratum basalis remains to regenerate the endometrium.\",\"The follicular phase is the variable portion of the cycle; the luteal phase is relatively more constant at about 14 days.\",\"Serum progesterone peaks in the mid-luteal phase, approximately 7 days after ovulation, so testing should be timed to the cycle rather than fixed to day 21.\"],\"e\":[\"The menstrual cycle has a variable follicular phase followed by a relatively constant luteal phase of about 14 days; follicular estradiol and inhibin B rise as the dominant follicle matures.\",\"A fixed 14-day follicular phase is false; cycle-length variation mainly reflects changes in the interval from menstruation to ovulation.\",\"Follicular growth increases estradiol until sustained high estradiol produces positive feedback and the midcycle LH surge; after ovulation, the corpus luteum produces progesterone during the luteal phase.\"],\"tk\":\"Cycle-length variation is driven mainly by the follicular phase, whereas the luteal phase is comparatively stable at about 14 days.\",\"pr\":\"A mid-luteal progesterone sample is best obtained about 7 days before the expected next menses, not automatically on cycle day 21.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating irregular cycles, date ovulation relative to the next period and interpret progesterone in that context rather than using a fixed calendar day.\",\"cn\":\"Cycle-length variation comes from the follicular phase; the luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days, the follicular phase is not.\",\"sl\":\"Endometrial phases: the estrogen (proliferative) phase is variable while the progesterone phase is fixed at 14 days. Cycle length varies because of follicular phase length; the luteal phase is fairly constant.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[5,9,10,11,15]],[\"L33_1\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F101\"},{\"id\":\"F102\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Cervical cancer risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding cervical cancer, all of the following are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Oral contraceptives are protective.\",\"Symptoms may include dyspareunia.\",\"Squamous cell carcinomas account for approximately 80%–90% of cases.\",\"It is more common in multiparous women.\",\"It is more common in lower socioeconomic groups.\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Long-term combined oral contraceptive use has been associated with a modest increase in cervical cancer risk in women with persistent high-risk HPV exposure; it is not considered protective.\",\"Invasive cervical cancer may present with postcoital bleeding, abnormal vaginal bleeding, watery discharge, pelvic pain, or dyspareunia.\",\"Historically, squamous cell carcinoma constitutes the large majority of cervical cancers, although adenocarcinoma represents an important minority.\",\"Higher parity is an epidemiologic cofactor for cervical cancer among women with persistent oncogenic HPV infection.\",\"Lower socioeconomic status correlates with higher cervical cancer burden through differences in screening access, HPV exposure, smoking, and other social determinants.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV, especially types 16 and 18, is the central cause of cervical cancer; smoking, immunosuppression, higher parity, and limited screening access increase risk.\",\"Oral contraceptives are not protective against cervical cancer, making that statement the exception; symptoms of invasive disease can include abnormal or postcoital bleeding and dyspareunia.\",\"Oncogenic HPV proteins E6 and E7 inactivate p53 and retinoblastoma tumor-suppressor pathways, promoting progression from persistent infection to cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and invasive carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Persistent high-risk HPV infection is necessary for most cervical cancers, and long-term oral contraceptive exposure is not a protective factor.\",\"pr\":\"HPV vaccination prevents infection with major oncogenic types but does not replace age-appropriate cervical screening in populations where screening is recommended.\",\"tp\":\"In a patient with postcoital bleeding or a visibly abnormal cervix, perform a speculum examination and arrange diagnostic evaluation rather than relying on a recent screening result alone.\",\"cn\":\"Oral contraceptives are a risk factor, not protective, for cervical cancer; multiparity and low socioeconomic class increase risk.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors include human papillomavirus (HPV), smoking, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), chlamydia, oral contraceptives, multiple pregnancies and low socioeconomic status. Squamous cell carcinomas are 80% to 90%; symptoms include pain during intercourse.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[10,8,6,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F102\"},{\"id\":\"F103\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hirsutism with regular menses\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Fiona is a 25-year-old woman with a body mass index (BMI) of 24 kg/m² and regular 27-day cycles. She reports gradually increasing facial hair since adolescence and has no virilization or other symptoms. What is the most likely cause?\",\"o\":[\"Hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian dysfunction\",\"Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia\",\"Ovarian hyperthecosis\",\"Familial hirsutism\",\"Exogenous androgen treatment\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Disruption of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis commonly causes irregular or absent ovulation; this patient has regular 27-day cycles.\",\"Nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency can cause hirsutism and is screened with an early-morning 17-hydroxyprogesterone, particularly when symptoms begin early or there is high-risk ancestry.\",\"Ovarian hyperthecosis usually causes marked androgen excess and may produce virilization, particularly in peri- or postmenopausal patients.\",\"Familial or idiopathic hirsutism occurs with regular menses, normal ovulation, and no virilization, reflecting increased follicular sensitivity or local androgen metabolism.\",\"Exogenous androgen exposure may cause rapid-onset hirsutism or virilization and requires a medication and household-exposure history.\"],\"e\":[\"Hirsutism with regular ovulatory cycles, normal body habitus, and no virilization can be idiopathic or familial after secondary causes of androgen excess are excluded.\",\"Gradual hair growth since adolescence, regular 27-day cycles, and absence of virilization are typical of familial or idiopathic hirsutism.\",\"Hair follicles convert testosterone to the more potent dihydrotestosterone via 5α-reductase; increased local androgen sensitivity can produce terminal facial hair despite otherwise normal reproductive function.\"],\"tk\":\"Regular cycles without virilization favor idiopathic or familial hirsutism after clinically important androgen-excess disorders are excluded.\",\"pr\":\"Rapid progression, deepening voice, clitoromegaly, or markedly elevated testosterone should prompt evaluation for an androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumor.\",\"tp\":\"Document the tempo of hair growth and signs of virilization first; if the presentation is atypical, obtain total testosterone and consider dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEAS) and 17-hydroxyprogesterone based on the differential.\",\"cn\":\"Slowly progressive hirsutism since adolescence with regular cycles, normal body mass index (BMI) and no virilization suggests familial (idiopathic) hirsutism.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of hyperandrogenism: polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) 75%, idiopathic hirsutism 15%, adrenal hyperplasia 3%. Assessment covers onset and duration, virilization and menstrual history.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[13,14,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F103\"},{\"id\":\"F104\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Early-pregnancy bleeding and miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following statements regarding early pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Misoprostol is contraindicated in women with bronchial asthma.\",\"The yolk sac normally appears only after fetal cardiac activity is visible.\",\"Vaginal bleeding occurs in approximately 20%–25% of clinically recognized early pregnancies.\",\"Anti-D immunoglobulin is routinely required for every miscarriage before 12 weeks.\",\"An intrauterine gestational sac should routinely be visible at 5 weeks on transabdominal ultrasound.\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Misoprostol is a PGE1 analogue and is not the prostaglandin classically contraindicated in asthma; bronchospasm is a concern with carboprost (PGF2α).\",\"The yolk sac is normally visualized before the embryo and before fetal cardiac activity on transvaginal ultrasound.\",\"First-trimester vaginal bleeding occurs in roughly 1 in 4 pregnancies and requires assessment for viability and ectopic pregnancy when clinically indicated.\",\"Current National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance does not recommend routine anti-D prophylaxis up to 11+6 weeks for miscarriage, ectopic pregnancy, or threatened miscarriage.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound detects an intrauterine gestational sac earlier than transabdominal imaging; absence on an abdominal scan at 5 weeks is not by itself abnormal.\"],\"e\":[\"Early-pregnancy bleeding is common, occurring in approximately 20%–25% of clinically recognized pregnancies, but pain, hemodynamic instability, or uncertain pregnancy location requires assessment for ectopic pregnancy.\",\"Vaginal bleeding in roughly one quarter of early pregnancies is clinically recognized; asthma does not contraindicate misoprostol, and anti-D is not routine for every early miscarriage.\",\"Misoprostol acts at prostaglandin E receptors to soften the cervix and stimulate uterine contractions, whereas carboprost is a PGF2α analogue that can provoke bronchospasm in patients with asthma.\"],\"tk\":\"Misoprostol is not the prostaglandin contraindicated in asthma; that caution applies principally to carboprost.\",\"pr\":\"NICE updated its early-pregnancy anti-D guidance in June 2026 and does not recommend anti-D prophylaxis through 11+6 weeks for miscarriage, ectopic pregnancy, or threatened miscarriage.\",\"tp\":\"For early bleeding, assess hemodynamic stability, pain, pregnancy location, and gestational age before choosing expectant, medical, or surgical management.\",\"cn\":\"Vaginal bleeding complicates about one in five (21%) clinically detected pregnancies.\",\"sl\":\"Bleeding complicates 21% of clinically detected pregnancies and 15% of clinically recognised pregnancies miscarry. The misoprostol slide lists contraindications as mitral stenosis, hypertension, haemoglobinopathy, anticoagulation and asthma.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[8,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key C (bleeding in 20-25%) matches slide 8 (21%). However, Dr Firas's slide 15 lists asthma as a contraindication to misoprostol, so by the slides option A is also true, although international guidance does not contraindicate misoprostol in asthma (that caution applies to carboprost).\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F104\"},{\"id\":\"F105\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"FSH-dependent follicular growth\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland and is essential for follicular growth until the antrum develops?\",\"o\":[\"Luteinizing hormone\",\"Follicle-stimulating hormone\",\"Gonadotropin-releasing hormone\",\"Estradiol\",\"Progesterone\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Luteinising hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary stimulates theca-cell androgen production and later supports dominant-follicle maturation and ovulation, but early follicular growth is primarily FSH-dependent.\",\"FSH from the anterior pituitary stimulates granulosa-cell proliferation, aromatase activity, and follicular development through the preantral and antral stages.\",\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) is secreted pulsatilely by the hypothalamus and regulates pituitary release of FSH and LH.\",\"Estradiol is produced mainly by granulosa cells through aromatization of theca-derived androgens and feeds back on the hypothalamic-pituitary axis.\",\"Progesterone is produced predominantly by the corpus luteum after ovulation and converts proliferative endometrium to a secretory state.\"],\"e\":[\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is an anterior pituitary gonadotropin that promotes granulosa-cell proliferation and follicular development, including acquisition of an antrum.\",\"The lead-in asks specifically for the anterior-pituitary hormone required for early follicular growth, which identifies FSH rather than hypothalamic GnRH or ovarian steroids.\",\"FSH induces granulosa-cell aromatase, converting theca-derived androgens to estradiol; rising estradiol and inhibin B then reduce FSH, helping select a dominant follicle.\"],\"tk\":\"FSH drives granulosa-cell function and early follicular growth, whereas LH acts mainly on theca cells and later triggers ovulation.\",\"pr\":\"The two-cell, two-gonadotropin model links LH-stimulated theca androgen production to FSH-stimulated granulosa aromatization.\",\"tp\":\"When localizing reproductive hormones, separate hypothalamic GnRH, pituitary FSH/LH, and ovarian estradiol/progesterone before comparing answer choices.\",\"cn\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary drives recruitment and growth of antral follicles.\",\"sl\":\"Antral follicles are recruited; only follicles most sensitive to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) develop further, and the most FSH-sensitive follicle becomes dominant, producing estradiol and inhibin B.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[3,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F105\"},{\"id\":\"F106\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Elevated alpha-fetoprotein associations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Raised maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is found in which of the following?\",\"o\":[\"Down syndrome\",\"Type 1 diabetes mellitus\",\"Maternal obesity\",\"Edwards syndrome\",\"Pre-eclampsia\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is typically low in Down syndrome (trisomy 21), not raised.\",\"Wrong. Pregestational (type 1) diabetes mellitus is associated with lower maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) values, which is why screening results are adjusted for it.\",\"Wrong. Maternal obesity lowers measured maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) by dilution in a larger blood volume.\",\"Wrong. Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is low in Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18), together with low human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and low unconjugated estriol.\",\"Correct. Unexplained raised maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) reflects abnormal placentation and is associated with later placental complications such as pre-eclampsia and fetal growth restriction.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal serum AFP is produced principally by the fetal liver and crosses into the maternal circulation; marked elevation classically raises concern for open neural-tube defects, abdominal-wall defects, multiple gestation, or placental pathology.\",\"Pre-eclampsia can be associated with elevated AFP, whereas trisomies 21 and 18 generally show reduced AFP and maternal obesity can lower measured concentration through dilution.\",\"Placental barrier disruption or abnormal placentation can increase transfer of fetal AFP into maternal blood, linking unexplained elevation with placenta-mediated complications.\"],\"tk\":\"Unexplained elevated maternal serum AFP can signal fetal structural defects or placental dysfunction, not only aneuploidy screening abnormalities.\",\"pr\":\"A high AFP result should first be interpreted after confirming gestational age, fetal number, and fetal anatomy, because dating error and multiple gestation are common explanations.\",\"tp\":\"When AFP is unexpectedly high, review dating and ultrasound findings before attributing the result to a placental disorder.\",\"cn\":\"Unexplained raised maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) signals placental dysfunction such as pre-eclampsia; trisomies 21 and 18, diabetes and obesity lower AFP.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F106\"},{\"id\":\"F107\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Uterine atony risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following conditions is associated with uterine atony?\",\"o\":[\"Multiple gestations\",\"Oligohydramnios\",\"Spontaneous rupture of membranes\",\"Small for gestational age\",\"Ergometrine administration\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Multiple gestation overdistends the myometrium and is a classic risk factor for uterine atony after placental delivery.\",\"Oligohydramnios reflects reduced amniotic fluid volume and does not produce the uterine overdistension that predisposes to atony.\",\"Membrane rupture alone is not a major atony risk; prolonged rupture with infection can increase PPH risk through chorioamnionitis and dysfunctional uterine contraction.\",\"A small-for-gestational-age fetus does not overdistend the uterus; placental disease may create other obstetric risks but not this mechanical atony mechanism.\",\"Ergometrine is a uterotonic that increases sustained uterine contraction and is used to treat or prevent atonic hemorrhage when not contraindicated.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage and is promoted by uterine overdistension, prolonged labor, chorioamnionitis, and exposure to uterine-relaxing drugs.\",\"Multiple gestation enlarges and stretches the uterus, making effective postpartum myometrial contraction less likely.\",\"After placental separation, myometrial contraction normally compresses spiral arteries as physiologic ligatures; inadequate contraction leaves these vessels open and produces brisk hemorrhage.\"],\"tk\":\"Uterine overdistension is a major risk factor for uterine atony, especially with multiple gestation, polyhydramnios, or macrosomia.\",\"pr\":\"The causes of postpartum hemorrhage are commonly organized as the 4 Ts: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, and Thrombin.\",\"tp\":\"In active PPH, simultaneously call for help, quantify blood loss, massage the uterus, administer appropriate uterotonics, establish intravenous (IV) access, and search for causes beyond atony.\",\"cn\":\"Uterine overdistension, as in multiple gestation, is a major risk factor for uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"sl\":\"Multifetal gestation: uterine overdistension leads to preterm labour, atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH); uterine atony is listed among maternal complications.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[24,25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F107\"},{\"id\":\"F108\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy insulin resistance\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which hormone is mainly responsible for increased maternal glucose intolerance during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Human placental lactogen\",\"Human chorionic gonadotropin\",\"Progesterone\",\"Estradiol\",\"Relaxin\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Human placental lactogen (hPL) increases maternal insulin resistance and lipolysis, helping preserve circulating glucose for fetal use.\",\"Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) supports the corpus luteum early in pregnancy and has weak TSH-receptor activity.\",\"Progesterone maintains endometrial decidualization and uterine quiescence; it contributes to pregnancy physiology but is not the main driver of insulin resistance in this item.\",\"Estradiol promotes uterine growth and increases hepatic production of several binding proteins, rather than serving as the principal mediator of gestational glucose intolerance.\",\"Relaxin contributes to connective-tissue and vascular adaptations of pregnancy and is not a major cause of maternal insulin resistance.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy becomes progressively insulin resistant, especially in the second and third trimesters, under the influence of placental hormones including human placental lactogen.\",\"hPL is the best answer because it promotes maternal glucose sparing and lipolysis as placental mass increases.\",\"By antagonizing insulin action, hPL reduces maternal peripheral glucose uptake and increases availability of glucose to cross the placenta, creating the physiologic basis for gestational glucose intolerance.\"],\"tk\":\"Human placental lactogen is a major diabetogenic hormone of pregnancy and contributes to the normal rise in maternal insulin resistance.\",\"pr\":\"Maternal insulin requirements often increase markedly in later pregnancy and then fall rapidly after placental delivery as placental hormone levels collapse.\",\"tp\":\"In women with diabetes, anticipate changing insulin requirements across gestation and reassess promptly postpartum to avoid hypoglycemia.\",\"cn\":\"Human placental lactogen (hPL) is the main anti-insulin placental hormone causing glucose intolerance in pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Human placental lactogen (hPL): anti-insulin and lipolytic, spares glucose for the fetus, rises with placental mass. Second half of pregnancy is catabolic with insulin resistance (hPL, growth hormone [GH] variant, cortisol, progesterone).\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[8,9]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F108\"},{\"id\":\"F109\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"First-trimester nuchal translucency\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Nuchal translucency measurement in the first trimester is used mainly to screen for which condition?\",\"o\":[\"Down syndrome\",\"Turner syndrome\",\"Klinefelter syndrome\",\"Kallmann syndrome\",\"Robertsonian translocation\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Increased nuchal translucency at 11–13+6 weeks is associated with trisomy 21 and other chromosomal abnormalities, as well as some structural anomalies.\",\"Turner syndrome (45,X) can produce markedly increased nuchal translucency or cystic hygroma, but the routine first-trimester screening application is principally aneuploidy risk assessment including trisomy 21.\",\"Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) is not classically identified by a distinctive nuchal-translucency pattern and often remains undiagnosed until later life.\",\"Kallmann syndrome is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia due to abnormal GnRH-neuron migration and is not a first-trimester nuchal-translucency target.\",\"A Robertsonian translocation is a chromosomal rearrangement diagnosed by cytogenetic testing; nuchal translucency estimates fetal aneuploidy risk rather than identifying the rearrangement itself.\"],\"e\":[\"First-trimester nuchal translucency (NT) measurement is performed at the crown-rump-length window corresponding roughly to 11–13+6 weeks and contributes to screening for trisomy 21 and other aneuploidies.\",\"Increased nuchal translucency is a major trisomy 21 screening marker, although it can also occur with other chromosomal and structural abnormalities.\",\"Increased NT reflects excess fluid in the fetal posterior neck and can arise from altered lymphatic drainage, cardiac dysfunction, or connective-tissue abnormalities, explaining why it is associated with both chromosomal and structural disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Increased first-trimester nuchal translucency raises the risk of trisomy 21 and other fetal abnormalities, but it is a screening finding rather than a diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"A markedly increased NT can be associated with congenital heart disease even when fetal karyotype is normal, so detailed structural assessment may be indicated.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret NT using the gestational-age/CRL-specific protocol and combine it with the chosen aneuploidy-screening strategy rather than treating the measurement as a stand-alone diagnostic test.\",\"cn\":\"Increased first-trimester nuchal translucency (NT) is mainly a screening marker for Down syndrome (trisomy 21).\",\"sl\":\"The combined test of nuchal translucency (NT), beta human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG) and pregnancy-associated plasma protein A (PAPP-A) at 11 to 14 weeks screens for chromosomal anomalies; cell-free DNA performs well for trisomy 21.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F109\"},{\"id\":\"F110\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Normal intrapartum CTG features\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following statements about fetal heart rate monitoring is false?\",\"o\":[\"A normal intrapartum baseline fetal heart rate is 110–160 beats/min.\",\"Baseline variability reflects integrity of the fetal central and autonomic nervous systems.\",\"Two accelerations within 20 minutes can satisfy a reactive antenatal nonstress test criterion.\",\"Normal baseline variability is 5–25 beats/min.\",\"The baseline fetal heart rate normally rises progressively as gestational age advances.\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) defines the normal intrapartum baseline fetal heart rate as 110–160 beats/min.\",\"Normal beat-to-beat variability depends on interaction of sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways with an intact fetal central nervous system.\",\"A reactive nonstress test commonly requires at least two qualifying accelerations within 20 minutes in a fetus at or beyond 32 weeks.\",\"A baseline variability of 5–25 beats/min is reassuring and reflects intact autonomic modulation of the fetal heart.\",\"The fetal baseline heart rate generally falls with advancing gestation as parasympathetic control matures, so a progressive rise is not normal physiology.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal intrapartum CTG assessment considers baseline rate, variability, decelerations, and the overall clinical context; a baseline of 110–160 beats/min and variability of 5–25 beats/min are reassuring features.\",\"The baseline fetal heart rate does not normally rise as gestation advances; maturation of parasympathetic control produces a gradual fall in baseline rate.\",\"Beat-to-beat variability results from continuous autonomic modulation of the sinoatrial node, so persistent loss of variability can accompany fetal hypoxia, medications, sleep cycling, or neurologic compromise.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal intrapartum CTG uses a baseline of 110–160 beats/min and variability of 5–25 beats/min in conjunction with deceleration assessment.\",\"pr\":\"Absence of accelerations on an otherwise normal intrapartum CTG does not by itself indicate fetal acidosis; accelerations are more central to antenatal nonstress-test reactivity.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret CTG dynamically: review the entire trace, maternal observations, contractions, and evolving risk factors rather than acting on one isolated feature.\",\"cn\":\"Baseline fetal heart rate falls, and variability increases, as gestation advances due to parasympathetic maturation.\",\"sl\":\"Before 28 weeks the cardiotocography (CTG) has a high baseline fetal heart rate (FHR) and reduced variability; with advancing gestation FHR slows down and variability increases. Baseline 110-150 bpm in late pregnancy; variability 5-25 bpm.\",\"s\":[[\"L17_0\",[12,13,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F110\"},{\"id\":\"F111\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding postpartum hemorrhage (PPH), which of the following statements is true?\",\"o\":[\"Secondary PPH is classified as occurring between 24 hours and 6 months postpartum.\",\"Uterine atony is the second most common cause of PPH.\",\"A maternal hemoglobin concentration below 8.0 g/dL is a risk factor for PPH.\",\"Choriocarcinoma is the most common cause of secondary PPH.\",\"Arterial embolization is the preferred first intervention in a hemodynamically unstable patient with massive bleeding.\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Secondary PPH begins after 24 hours but is generally defined only through the early postpartum weeks, not out to 6 months.\",\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of primary PPH, accounting for most cases of severe postpartum bleeding.\",\"Significant maternal anemia is recognized as a PPH risk factor and also reduces physiologic tolerance of blood loss; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) uses Hb below 85 g/L at labor onset as an antenatal risk threshold.\",\"Secondary PPH is more often related to endometritis or retained products of conception than gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.\",\"Uterine artery embolization is a uterus-sparing option when resources and hemodynamic circumstances permit; profound instability requires immediate resuscitation and rapid operative hemorrhage control when necessary.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum hemorrhage requires rapid recognition and treatment; uterine atony is the leading cause, while trauma, retained tissue, and coagulopathy are other major categories.\",\"Severe antenatal anemia, including hemoglobin below about 8–8.5 g/dL, increases PPH risk and substantially worsens the consequences of acute blood loss.\",\"Low red-cell mass reduces oxygen-carrying reserve, so a given hemorrhage causes earlier tissue hypoxia and decompensation; this is why antenatal identification and treatment of maternal anemia are central to hemorrhage preparedness.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal anemia is both a recognized PPH risk factor and a major determinant of tolerance to postpartum blood loss.\",\"pr\":\"Secondary PPH should prompt assessment for endometritis and retained placental tissue, particularly when bleeding is accompanied by fever, uterine tenderness, or offensive lochia.\",\"tp\":\"For a patient at high PPH risk, document the risk antenatally, correct anemia when possible, ensure venous access and blood availability as indicated, and plan active management of the third stage.\",\"cn\":\"Antenatal anaemia is a risk factor for postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) and should be treated; secondary PPH is 24 hours to 12 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Antenatal anaemia should be investigated and treated as part of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) prevention. Secondary PPH is abnormal bleeding between 24 hours and 12 weeks postnatally; causes are endometritis, retained tissue and subinvolution.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[6,24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F111\"},{\"id\":\"F112\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Booking-visit investigations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding routine prenatal care, all of the following investigations are indicated at the booking visit except:\",\"o\":[\"Hepatitis B surface antigen\",\"Blood grouping\",\"Rubella antibody status\",\"Antibody screen (indirect Coombs test)\",\"75-g oral glucose tolerance test\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Screening for hepatitis B surface antigen in pregnancy identifies maternal infection so neonatal immunoprophylaxis and maternal follow-up can be arranged.\",\"Maternal ABO and rhesus D (RhD) typing is performed at booking to identify RhD-negative patients and transfusion-relevant information.\",\"Assessment of rubella immunity identifies nonimmune patients who should be offered postpartum vaccination because live rubella vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy.\",\"A maternal red-cell antibody screen at booking detects clinically significant alloantibodies that can cause fetal anemia.\",\"A 75-g oral glucose tolerance test is not a universal booking investigation; it is generally offered later or earlier only when risk-based testing is indicated.\"],\"e\":[\"The booking visit establishes maternal blood group, RhD status, red-cell antibodies, infection screening, and baseline maternal health.\",\"A 75-g oral glucose tolerance test is not part of the universal booking panel; gestational-diabetes testing is timed according to guideline and risk factors.\",\"Gestational diabetes develops as pregnancy-related insulin resistance rises with placental hormone production, so routine diagnostic testing is usually performed after this metabolic shift rather than automatically at the first visit.\"],\"tk\":\"Booking investigations establish blood group, alloantibody status, and key infection risks; an OGTT is not a universal booking test.\",\"pr\":\"Patients with previous gestational diabetes may need earlier glucose assessment in addition to later pregnancy testing according to local protocol.\",\"tp\":\"At booking, document what has already been tested and schedule gestation-specific screening so important tests are neither omitted nor unnecessarily duplicated.\",\"cn\":\"The 75-g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) is not a booking test; it is offered at 24-28 weeks for risk factors.\",\"sl\":\"Booking investigations: complete blood count (CBC), blood group, urine analysis and culture, hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), rubella IgG and screening for sexually transmitted diseases. The 75-g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) is offered at 24 to 28 weeks to women with any risk factor.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[10]],[\"L10_0\",[28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F112\"},{\"id\":\"F113\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ovarian tumor histogenesis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 63-year-old woman presents with an abdominal mass and weight loss and is diagnosed with an ovarian tumor. What is the most common broad histologic category of ovarian tumor in this age group?\",\"o\":[\"Epithelial tumor\",\"Germ cell tumor\",\"Stromal tumor\",\"Trophoblastic tumor\",\"Sex cord tumor\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Epithelial ovarian tumors are the dominant ovarian neoplasm group in older adults and account for most ovarian malignancies.\",\"Germ-cell tumors are most important in adolescents and younger women; malignant germ-cell tumors represent a small proportion of ovarian cancers overall.\",\"Sex-cord stromal tumors include granulosa-cell tumors, thecomas, and fibromas and may secrete estrogen, androgen, or inhibin depending on subtype.\",\"Primary ovarian trophoblastic tumors are rare; gestational trophoblastic disease usually arises from pregnancy-related trophoblastic tissue.\",\"Sex-cord tumors may be hormonally active, such as estrogen-producing granulosa-cell tumors, but are not the commonest ovarian tumor class in a 63-year-old.\"],\"e\":[\"In postmenopausal women, most malignant ovarian neoplasms are epithelial in origin, including high-grade serous, endometrioid, clear-cell, and mucinous carcinomas.\",\"A 63-year-old with an adnexal mass and weight loss therefore most likely has an epithelial tumor rather than a germ-cell or sex-cord stromal neoplasm.\",\"Müllerian-type epithelium of the tube/ovary/peritoneum gives rise to most epithelial ovarian cancers; high-grade serous carcinoma is commonly associated with TP53 alteration and often originates in the fimbrial tube.\"],\"tk\":\"Epithelial tumors predominate among ovarian malignancies in older women, whereas germ-cell tumors are concentrated in younger patients.\",\"pr\":\"Many high-grade serous carcinomas are now understood to arise from serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma in the fimbrial fallopian tube.\",\"tp\":\"In a postmenopausal patient with a suspicious adnexal mass, characterize morphology and spread on imaging and use appropriate tumor markers to plan referral to gynecologic oncology.\",\"cn\":\"Epithelial tumours account for about 90% of ovarian cancers and predominate in postmenopausal women.\",\"sl\":\"Epithelial tumours make up 90% of ovarian cancers, with serous 75%. 80% of ovarian cancers are postmenopausal.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[5,2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F113\"},{\"id\":\"F114\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Preterm birth risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding preterm labor and birth, one statement is false:\",\"o\":[\"After one spontaneous preterm delivery, the risk of preterm delivery in a subsequent pregnancy is increased and is approximately 15% or higher.\",\"Large loop excision of the transformation zone (LLETZ) treatment increases the risk of preterm birth.\",\"Preterm labor is a heterogeneous condition.\",\"In-utero exposure to diethylstilbestrol can increase the risk of preterm birth.\",\"Rates of preterm birth are consistently lower than they were 10 years ago.\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A previous spontaneous preterm birth is one of the strongest recurrence predictors; risk rises further with earlier or repeated prior preterm births.\",\"Excisional cervical procedures such as large loop excision of the transformation zone (LLETZ)/LEEP are associated with increased preterm-birth risk, particularly with greater excision depth.\",\"Spontaneous preterm labor is heterogeneous, arising through pathways that include inflammation, uterine overdistension, cervical dysfunction, hemorrhage, and maternal-fetal stress.\",\"Prenatal diethylstilbestrol exposure can cause Müllerian tract abnormalities and adverse reproductive outcomes, including preterm birth.\",\"Preterm-birth rates have not shown a uniform sustained decline across populations; epidemiology varies by region and by medically indicated versus spontaneous birth.\"],\"e\":[\"Preterm birth is a syndrome with multiple pathways, and prior spontaneous preterm birth, cervical surgery, uterine anomalies, infection/inflammation, and short cervix are important risk factors.\",\"Preterm-birth rates do not show a uniform decade-on-decade decline; population trends vary by region and by spontaneous versus medically indicated birth.\",\"Distinct mechanisms converge on cervical remodeling, membrane activation, and myometrial contractility, explaining why a single preventive strategy cannot eliminate all preterm birth.\"],\"tk\":\"Prior spontaneous preterm birth is a major recurrence risk factor, and preterm birth represents several biologically distinct pathways.\",\"pr\":\"The predictive value of transvaginal cervical length is greatest in patients with relevant history or symptoms; a shorter cervix is associated with higher spontaneous preterm-birth risk.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing preterm-birth risk, document the gestational age and phenotype of any previous preterm delivery and distinguish spontaneous labor, preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM), and medically indicated birth.\",\"cn\":\"Preterm birth is heterogeneous with recurrence and cervical-surgery risk; rates are increasing, not consistently falling.\",\"sl\":\"Preterm birth is 5-10% of all deliveries in developed countries and rates are on the increase. Risk factors include previous preterm birth and cervical procedures (large loop excision of the transformation zone, LLETZ, cone).\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[5,7,8,16,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F114\"},{\"id\":\"F115\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Placenta previa with preterm bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents at 31+6 weeks with painless vaginal bleeding that is settling on arrival. Ultrasound 1 week earlier showed the placenta covering the internal cervical os. Her vital signs and cardiotocography (CTG) are reassuring. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Emergency cesarean section\",\"Cross-match group-specific blood and admit for observation\",\"Cross-match group-specific blood, admit for observation, and administer antenatal corticosteroids\",\"Admit for tocolysis and corticosteroids\",\"Discharge and review in clinic\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Immediate cesarean delivery is indicated for ongoing major hemorrhage, maternal instability, labor, or fetal compromise; none is present after the bleeding settles.\",\"Admission and blood preparation address hemorrhage risk, but at 31+6 weeks the possibility of preterm delivery also makes antenatal corticosteroids clinically important.\",\"Stable placenta previa with a settled preterm bleed is managed expectantly with admission/observation, blood availability, and antenatal corticosteroids because recurrent bleeding may precipitate preterm delivery.\",\"Routine tocolysis is not the default treatment for placenta-previa bleeding and should not delay delivery when hemorrhage or fetal compromise occurs.\",\"A symptomatic complete placenta previa at 31+6 weeks carries a substantial risk of recurrent bleeding and warrants inpatient assessment rather than immediate discharge.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa typically causes painless antepartum bleeding; delivery is required for uncontrolled hemorrhage, maternal instability, labor that cannot be safely managed, or fetal compromise.\",\"This patient is stable at 31+6 weeks with a reassuring cardiotocography (CTG) and settling bleeding, so expectant inpatient management with cross-matched blood and antenatal corticosteroids is appropriate.\",\"Placental tissue over the internal os cannot accommodate cervical change without shearing from the lower uterine segment, creating recurrent hemorrhage risk; corticosteroids reduce neonatal respiratory morbidity if preterm birth follows.\"],\"tk\":\"Stable preterm placenta previa with a settled bleed is generally managed expectantly, with readiness for recurrent hemorrhage and fetal prematurity.\",\"pr\":\"Digital vaginal examination should be avoided until placenta previa has been excluded, because manipulation can provoke major hemorrhage.\",\"tp\":\"For bleeding placenta previa, establish maternal stability first, obtain blood for group/cross-match, assess fetal status, and keep a low threshold for urgent delivery if bleeding recurs heavily.\",\"cn\":\"Stable preterm placenta previa with settling bleeding: admit, cross-match blood and give antenatal corticosteroids.\",\"sl\":\"All women with antepartum haemorrhage (APH) heavier than spotting should remain in hospital at least until bleeding stops. Offer a single course of antenatal corticosteroids between 24+0 and 34+6 weeks if at risk of preterm birth; major bleeding needs 4 units cross-matched.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[19,21,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F115\"},{\"id\":\"F116\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"WHO semen reference values\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A semen analysis from a 30-year-old man with a 3-year history of infertility is reviewed using World Health Organization (WHO) 2010 lower reference values. Which parameter is abnormal?\",\"o\":[\"Total motility 32%\",\"Sperm concentration 19 million/mL\",\"Normal morphology 94%\",\"Semen volume 4 mL\",\"Total vitality 63%\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Under WHO 2010 criteria, the lower reference limit for total motility (progressive + non-progressive) is 40%, so 32% is below the reference threshold.\",\"The WHO 2010 lower reference limit for sperm concentration is 15 million/mL, so 19 million/mL is above that threshold.\",\"WHO strict morphology uses a low lower-reference threshold of approximately 4% normal forms; 94% normal morphology is not abnormal.\",\"The WHO 2010 lower reference limit for semen volume is 1.5 mL, so 4 mL is within an expected range.\",\"The WHO 2010 lower reference limit for vitality is 58% live spermatozoa, so 63% is above the threshold.\"],\"e\":[\"WHO 2010 semen reference limits included total motility 40%, progressive motility 32%, concentration 15 million/mL, volume 1.5 mL, morphology 4%, and vitality 58%.\",\"Using WHO 2010 thresholds, total motility of 32% is abnormal because the lower reference limit is 40%; the other reported measurements exceed their respective lower limits.\",\"Motility reflects the proportion of sperm capable of movement; reduced total motility can impair transport through the female reproductive tract even when concentration and morphology are adequate.\"],\"tk\":\"WHO 2010 distinguished total motility (40%) from progressive motility (32%), so confusing the two thresholds changes the interpretation.\",\"pr\":\"The WHO sixth edition (2021) superseded the 2010 manual and emphasizes that semen reference distributions are not absolute boundaries between fertile and infertile men.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret semen analysis only after confirming collection conditions and repeat an abnormal test when appropriate, because substantial within-person variation is common.\",\"cn\":\"World Health Organization (WHO) 2010 lower reference limit for total sperm motility is 40% (progressive 32%); 32% total motility is abnormal.\",\"sl\":\"Semen analysis (World Health Organization [WHO] 2010): volume 1.5 mL or more, pH 7.2 or more, concentration 15 million/mL or more, total number 39 million, total motility 40% or more or 32% progressive, vitality 58%, normal morphology 4% or more.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F116\"},{\"id\":\"F117\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Infrequent menstruation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has menstrual periods every 5 to 6 weeks and occasionally every 8 weeks. This pattern is best described as:\",\"o\":[\"Amenorrhea\",\"Oligomenorrhea\",\"Polymenorrhea\",\"Hypomenorrhea\",\"Metrorrhagia\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Amenorrhea denotes absence of menstruation rather than cycles that continue at prolonged intervals.\",\"Cycles occurring at intervals greater than about 35–38 days are classically termed oligomenorrhea or, in newer terminology, infrequent menstruation.\",\"Polymenorrhea refers to unusually frequent cycles with shortened intervals, historically less than about 21 days.\",\"Hypomenorrhea describes abnormally light or scant menstrual flow rather than increased cycle length.\",\"Metrorrhagia is older terminology for bleeding at irregular times between expected menses, not simply infrequent cyclic menstruation.\"],\"e\":[\"Menstrual cycles separated by 5–8 weeks are longer than the expected adult interval and are classically described as oligomenorrhea, now often termed infrequent menstruation.\",\"The patient continues to menstruate, excluding amenorrhea, but the prolonged intervals identify an ovulatory-frequency abnormality rather than a problem of flow volume.\",\"Infrequent menses often reflect oligo-ovulation or anovulation, which lengthens the follicular phase and reduces the number of withdrawal bleeds over time.\"],\"tk\":\"Oligomenorrhea describes infrequent menstrual cycles, typically with intervals longer than the normal adult range.\",\"pr\":\"Chronic oligo-ovulation can produce prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure, increasing the risk of endometrial hyperplasia when not counterbalanced by progesterone.\",\"tp\":\"For persistent infrequent menses, first exclude pregnancy and then assess likely endocrine causes such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), thyroid disease, hyperprolactinemia, or ovarian insufficiency based on the history.\",\"cn\":\"Menses at intervals longer than 38 days are infrequent menstruation, traditionally called oligomenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Normal frequency of menses is 24-38 days; infrequent menses is a cycle length over 38 days, frequent is under 24 days, and absent menses is amenorrhoea.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[3,5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F032\"},{\"id\":\"F118\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":30,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PCOS and endometrial hyperplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 43-year-old nulliparous woman with polycystic ovary syndrome presents with intermenstrual bleeding. Endometrial biopsy shows simple hyperplasia. Which feature is characteristically associated with the underlying disorder in this patient?\",\"o\":[\"Anovulatory cycles\",\"Hyperthyroidism\",\"Hypothyroidism\",\"Hyperprolactinemia\",\"Vaginal adenosis\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"PCOS commonly causes chronic oligo-ovulation or anovulation, leading to prolonged estrogen exposure without regular progesterone opposition and increasing endometrial hyperplasia risk.\",\"Hyperthyroidism can alter menstrual patterns but is not a defining feature of PCOS; thyroid disease should be excluded when evaluating ovulatory dysfunction.\",\"Hypothyroidism can cause menstrual irregularity and hyperprolactinemia but represents an alternative endocrine disorder rather than a characteristic PCOS feature.\",\"Persistent hyperprolactinemia suppresses gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) and may cause amenorrhea or galactorrhea; it is an important mimic to exclude before diagnosing PCOS.\",\"Vaginal adenosis is classically linked to in-utero diethylstilbestrol exposure and is unrelated to the endocrine pathophysiology of PCOS.\"],\"e\":[\"PCOS is characterized by ovulatory dysfunction and hyperandrogenism, with polycystic ovarian morphology serving as a third Rotterdam feature after exclusion of mimics.\",\"This 43-year-old has endometrial hyperplasia, a complication of chronic anovulation in which the endometrium receives prolonged estrogenic stimulation without cyclic progesterone.\",\"Failure to ovulate prevents corpus-luteum formation and therefore sustained luteal progesterone, allowing continued endometrial proliferation that can progress to hyperplasia and, over time, endometrioid carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Chronic anovulation in PCOS exposes the endometrium to unopposed estrogen, increasing the risk of hyperplasia.\",\"pr\":\"Endometrial protection in chronic anovulation can be provided with cyclic progestin, combined hormonal contraception, or a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system, depending on patient goals and contraindications.\",\"tp\":\"Abnormal bleeding in a patient with PCOS should not automatically be attributed to the syndrome; evaluate endometrial risk based on age, bleeding pattern, duration of anovulation, and other risk factors.\",\"cn\":\"Chronic anovulation in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) leaves the endometrium under unopposed estrogen, causing hyperplasia.\",\"sl\":\"Rotterdam criteria include oligo- and/or anovulation. Anovulatory cycles give unopposed oestrogen with a thick, unstable endometrium and a long-term risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer; common in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).\",\"s\":[[\"L30_0\",[6,14]],[\"L33_1\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F118\"},{\"id\":\"F119\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Supportive care in labor\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In the general care of a woman in labor, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Epidural analgesia can impair bladder sensation and contribute to urinary retention.\",\"Drinking is contraindicated during labor.\",\"Continuous emotional support can improve the childbirth experience and may improve labor outcomes.\",\"Semi-recumbent, squatting, kneeling, or left lateral positions can all be appropriate for birth.\",\"Aortocaval compression can be reduced by maintaining at least a 15° left lateral tilt.\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Neuraxial analgesia can reduce bladder sensation and detrusor function, increasing the need for scheduled bladder assessment and catheterization when necessary.\",\"Current intrapartum guidance encourages women to drink when thirsty; routine prohibition of oral fluids in uncomplicated labor is not recommended.\",\"Continuous labor support is associated with improved maternal experience and can reduce some obstetric interventions in selected settings.\",\"Upright and lateral maternal positions can be used in labor when clinically appropriate and may improve comfort and maternal autonomy.\",\"A left lateral tilt reduces compression of the inferior vena cava and aorta by the gravid uterus and can improve venous return and uteroplacental perfusion.\"],\"e\":[\"Modern intrapartum care supports mobility, individualized positioning, oral hydration according to thirst, bladder care, and continuous emotional support when no contraindication exists.\",\"Routine prohibition of oral fluids is outdated; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance explicitly advises drinking during labor when thirsty and cautions against excessive intake rather than normal hydration.\",\"Maintaining hydration supports maternal physiology, while excessive hypotonic fluid can cause peripartum hyponatremia, especially when combined with intravenous (IV) fluids or oxytocin, so intake and output should be monitored when risk factors are present.\"],\"tk\":\"Routine fluid restriction is not appropriate in uncomplicated labor; women should generally drink according to thirst.\",\"pr\":\"Peripartum hyponatremia is defined by NICE as maternal serum sodium below 130 mmol/L and can cause maternal and neonatal neurologic complications.\",\"tp\":\"Review bladder function at least every few hours during labor, particularly with epidural analgesia or IV fluids, and catheterize when retention is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Women in labour are encouraged to drink water or isotonic drinks; oral fluids are not contraindicated.\",\"sl\":\"Hydration: encouraged to drink isotonic drinks or water; intravenous fluid may be necessary if labour is prolonged. Freedom of movement is encouraged; semi-recumbent, squatting, kneeling or left-lateral positions are used.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[45,46,47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F119\"},{\"id\":\"F120\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse is associated with all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"Heavy lifting\",\"Abdominal wall defect\",\"Obesity\",\"Chronic pulmonary disease\",\"Constipation and straining\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Repeated heavy lifting can chronically increase intra-abdominal pressure and contribute to pelvic-floor loading.\",\"An abdominal wall defect is not a standard established risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse in the way childbirth-related pelvic-floor injury and chronic raised intra-abdominal pressure are.\",\"Obesity increases chronic intra-abdominal pressure and is an established risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse.\",\"Chronic cough from pulmonary disease repeatedly raises intra-abdominal pressure and can contribute to prolapse.\",\"Chronic constipation and straining repeatedly stress pelvic support structures and are recognized prolapse risk factors.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic organ prolapse results from failure of pelvic-floor muscles, connective tissue, and apical support, with vaginal childbirth, age, obesity, and chronic raised intra-abdominal pressure as major contributors.\",\"Heavy lifting, obesity, chronic cough, and straining all increase pelvic-floor loading, whereas an abdominal wall defect is not a standard independent prolapse risk factor.\",\"Repetitive mechanical stress and childbirth-related levator or fascial injury weaken vaginal support, allowing the bladder, uterus/apex, or rectum to descend toward or beyond the hymen.\"],\"tk\":\"Chronic increases in intra-abdominal pressure and childbirth-related pelvic-floor injury are major contributors to pelvic organ prolapse.\",\"pr\":\"The Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) system objectively describes vaginal support using measured points referenced to the hymen rather than relying only on older descriptive terms such as cystocele or rectocele.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about a vaginal bulge, pressure, urinary and bowel symptoms, and how prolapse affects activity because treatment is driven by symptom burden rather than anatomy alone.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) risk factors are parity, constipation and straining, heavy lifting, obesity and chronic pulmonary disease; not abdominal wall defects.\",\"sl\":\"Etiology: parity is the strongest risk factor; constipation and straining (61% vs 4% develop POP); heavy lifting, obesity and chronic pulmonary disease (increase abdominal pressure).\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[8,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F120\"},{\"id\":\"F121\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum endometritis risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are recognized risk factors for postpartum endometritis except:\",\"o\":[\"Precipitous labor\",\"Prolonged rupture of membranes\",\"Manual removal of the placenta\",\"Multiple vaginal examinations\",\"Intrauterine catheter or internal monitoring\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A very rapid precipitous labor is not a standard risk factor for postpartum endometritis; prolonged labor rather than short labor increases risk.\",\"Prolonged rupture of membranes allows ascending vaginal flora to reach the uterine cavity and increases postpartum endometritis risk.\",\"Manual placental removal introduces bacteria and may leave retained tissue, both of which increase postpartum endometritis risk.\",\"Repeated digital cervical examinations, especially after membrane rupture, increase ascending bacterial inoculation and are a recognized risk factor.\",\"Internal fetal or uterine monitoring requires transcervical instrumentation and is associated with increased postpartum uterine infection risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum endometritis is usually a polymicrobial ascending uterine infection and is most strongly associated with cesarean delivery, prolonged labor or membrane rupture, multiple examinations, internal monitoring, and retained/manual placental tissue.\",\"Multiple vaginal examinations are a recognized risk factor; the exception is precipitous labor, whereas prolonged labor increases infection risk.\",\"Disruption of cervical barriers and repeated instrumentation increase exposure of the postpartum decidual surface to mixed vaginal flora, producing uterine tenderness, fever, and foul lochia.\"],\"tk\":\"Multiple vaginal examinations after membrane rupture increase postpartum endometritis risk, while prolonged rather than precipitous labor is the typical labor-duration risk factor.\",\"pr\":\"First-line therapy for uncomplicated postpartum endometritis commonly uses intravenous (IV) clindamycin plus gentamicin until the patient is clinically improved and afebrile.\",\"tp\":\"In postpartum fever with uterine tenderness, review delivery mode, duration of membrane rupture, instrumentation, retained tissue, and response to broad-spectrum antibiotics; persistent fever should trigger reassessment for abscess or septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum endometritis risk factors include caesarean birth, prolonged rupture of membranes, retained products and intrauterine manipulation; precipitous labour is not one.\",\"sl\":\"Puerperal sepsis risk factors: caesarean section (CS), prolonged rupture of membranes (ROM), retained products; obesity, diabetes, anaemia, immunosuppression; and Group A Streptococcus contact.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F121\"},{\"id\":\"F122\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal congenital heart disease\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding congenital cardiac disease in pregnancy, one statement is wrong:\",\"o\":[\"There is approximately a 3%–5% chance that the newborn will have congenital heart disease when a parent is affected.\",\"Fetal echocardiography should be considered when the father has congenital heart disease.\",\"Approximately 25% of affected newborns will have exactly the same congenital cardiac lesion as the affected parent.\",\"A small uncomplicated patent ductus arteriosus is generally a lower-risk lesion in pregnancy.\",\"The second stage of labor may be shortened with assisted delivery in selected women with significant cardiac disease.\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Parental congenital heart disease increases recurrence risk in offspring above the background population risk, commonly into the low single-digit percentage range.\",\"A first-degree family history of congenital heart disease, including an affected father, is an indication for targeted fetal cardiac assessment in many protocols.\",\"Recurrence of congenital heart disease does not imply that one quarter of affected offspring will reproduce the identical maternal lesion; recurrence patterns vary markedly by defect and genetic mechanism.\",\"A repaired or hemodynamically insignificant patent ductus arteriosus is usually better tolerated than lesions causing cyanosis, severe pulmonary hypertension, or major ventricular dysfunction.\",\"In selected high-risk cardiac lesions, limiting prolonged Valsalva and hemodynamic stress with assisted vaginal delivery may be considered, although routine instrumentation is not required for every patient.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases plasma volume, heart rate, and cardiac output, so maternal congenital heart disease must be stratified by lesion, ventricular function, pulmonary pressure, rhythm, and prior events.\",\"The incorrect statement is the fixed 25% claim for an identical maternal lesion in affected offspring; recurrence risk and phenotype depend on the specific congenital defect and inheritance pattern.\",\"Congenital heart disease is etiologically heterogeneous, so shared genetic susceptibility may raise recurrence without reproducing the same anatomy in a predictable fraction of pregnancies.\"],\"tk\":\"Offspring of a parent with congenital heart disease have an increased recurrence risk, but the exact lesion and magnitude depend on the parental defect.\",\"pr\":\"The highest maternal risks occur with lesions such as pulmonary arterial hypertension/Eisenmenger physiology, severe ventricular dysfunction, and major obstructive disease.\",\"tp\":\"Arrange preconception or early-pregnancy multidisciplinary review for significant congenital heart disease and document a lesion-specific delivery and postpartum monitoring plan.\",\"cn\":\"When a parent has congenital heart disease, about half of affected offspring have the same lesion as the parent, not 25%.\",\"sl\":\"Congenital heart disease in the offspring: in about half of these cases the abnormality is the same as in the parent; fetal echocardiography should be offered when the mother (or father) has congenital heart disease. A small to moderate patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) with normal pulmonary pressures adds no maternal risk.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[8,11,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F122\"},{\"id\":\"F123\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Induction of labor contraindications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following may be indications for induction of labor except:\",\"o\":[\"Maternal diabetes mellitus when delivery is indicated\",\"Recurrent reduced fetal movements after assessment when planned delivery is judged appropriate\",\"Acute fetal compromise requiring immediate cesarean birth\",\"Chorioamnionitis\",\"Severe preeclampsia when vaginal birth is clinically appropriate\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal diabetes may justify planned delivery depending on glycemic control, complications, fetal growth, and gestational age.\",\"Recurrent reduced fetal movements can lead to individualized planned delivery after fetal assessment, especially near term or when additional risk factors are present.\",\"When fetal compromise requires immediate birth, the time needed for cervical ripening or induction is unsafe and urgent operative delivery is indicated.\",\"Intra-amniotic infection/chorioamnionitis is an indication for delivery; induction and vaginal birth are usually appropriate unless an obstetric reason for cesarean exists.\",\"Preeclampsia with an indication for delivery may be managed by induction when maternal and fetal status permit and immediate cesarean birth is not required.\"],\"e\":[\"Induction is appropriate when the benefits of planned vaginal birth outweigh the risks of continuing pregnancy and there is enough time for cervical ripening and labor.\",\"Acute fetal compromise requiring immediate cesarean birth is the exception because induction cannot provide sufficiently rapid delivery, whereas diabetes, preeclampsia, infection, or recurrent reduced movements may justify planned delivery in suitable circumstances.\",\"The decision depends on the urgency of birth, fetal condition, gestational age, and cervical favorability; severe compromise shifts the goal from initiating labor to the fastest safe route of delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Induction is unsuitable when fetal compromise requires immediate birth, because the fetus cannot safely tolerate the delay inherent in labor induction.\",\"pr\":\"Confirmed fetal growth restriction with fetal compromise is specifically a setting in which National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends cesarean birth rather than induction.\",\"tp\":\"Before starting induction, document the indication, fetal status, presentation, membrane status, and Bishop score, then confirm there is no reason that birth must occur more rapidly.\",\"cn\":\"Induction of labour (IOL) is contraindicated in fetal distress needing immediate delivery; it suits diabetes, hypertension and other indications.\",\"sl\":\"Indications: post-date pregnancy, prelabour rupture of membranes, diabetes, hypertension, small for gestational age, reduced fetal movements. Contraindications include abnormal placentation, fetal distress, previous classical cesarean section and breech or transverse lie.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[16,17,18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F123\"},{\"id\":\"F124\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"RhD alloimmunization prophylaxis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 22-year-old G2P1 woman is rhesus D (RhD) negative and has no immune anti-D antibodies. All of the following statements are true except:\",\"o\":[\"She does not need routine anti-D prophylaxis at 28 weeks if she received anti-D at 26 weeks after abdominal trauma.\",\"She should receive anti-D after amniocentesis if the fetus is or may be RhD positive.\",\"She needs an antibody screen at the booking visit.\",\"Anti-D given for a sensitizing event is additional to routine antenatal prophylaxis.\",\"She should receive routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis at about 28 weeks according to the local approved regimen.\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Anti-D given for a potentially sensitizing event does not replace routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis; RAADP should still be given according to schedule.\",\"Amniocentesis is a potentially sensitizing procedure, so unsensitized RhD-negative patients generally require anti-D prophylaxis when fetal rhesus D (RhD) exposure is possible.\",\"All pregnant patients should have ABO/RhD typing and a red-cell antibody screen at booking to identify alloimmunization.\",\"Guidance treats event-related anti-D and routine antenatal prophylaxis as separate indications, because each protects against different opportunities for fetomaternal hemorrhage.\",\"Unsensitized RhD-negative patients are offered routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis, commonly as a single dose at 28–30 weeks or a two-dose regimen.\"],\"e\":[\"Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents RhD alloimmunization in unsensitized RhD-negative patients after potentially sensitizing events and through routine antenatal prophylaxis.\",\"A dose given after abdominal trauma at 26 weeks does not replace the scheduled routine dose around 28–30 weeks, making that statement false.\",\"Passive anti-D coats fetal RhD-positive erythrocytes entering the maternal circulation before the maternal immune system mounts a durable anti-D response; because fetomaternal hemorrhage can occur again later, prophylaxis is event-specific and time-specific.\"],\"tk\":\"Anti-D for a sensitizing event does not replace routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis in an unsensitized RhD-negative pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance allows routine prophylaxis as either two doses at 28 and 34 weeks or a single dose at 28–30 weeks, depending on the licensed regimen.\",\"tp\":\"At every bleeding episode, abdominal trauma, invasive procedure, or delivery, check RhD/antibody status and the timing of previous anti-D rather than assuming an earlier dose covers the remainder of pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Anti-D given for a sensitising event does not replace routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis (RAADP) at 28 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Rh immunoglobulin (RhIG) is given after sensitising events (trauma, procedures, early pregnancy complications) and as routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis (RAADP) at 28 and 34 weeks or once at 28-30 weeks. Anti-D for antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is given independent of whether routine prophylaxis has been given.\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[19]],[\"L08_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F124\"},{\"id\":\"F125\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Chorionic villus sampling\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding chorionic villus sampling (CVS), which of the following statements is true?\",\"o\":[\"Transcervical CVS is used only when the placenta is anterior and low.\",\"CVS carries a 50% miscarriage risk even in expert hands.\",\"CVS can be performed safely as early as 6 weeks.\",\"The transcervical or transabdominal route is selected according to placental location and operator assessment.\",\"CVS is used as a screening method for open spina bifida.\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A transcervical route is not restricted to an anterior low placenta; access is individualized according to placental position and procedural feasibility.\",\"Procedure-related pregnancy loss after expert CVS is far below 50% and is generally measured in fractions of a percent above background risk.\",\"CVS should not be performed before 10 weeks, because very early procedures have been associated with fetal limb-reduction defects.\",\"CVS can be performed transcervically or transabdominally, with route chosen according to placental location, anatomy, operator experience, and patient factors.\",\"CVS provides fetal genetic material for diagnostic chromosomal or molecular testing; because it does not sample amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), open neural-tube defects require separate assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"Chorionic villus sampling is an invasive prenatal diagnostic procedure usually performed from about 10 weeks onward and can provide placental tissue for karyotype, microarray, or targeted molecular testing.\",\"Route selection is individualized between transcervical and transabdominal access according to placental location, anatomy, and procedural assessment.\",\"Trophoblastic villi contain fetal genetic material, allowing earlier diagnosis than amniocentesis, but because amniotic fluid is not sampled, open neural-tube defects require separate AFP/ultrasound assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"CVS is an early invasive diagnostic test performed after 10 weeks, with route determined by placental location and procedural feasibility.\",\"pr\":\"Confined placental mosaicism can cause discordance between CVS cytogenetic results and the true fetal karyotype, sometimes requiring confirmatory amniocentesis.\",\"tp\":\"Before CVS, confirm gestational age, viability, placental location, indication, and rhesus D (RhD) status, and counsel about procedure-related loss and possible mosaic results.\",\"cn\":\"Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) is done from about 10 weeks, transcervically or transabdominally according to placental site and operator assessment.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F125\"},{\"id\":\"F126\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Second-trimester recurrent pregnancy loss\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the most common cause of recurrent pregnancy loss in the second trimester?\",\"o\":[\"Uterine anomalies or cervical insufficiency\",\"Inherited thrombophilia\",\"Acquired thrombophilia\",\"Unexplained miscarriages\",\"Chromosomal abnormalities\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Recurrent mid-trimester pregnancy loss classically raises concern for congenital uterine anomalies or cervical insufficiency, especially when losses are painless and preceded by cervical dilation.\",\"Common inherited thrombophilias are not the leading explanation for recurrent second-trimester loss and routine broad thrombophilia testing is not indicated without a relevant thrombotic history.\",\"Antiphospholipid syndrome is an important treatable cause of recurrent pregnancy morbidity but is not the single most common structural pattern behind second-trimester recurrent loss.\",\"A substantial fraction of recurrent pregnancy loss remains unexplained after evaluation, but second-trimester timing specifically increases suspicion for cervical or uterine structural disease.\",\"Fetal chromosomal abnormalities dominate sporadic early first-trimester miscarriage but account for a smaller share of recurrent mid-trimester losses.\"],\"e\":[\"The timing of recurrent pregnancy loss helps prioritize the differential: chromosomal causes predominate early, whereas recurrent painless second-trimester losses raise concern for cervical insufficiency and uterine structural abnormalities.\",\"Uterine anomalies or cervical insufficiency best explain the characteristic pattern of recurrent painless mid-trimester loss.\",\"Weak cervical stroma or distortion of the uterine cavity can permit progressive, often painless cervical dilation as fetal and amniotic-fluid load increases, culminating in membrane prolapse and pregnancy loss.\"],\"tk\":\"Recurrent painless second-trimester loss should prompt evaluation for cervical insufficiency and uterine structural abnormalities.\",\"pr\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome is diagnosed using both clinical pregnancy/thrombotic criteria and persistent laboratory antiphospholipid antibodies, not a single positive test.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically whether prior losses involved painless cervical dilation, membrane prolapse, contractions, fetal anomalies, or placental pathology because those details guide the recurrent-loss workup.\",\"cn\":\"Second-trimester (recurrent) miscarriage is mainly caused by uterine abnormality or cervical weakness, whereas first-trimester loss is mainly chromosomal.\",\"sl\":\"Second trimester: uterine abnormality, cervical weakness, bacterial vaginosis and multiple pregnancy. 50% of first-trimester miscarriages are chromosomally abnormal.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[9]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F126\"},{\"id\":\"F127\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antepartum hemorrhage evaluation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding antepartum hemorrhage, one statement is false:\",\"o\":[\"It is defined as bleeding from the genital tract in late pregnancy before delivery.\",\"In the presence of pain, a digital vaginal examination is advisable before excluding placenta previa.\",\"Placenta previa and placental abruption can occasionally coexist.\",\"Smoking is an independent risk factor for placental abruption.\",\"Ultrasound should be used to localize the placenta in antepartum bleeding.\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antepartum hemorrhage refers to genital-tract bleeding in the latter part of pregnancy and before birth; the exact gestational cutoff varies by guideline.\",\"A digital vaginal examination must be avoided until placenta previa has been excluded, because cervical manipulation can provoke catastrophic placental bleeding.\",\"Placenta previa and abruption are distinct disorders but can coexist, so identifying one does not absolutely exclude the other.\",\"Maternal smoking is associated with increased placental-abruption risk, as are hypertension, cocaine exposure, trauma, and prior abruption.\",\"Ultrasound is essential to identify or exclude placenta previa, although a normal scan does not exclude placental abruption, which remains a clinical diagnosis.\"],\"e\":[\"Antepartum bleeding requires immediate assessment of maternal stability, fetal status, and placental location; placenta previa classically causes painless bleeding, whereas abruption more often causes pain and uterine tenderness.\",\"A digital vaginal examination before excluding placenta previa is unsafe, because cervical manipulation may provoke severe hemorrhage when the placenta overlies the os.\",\"In placenta previa, cervical contact can shear placental attachment in the lower uterine segment and abruptly open maternal vessels, converting limited bleeding into severe hemorrhage.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not perform a digital vaginal examination in antepartum bleeding until placenta previa has been excluded.\",\"pr\":\"Ultrasound has high value for diagnosing placenta previa but limited sensitivity for placental abruption, so a negative scan cannot rule out abruption.\",\"tp\":\"Stabilize the mother first, obtain fetal monitoring when viable, establish placental location, and use a sterile speculum examination if needed before considering any digital examination.\",\"cn\":\"Digital vaginal examination must not be done in antepartum haemorrhage (APH) until ultrasound excludes placenta previa.\",\"sl\":\"If placenta praevia is possible, digital vaginal examination should not be performed until an ultrasound has excluded placenta praevia. Antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is bleeding from or into the genital tract from 24+0 weeks and before birth.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[2,15,27,41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F127\"},{\"id\":\"F128\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Antihypertensive therapy in preeclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following antihypertensive medications may be used in the treatment of hypertension associated with preeclampsia except:\",\"o\":[\"Nifedipine\",\"Furosemide\",\"Methyldopa\",\"Labetalol\",\"Hydralazine\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oral nifedipine is an accepted agent for hypertension in pregnancy and is also used for acute severe hypertension in appropriate immediate-release regimens.\",\"Furosemide is not a routine antihypertensive for preeclampsia because intravascular volume is often contracted; it is reserved for indications such as pulmonary edema.\",\"Methyldopa has a long history of use for non-acute hypertension in pregnancy, although it is less useful for rapid control of severe hypertension.\",\"Labetalol is a first-line drug for hypertension in pregnancy and can be given orally or intravenously depending on urgency.\",\"Intravenous hydralazine is an accepted treatment for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Treatment of hypertension in preeclampsia uses pregnancy-compatible agents such as labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa, and IV hydralazine, chosen according to severity and urgency.\",\"Furosemide is the exception because it is not used routinely to lower blood pressure in preeclampsia; it is reserved for volume-overload complications such as pulmonary edema.\",\"Preeclampsia features endothelial dysfunction and reduced effective intravascular volume despite edema, so indiscriminate diuresis can further compromise uteroplacental perfusion.\"],\"tk\":\"Furosemide is not a routine antihypertensive for preeclampsia and is mainly reserved for pulmonary edema or another clear diuretic indication.\",\"pr\":\"Acute severe pregnancy hypertension requires prompt treatment with agents such as labetalol, oral nifedipine, or IV hydralazine to reduce maternal stroke risk.\",\"tp\":\"When severe blood pressure is confirmed, treat promptly while simultaneously assessing neurologic symptoms, pulmonary edema, platelets, renal and liver function, and the need for delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Furosemide is not an antihypertensive for pre-eclampsia; labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa and hydralazine are used.\",\"sl\":\"Oral antihypertensives: labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa. Intravenous (IV) treatment of severe hypertension: labetalol 20 mg bolus escalating to 220 mg, or hydralazine 5 mg boluses to 15 mg; oral immediate-release nifedipine if no IV access.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[11,44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F128\"},{\"id\":\"F129\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Early-pregnancy ultrasound chronology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements regarding early pregnancy are true except:\",\"o\":[\"The mean gestational sac diameter increases by approximately 1 mm per day in early pregnancy.\",\"Pregnancy location should not be determined from a single serum β-hCG value alone.\",\"Heterotopic pregnancy is more common after assisted reproductive technology.\",\"The yolk sac is first visualized only after fetal cardiac activity has appeared at about 7 weeks.\",\"Fetal cardiac activity can often be seen by transvaginal ultrasound at about 6 weeks.\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Early gestational sac growth is roughly 1 mm/day on average, although growth rate alone is not sufficiently reliable to diagnose viability.\",\"Modern early-pregnancy assessment combines serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) trends and transvaginal ultrasound; a single rigid discriminatory value should not be used by itself to declare pregnancy location.\",\"The incidence of heterotopic pregnancy is markedly higher after assisted reproduction than after spontaneous conception.\",\"The yolk sac appears before the embryo and fetal cardiac activity, usually becoming visible by transvaginal ultrasound around 5–5.5 weeks.\",\"With transvaginal ultrasound, embryonic cardiac activity commonly becomes visible around 5.5–6 weeks as the embryo becomes measurable.\"],\"e\":[\"Early transvaginal ultrasound follows a predictable sequence: gestational sac, yolk sac, embryo, then cardiac activity, although exact timing varies and viability decisions require conservative diagnostic criteria.\",\"The yolk sac is normally visualized before fetal cardiac activity, making a reversed sequence physiologically incorrect.\",\"The yolk sac develops within the chorionic cavity before the embryo is large enough for reliable cardiac visualization, so reversing this sequence creates an anatomically impossible timeline.\"],\"tk\":\"The yolk sac precedes visible embryonic cardiac activity on early transvaginal ultrasound.\",\"pr\":\"A single β-hCG 'discriminatory zone' should not be used to terminate a potentially viable pregnancy; ultrasound findings and serial clinical assessment are safer than one threshold.\",\"tp\":\"When viability is uncertain, document mean sac diameter or crown-rump length precisely and repeat transvaginal ultrasound at the guideline-recommended interval rather than making a premature diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"On early ultrasound the gestational sac appears first, then yolk sac, then fetal pole, then fetal heart at about 6 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational sac increases by 1.3 mm/day; yolk sac should be seen next; fetal pole (gestational sac 18 mm), fetal heart (FH) at 6 weeks (crown-rump length [CRL] 4-10 mm).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F129\"},{\"id\":\"F130\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Breech presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"When examining a woman at term, fetal heart tones are heard loudest above the maternal umbilicus. Which presentation is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"Cephalic presentation\",\"Transverse lie\",\"Breech presentation\",\"Multiple gestation\",\"Shoulder presentation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"In a cephalic presentation, the fetal back and heart are usually heard best below the maternal umbilicus.\",\"With a transverse lie, fetal heart tones are commonly heard around the level of the umbilicus rather than clearly above it.\",\"In a breech presentation, the fetal thorax lies higher in the uterus, so heart tones are often loudest above the umbilicus.\",\"Multiple gestation may produce two distinct fetal heart-rate foci, but location alone above the umbilicus does not establish twins.\",\"A shoulder presentation is associated with transverse lie; the fetal heart is generally heard near the umbilical level depending on back position.\"],\"e\":[\"The location of maximal fetal heart sound intensity reflects the position of the fetal back and thorax.\",\"Heart tones heard best above the maternal umbilicus are typical of a breech presentation because the fetal chest lies in the upper uterus.\",\"In breech, the fetal pelvis occupies the lower uterine segment while the thorax and heart remain cephalad, shifting the auscultatory maximum above the level expected in cephalic presentation.\"],\"tk\":\"Fetal heart tones heard best above the umbilicus suggest breech presentation, whereas cephalic presentations are usually heard below it.\",\"pr\":\"Leopold maneuvers can estimate lie, presentation, and engagement, but ultrasound is used when the presentation is uncertain or management depends on confirmation.\",\"tp\":\"If auscultation and abdominal palpation suggest a non-cephalic presentation at term, confirm fetal lie and presentation with bedside ultrasound before planning delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Fetal heart sounds heard loudest above the maternal umbilicus at term suggest breech presentation.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F130\"},{\"id\":\"F131\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Preeclampsia severe features\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a current severe feature of preeclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"Elevated serum uric acid alone\",\"Persistent blood pressure of at least 160 mmHg systolic or 110 mmHg diastolic\",\"Serum creatinine above 1.1 mg/dL or doubling of baseline in the absence of pre-existing renal disease\",\"Pulmonary edema\",\"Platelet count below 100,000/µL\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An elevated uric acid concentration may accompany preeclampsia but is not itself a current diagnostic severe feature.\",\"Severe-range hypertension, at least 160 systolic or 110 diastolic, is a severe feature because of the risk of maternal stroke and other end-organ injury.\",\"New renal insufficiency meeting this threshold is a severe feature of preeclampsia.\",\"Pulmonary edema is a severe feature indicating major endothelial leak, cardiac loading, or fluid-management complications.\",\"Thrombocytopenia below 100,000/µL is a severe feature and reflects platelet consumption and endothelial injury.\"],\"e\":[\"Current definitions of preeclampsia with severe features emphasize severe hypertension and maternal end-organ dysfunction, including thrombocytopenia, renal insufficiency, impaired liver function, pulmonary edema, or persistent neurologic symptoms.\",\"Elevated uric acid alone is not a severe-feature criterion; heavy proteinuria also no longer determines severity by itself.\",\"Preeclampsia arises from abnormal placentation and systemic endothelial dysfunction, which drives vasoconstriction, capillary leak, platelet activation, renal injury, and cerebral symptoms; severity is therefore defined by organ injury rather than protein quantity alone.\"],\"tk\":\"Severe preeclampsia is defined by severe-range blood pressure or maternal end-organ dysfunction, not by uric acid or the amount of proteinuria alone.\",\"pr\":\"Proteinuria is not required for the diagnosis of preeclampsia when new hypertension is accompanied by qualifying thrombocytopenia, renal injury, liver dysfunction, pulmonary edema, or cerebral/visual symptoms.\",\"tp\":\"When preeclampsia is suspected, repeat severe-range blood pressure promptly and obtain platelets, creatinine, liver tests, symptom review, and fetal assessment because management hinges on organ involvement and gestational age.\",\"cn\":\"Severe pre-eclampsia is defined by severe-range blood pressure or end-organ dysfunction; raised uric acid alone is not a severe feature.\",\"sl\":\"Severe features: persistent blood pressure 160 systolic or 110 diastolic; cerebral or visual disturbance, epigastric or right upper quadrant pain, pulmonary oedema; platelets below 150,000/microL, creatinine 90 micromol/L or more or doubling, transaminases over twice normal. Uric acid is not listed.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[36,37,38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F131\"},{\"id\":\"F132\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Persistent pelvic pain syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about chronic pelvic pain are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Multidisciplinary management may be required when pain has gynecologic, musculoskeletal, gastrointestinal, urologic, and psychosocial contributors.\",\"It is commonly defined as pelvic pain lasting at least 6 months.\",\"It may be associated with endometriosis, pelvic adhesions, bladder pain syndrome, or gastrointestinal disorders.\",\"Obesity is a defining diagnostic criterion for chronic pelvic pain.\",\"A history of sexual or physical abuse may coexist and can influence symptom burden and care needs.\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Persistent pelvic pain often benefits from a multidisciplinary approach, especially when multiple organ systems or central sensitization are involved.\",\"Traditional definitions use pain lasting 6 months or longer, although some modern frameworks emphasize persistent or recurrent pain regardless of a rigid duration cutoff.\",\"Chronic pelvic pain has a broad differential that includes endometriosis, adhesions, bladder pain syndrome, irritable bowel syndrome, and musculoskeletal causes.\",\"Obesity is not a defining criterion for chronic pelvic pain; diagnosis is based on the pain syndrome and its duration/context rather than body mass index.\",\"A history of trauma or abuse is more common in some chronic-pain populations and should be addressed sensitively without assuming a psychogenic cause.\"],\"e\":[\"Chronic pelvic pain is a multifactorial pain syndrome with gynecologic, urologic, gastrointestinal, musculoskeletal, neurologic, and psychosocial contributors.\",\"Obesity is not a defining criterion, whereas recurrent pain over months, endometriosis or other organ-system causes, trauma history, and the need for multidisciplinary care are clinically relevant considerations.\",\"Persistent nociceptive input can produce peripheral and central sensitization, so pain severity may persist even after the original tissue trigger is reduced, explaining why treatment often requires more than lesion-directed surgery.\"],\"tk\":\"Chronic pelvic pain is a syndrome defined by persistent pelvic pain and functional impact, not by obesity or another single demographic feature.\",\"pr\":\"Repeated surgery without a clear target can worsen adhesions and pain; central sensitization and pelvic-floor myalgia should be considered when symptoms are disproportionate to structural findings.\",\"tp\":\"Take a structured pain history covering cycle relation, dyspareunia, bowel and bladder symptoms, musculoskeletal triggers, prior surgery, trauma history, and functional goals before selecting investigations.\",\"cn\":\"Chronic pelvic pain is pelvic pain lasting at least 6 months with multifactorial causes; obesity is not a diagnostic criterion.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F132\"},{\"id\":\"F133\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Follicular phase\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which phase of the menstrual cycle spans from the beginning of menstruation until ovulation?\",\"o\":[\"Luteal phase\",\"Secretory phase\",\"Follicular phase\",\"Corpus luteum\",\"Progesterone\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The luteal phase begins after ovulation and continues until the next menstruation, with progesterone secretion from the corpus luteum.\",\"The endometrial secretory phase follows ovulation under progesterone influence and corresponds roughly to the ovarian luteal phase.\",\"The ovarian follicular phase begins on day 1 of menstruation and ends with ovulation.\",\"The corpus luteum is the post-ovulatory follicular structure that produces progesterone rather than a phase spanning menstruation to ovulation.\",\"Progesterone is a steroid hormone that predominates after ovulation and is not a menstrual-cycle phase.\"],\"e\":[\"The ovarian cycle is divided into a follicular phase before ovulation and a luteal phase afterward.\",\"The interval from the first day of menstruation until ovulation is therefore the follicular phase.\",\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) recruits follicles and rising estradiol drives endometrial proliferation; sustained estradiol then triggers the LH surge, which ends the follicular phase by inducing ovulation.\"],\"tk\":\"The follicular phase extends from day 1 of menstruation to ovulation, and its variable length accounts for most variation in cycle duration.\",\"pr\":\"The ovarian follicular phase corresponds mainly to the endometrial proliferative phase, whereas the luteal phase corresponds to the secretory phase.\",\"tp\":\"When interpreting cycle timing, anchor day 1 to the first day of full menstrual flow and estimate the luteal phase backward from the next menses.\",\"cn\":\"The follicular (preovulatory) phase runs from the start of menstruation to ovulation.\",\"sl\":\"Ovarian cycle: follicular phase (preovulatory) with recruitment and dominant follicle selection, ovulatory phase with the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge, and luteal phase (postovulatory) with the corpus luteum.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F133\"},{\"id\":\"F134\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Obstetric conjugate\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which is the narrowest anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet through which the fetal head must pass?\",\"o\":[\"True conjugate\",\"Diagonal conjugate\",\"Transverse diameter\",\"Obstetric conjugate\",\"Pelvic brim\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The true/anatomic conjugate extends from the sacral promontory to the superior pubic symphysis and is longer than the clinically limiting AP inlet diameter.\",\"The diagonal conjugate extends from the sacral promontory to the inferior border of the pubic symphysis and can be estimated clinically by vaginal examination.\",\"The inlet transverse diameter is the widest transverse dimension and is not the narrowest anteroposterior passage.\",\"The obstetric conjugate is the shortest clinically relevant anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet, extending from the sacral promontory to the closest point on the posterior pubic symphysis.\",\"The pelvic brim is the boundary of the inlet, not a measured pelvic diameter.\"],\"e\":[\"The obstetric conjugate is the shortest anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet available for passage of the fetal head.\",\"It cannot be measured directly on routine vaginal examination; the diagonal conjugate can be estimated and used to infer whether the obstetric conjugate is adequate.\",\"Because the posterior surface of the pubic symphysis projects into the birth canal, the obstetric conjugate is shorter than the anatomic and diagonal conjugates and therefore represents the limiting AP inlet dimension.\"],\"tk\":\"The obstetric conjugate is the limiting anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet for fetal head engagement.\",\"pr\":\"The obstetric conjugate is conventionally estimated from the diagonal conjugate by subtracting roughly 1.5–2 cm, although clinical pelvimetry has limited predictive value for labor outcome.\",\"tp\":\"Do not use isolated pelvimetry measurements to predict vaginal-birth success; integrate fetal size, presentation, station, and actual labor progress.\",\"cn\":\"The obstetric conjugate is the shortest anteroposterior (AP) diameter of the pelvic inlet the fetal head must pass.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F134\"},{\"id\":\"F135\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":7,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Mature cystic teratoma\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following nonmalignant adnexal masses is derived from all three germ cell layers?\",\"o\":[\"Ovarian fibroma\",\"Paratubal cyst\",\"Hydrosalpinx\",\"Mature cystic teratoma\",\"Mucinous cystadenoma\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An ovarian fibroma is a benign sex-cord stromal tumor composed of spindle fibroblastic cells and may be associated with Meigs syndrome.\",\"A paratubal cyst usually arises from mesothelial or Müllerian remnants adjacent to the tube and does not contain derivatives of all germ layers.\",\"A hydrosalpinx is a fluid-distended, obstructed fallopian tube usually caused by prior infection, endometriosis, or surgery.\",\"A mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is a benign germ-cell tumor containing mature ectodermal, mesodermal, and endodermal tissues.\",\"A mucinous cystadenoma is a benign epithelial ovarian tumor lined by mucin-secreting epithelium rather than a three-germ-layer neoplasm.\"],\"e\":[\"A mature cystic teratoma is the most common benign ovarian germ-cell tumor and contains mature tissues derived from ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm.\",\"A mature cystic teratoma uniquely contains mature derivatives of all three germ layers among these adnexal masses.\",\"Teratomas arise from totipotent germ cells, allowing differentiation into skin and neural tissue, fat and cartilage, or respiratory/gastrointestinal epithelium within a single tumor.\"],\"tk\":\"Mature cystic teratomas contain mature derivatives of all three embryonic germ layers.\",\"pr\":\"Mature cystic teratomas are prone to ovarian torsion, and rare malignant transformation most often produces squamous cell carcinoma arising from ectodermal tissue.\",\"tp\":\"On ultrasound, look for echogenic sebaceous material, calcification, shadowing, or a Rokitansky nodule; acute pain should raise concern for torsion.\",\"cn\":\"Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid) is a benign germ-cell tumour containing tissues from all three germ layers.\",\"sl\":\"Teratoma is listed among germ cell tumours, which arise from undifferentiated primordial germ cells; the three germ layers are not stated on the slide.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F135\"},{\"id\":\"F136\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Lethal versus non-lethal fetal anomalies\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following conditions is not generally considered lethal or incompatible with sustained postnatal survival?\",\"o\":[\"Anencephaly\",\"Osteogenesis imperfecta, non-lethal type\",\"Bilateral renal agenesis\",\"Thanatophoric dysplasia\",\"Bilateral pulmonary agenesis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Anencephaly lacks major portions of the cerebral hemispheres and skull and is a lethal neural-tube defect, although brief postnatal survival can occur.\",\"Non-lethal osteogenesis imperfecta includes survivable collagen disorders with recurrent fractures, blue sclerae, hearing loss, or deformity depending on subtype.\",\"Bilateral renal agenesis causes severe oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia, making sustained extrauterine survival impossible.\",\"Thanatophoric dysplasia is a severe skeletal dysplasia with a very small thorax and lethal pulmonary hypoplasia.\",\"Bilateral pulmonary agenesis prevents adequate gas exchange and is incompatible with sustained postnatal survival.\"],\"e\":[\"Some fetal disorders are uniformly or nearly uniformly lethal because they prevent development of organs essential for ventilation, neurologic function, or renal-amniotic fluid physiology.\",\"Non-lethal osteogenesis imperfecta differs because many OI subtypes permit long-term survival despite bone fragility and deformity.\",\"In lethal anomalies such as bilateral renal agenesis or severe skeletal dysplasia, death is driven chiefly by profound pulmonary hypoplasia or absent effective respiratory capacity.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that osteogenesis imperfecta is a spectrum with both lethal and non-lethal forms, so the diagnosis alone does not imply nonviability.\",\"pr\":\"Type II osteogenesis imperfecta is typically perinatally lethal, whereas types I, III, and IV can be compatible with survival.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling after a fetal anomaly diagnosis, define the exact phenotype or molecular subtype before using prognostic terms such as lethal or non-lethal.\",\"cn\":\"Anencephaly, bilateral renal agenesis, bilateral pulmonary agenesis and thanatophoric dysplasia are lethal; non-lethal osteogenesis imperfecta types allow long-term survival.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F136\"},{\"id\":\"F137\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Presumptive symptoms of early pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not a typical presumptive symptom of early pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Amenorrhea\",\"Bleeding\",\"Urinary frequency\",\"Breast tenderness\",\"Nausea and vomiting\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Amenorrhea is a common presumptive sign because continued progesterone support prevents the expected menstrual shedding after implantation.\",\"Bleeding is not a typical presumptive symptom; first-trimester bleeding instead warrants assessment for causes such as threatened pregnancy loss or ectopic pregnancy.\",\"Urinary frequency can begin early as renal blood flow and pelvic vascularity increase, even before the uterus becomes markedly enlarged.\",\"Breast tenderness reflects estrogen- and progesterone-mediated glandular change and is a common early pregnancy symptom.\",\"Nausea and vomiting commonly begin in the first trimester as human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) concentrations rise, with severity varying widely.\"],\"e\":[\"Presumptive symptoms of pregnancy include amenorrhea, nausea, breast changes, and urinary frequency, but none confirms pregnancy by itself.\",\"Bleeding is not part of the usual presumptive symptom complex and should be characterized by amount, pain, and gestational context.\",\"Early-pregnancy symptoms reflect rapidly changing hCG, estrogen, and progesterone levels plus pelvic vascular and uterine changes.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish common presumptive symptoms of pregnancy from warning symptoms such as vaginal bleeding that require clinical assessment.\",\"pr\":\"Light spotting may occur in viable pregnancies, but painful or heavy bleeding raises concern for miscarriage or ectopic pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Ask every patient with early-pregnancy bleeding about pain, syncope, shoulder-tip pain, and bleeding volume before deciding on outpatient evaluation.\",\"cn\":\"Amenorrhoea, nausea and vomiting, breast tenderness and urinary frequency are presumptive symptoms of pregnancy; bleeding is not.\",\"sl\":\"Nausea and vomiting occur in about 70-80% of pregnancies; urinary frequency is common in the first and third trimesters; breasts enlarge and become tender with prominent veins and darker areolae.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[41,43,56]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F137\"},{\"id\":\"F138\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Complete breech presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes a complete breech presentation?\",\"o\":[\"Lower extremities flexed at the hips and extended at the knees\",\"Lower extremities flexed at the hips and both knees flexed\",\"A foot in the birth canal\",\"One or both hips are not flexed\",\"Both feet or knees below the breech\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Frank breech has flexed hips with extended knees, so the buttocks present without the feet.\",\"Complete breech has flexion at both the hips and knees, producing a cross-legged posture.\",\"A footling breech occurs when one or both feet present below the buttocks and may enter the birth canal first.\",\"Incomplete or footling breech occurs when one or both hips are not maintained in flexion, allowing a foot or knee to present below the buttocks.\",\"Feet or knees lying below the breech describe an incomplete or footling/kneeling breech, which carries a higher risk of cord prolapse.\"],\"e\":[\"Breech presentations are classified by the relationship of the fetal hips and knees.\",\"A complete breech has both hips flexed and both knees flexed, whereas frank breech has extended knees and footling breech has a presenting foot.\",\"The configuration matters because a poorly filling presenting part increases the chance of umbilical cord prolapse and influences delivery planning.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify complete breech by flexion of both fetal hips and both knees.\",\"pr\":\"Frank breech is the most common breech subtype at term and has flexed hips with extended knees.\",\"tp\":\"Document the breech subtype, fetal head attitude, estimated weight, placental location, and operator expertise before discussing external cephalic version (ECV) or planned mode of birth.\",\"cn\":\"Complete breech: both hips flexed and both knees flexed; frank breech has extended knees.\",\"sl\":\"Complete breech (5-10%): flexed at the hips and flexed at the knees. Frank breech (50-70%): both hips flexed, knees extended. Footling breech (10-30%): one or both hips extended.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F138\"},{\"id\":\"F139\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oncogenic versus low-risk HPV types\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following human papillomavirus (HPV) types are high risk for cervical cancer except:\",\"o\":[\"33\",\"35\",\"6\",\"18\",\"16\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"HPV 33 belongs to the high-risk oncogenic alpha-papillomavirus group associated with cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and carcinoma.\",\"HPV 35 is a recognized high-risk carcinogenic genotype linked to cervical precancer and cancer.\",\"HPV 6 is a low-risk genotype strongly associated with anogenital warts rather than invasive cervical cancer.\",\"HPV 18 is a major high-risk type and is disproportionately associated with cervical adenocarcinoma.\",\"HPV 16 is the most important oncogenic genotype and accounts for a large fraction of cervical squamous cancers and high-grade lesions.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV is the central causal event in most cervical cancers.\",\"HPV 6 is low risk, whereas types 16, 18, 33, and 35 belong to oncogenic groups associated with high-grade lesions.\",\"High-risk viral proteins E6 and E7 promote degradation of p53 and retinoblastoma-pathway control, facilitating dysplasia and malignant transformation.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate low-risk HPV 6/11 from oncogenic high-risk HPV genotypes such as 16 and 18.\",\"pr\":\"HPV 16 and 18 account for the largest share of cervical cancers, but vaccination covers additional oncogenic genotypes in the 9-valent vaccine.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret HPV results together with age, cytology, and local screening algorithms rather than treating a positive high-risk result as a cancer diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) 6 is low-risk, causing genital warts; HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 35 and 45 are high-risk oncogenic types.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors: human papillomavirus (HPV) 16, 18, 31, 33, 45; 50% are caused by HPV 16 and 18.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[10,45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F027\"},{\"id\":\"F140\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":5,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Syphilis in pregnancy and congenital infection\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"One of the following statements about syphilis is wrong:\",\"o\":[\"Chancre is a painful nodule\",\"Could be transmitted vertically during labor\",\"Secondary syphilis presents as a polymorphic rash on palms and soles\",\"Early latent disease has no clinical evidence\",\"Could be transmitted to the fetus\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The primary syphilitic chancre is classically painless, firm, and clean-based rather than a painful nodule.\",\"Syphilis is transmitted predominantly transplacentally, but perinatal exposure to infectious genital lesions can also transmit infection around delivery.\",\"Secondary syphilis can cause a diffuse maculopapular eruption involving the palms and soles, often with systemic symptoms or generalized lymphadenopathy.\",\"Latent syphilis has reactive serology without clinical manifestations; early latent disease follows recently acquired infection.\",\"Maternal infection can cross the placenta and cause congenital syphilis, fetal loss, hydrops, prematurity, or neonatal disease.\"],\"e\":[\"Syphilis progresses through primary, secondary, latent, and tertiary stages, while pregnancy introduces the additional risk of transplacental fetal infection.\",\"A primary chancre is typically painless, making the description of a painful nodule the false statement.\",\"Treponema pallidum disseminates hematogenously, and placental transmission can produce multisystem fetal disease even when maternal symptoms are mild or absent.\"],\"tk\":\"Remember that the primary syphilitic chancre is classically painless, and maternal infection can cause congenital syphilis.\",\"pr\":\"Penicillin G remains the only proven therapy that adequately treats maternal syphilis and prevents congenital infection.\",\"tp\":\"Verify maternal stage, previous treatment, quantitative nontreponemal titer, and partner treatment; involve obstetric and infectious-disease teams when infection is diagnosed during pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"The primary syphilitic chancre is typically a single, painless, indurated anogenital ulcer.\",\"sl\":\"Primary syphilis: chancre (develops from a single papule), anogenital, single, painless and indurated with clean base. Transmission is by direct contact or vertically during pregnancy; secondary syphilis is multisystem with rash; latent disease is asymptomatic.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[42,43,44,46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F140\"},{\"id\":\"F141\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Operative vaginal instruments\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"One of the following forceps or vacuum associations is wrongly matched:\",\"o\":[\"Wrigley forceps — rotational delivery\",\"Piper forceps — delivery of the aftercoming head of breech\",\"Forceps delivery — selected preterm births\",\"Kielland forceps — midcavity rotational delivery\",\"Metallic vacuum — selected occipitoposterior positions\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrigley forceps are short outlet forceps designed for low/outlet delivery, not for major rotational correction of a malpositioned midcavity head.\",\"Piper forceps are designed to assist delivery of the aftercoming head in breech birth.\",\"Forceps may be chosen instead of vacuum in selected preterm births because vacuum extraction at early gestations raises concern for fetal scalp and intracranial injury.\",\"Kielland forceps have a sliding lock and minimal pelvic curve that permit rotation of a malpositioned fetal head during appropriately selected midcavity operative birth.\",\"A metal vacuum cup can provide better traction than a soft cup in some occipitoposterior or rotational deliveries, although instrument choice depends on position and operator skill.\"],\"e\":[\"Operative vaginal instruments have distinct designs for station, rotation, and fetal presentation.\",\"The mismatched association is Wrigley forceps with rotational delivery; Wrigley forceps are short low/outlet forceps, while Kielland forceps are the classic rotational instrument.\",\"Instrument selection balances traction and rotational requirements against fetal and maternal injury; at earlier gestations, forceps may be favored over ventouse because vacuum can increase scalp and intracranial trauma.\"],\"tk\":\"Match operative instruments to their intended use: Wrigley for low/outlet use, Kielland for rotation, and Piper for the aftercoming breech head.\",\"pr\":\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) advises that Kielland rotational forceps should be used only by experienced operators or under direct experienced supervision.\",\"tp\":\"Before any assisted vaginal birth, confirm full dilation, ruptured membranes, exact head position, station, adequate pelvis, analgesia, empty bladder, and a contingency plan for failed instrumentation.\",\"cn\":\"Wrigley forceps are low/outlet forceps; Kielland forceps are used for rotation and Piper forceps for the aftercoming breech head.\",\"sl\":\"If the aftercoming head does not deliver spontaneously, use the Mauriceau-Smellie-Veit manoeuvre or suprapubic pressure; Piper forceps may be used to assist delivery of the head. Wrigley and Kielland forceps are not covered.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F141\"},{\"id\":\"F142\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Acute VTE management in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is true in the management of suspected venous thromboembolism during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"A resuscitative hysterotomy should wait until 30 minutes of unsuccessful maternal resuscitation after 20 weeks\",\"The affected leg should never be elevated because elevation can dislodge thrombus\",\"Venous thromboembolism is less common postpartum than during pregnancy\",\"Therapeutic low-molecular-weight heparin should be started while objective testing is arranged unless strongly contraindicated\",\"Mobilization with graduated compression stockings should be discouraged\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal cardiac-arrest guidance prioritizes resuscitative hysterotomy within minutes, not a 30-minute delay, when the uterus is large enough to impede resuscitation and there is no return of circulation.\",\"Leg elevation may be used for comfort and edema; there is no evidence that simple elevation causes clinically important thrombus embolization.\",\"The postpartum period carries a particularly high daily VTE risk, so thromboembolism is not less important after birth.\",\"With clinically suspected VTE, LMWH is started promptly while objective testing proceeds unless anticoagulation is strongly contraindicated.\",\"Once appropriately anticoagulated and clinically stable, mobilization and compression may be used rather than prolonged immobility, which itself increases thrombosis risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy-associated VTE is a major cause of maternal morbidity, and suspected disease requires rapid objective investigation plus empiric anticoagulation when safe.\",\"The supported management statement is to begin therapeutic LMWH while VTE is being excluded by objective testing.\",\"Pregnancy and the puerperium create hypercoagulability through increased procoagulant factors, reduced fibrinolysis, venous stasis, and vascular injury; anticoagulation limits thrombus propagation and embolization.\"],\"tk\":\"In suspected pregnancy-associated VTE, start LMWH promptly while arranging objective testing unless anticoagulation is strongly contraindicated.\",\"pr\":\"LMWH does not cross the placenta and is the standard anticoagulant for most acute VTE treatment during pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Before giving LMWH, assess bleeding risk and timing of neuraxial procedures, then document weight-based therapeutic dosing and the diagnostic imaging plan.\",\"cn\":\"In suspected venous thromboembolism (VTE) in pregnancy, start low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) immediately until objective testing excludes it, unless strongly contraindicated.\",\"sl\":\"In clinically suspected deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or pulmonary embolism (PE), low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) should be commenced immediately until the diagnosis is excluded by objective testing, unless strongly contraindicated. The leg should be elevated with a graduated compression stocking, and mobilisation with stockings encouraged.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[18,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F142\"},{\"id\":\"F143\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Respiratory physiology in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding oxygen consumption in pregnancy, all of the following are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Total body oxygen consumption increases by about 15% to 20% in pregnancy\",\"The respiratory rate does not change in pregnancy\",\"Tidal volume increases by 40% in pregnancy\",\"Pregnancy is a state of respiratory alkalosis\",\"20% of pregnant women complain of dyspnea at some time during pregnancy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal oxygen consumption rises by roughly 20% because of increased metabolic demand from the uterus, placenta, fetus, kidneys, and maternal tissues.\",\"The resting respiratory rate changes little in normal pregnancy; increased ventilation is driven mainly by a larger tidal volume.\",\"Tidal volume increases substantially, commonly by about 30–40%, raising minute ventilation.\",\"Progesterone stimulates ventilation, lowering PaCO2 and producing a compensated chronic respiratory alkalosis with renal bicarbonate loss.\",\"Physiologic dyspnea is much more common than 20%, affecting a large proportion of healthy pregnant patients despite normal oxygenation.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases minute ventilation and oxygen consumption while resting respiratory rate remains near baseline.\",\"Physiologic breathlessness is common during pregnancy, so the claim that only 20% are affected understates its frequency; ventilatory demand rises substantially.\",\"Progesterone increases central respiratory drive, expanding tidal volume and reducing PaCO2; renal bicarbonate excretion then compensates for the resulting respiratory alkalosis.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal pregnancy produces higher tidal volume and minute ventilation with little change in respiratory rate.\",\"pr\":\"A mild fall in PaCO2 to roughly the low 30s mmHg is physiologic in pregnancy; a 'normal' nonpregnant PaCO2 near 40 mmHg can signal relative hypoventilation in a sick pregnant patient.\",\"tp\":\"Do not attribute new dyspnea automatically to pregnancy; check oxygen saturation and assess for anemia, asthma, infection, pulmonary edema, and thromboembolism when symptoms are disproportionate.\",\"cn\":\"Physiological breathlessness is common in pregnancy (far more than 20%); tidal volume rises with unchanged respiratory rate, producing respiratory alkalosis.\",\"sl\":\"Tidal volume increases 30-40% with an unchanged respiratory rate, so minute ventilation rises 30-50%; oxygen consumption rises 20-30%; blood gases show a compensated respiratory alkalosis. Physiological dyspnoea is common, often from the first trimester.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[31,33,35]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F143\"},{\"id\":\"F144\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Second-trimester quadruple screening\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The quadruple screen evaluates all of the following markers except:\",\"o\":[\"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)\",\"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)\",\"Unconjugated estriol\",\"Fetal lung maturity\",\"Inhibin-A\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal serum AFP is one component of the quadruple screen and is altered by conditions including open neural-tube defects and aneuploidy.\",\"The quadruple screen measures maternal serum hCG, which contributes to trisomy-risk estimation in combination with the other analytes.\",\"Unconjugated estriol reflects fetoplacental steroid metabolism and is typically reduced in trisomy 21 and trisomy 18 screening patterns.\",\"Fetal lung maturity is assessed by different late-gestation methods; it is not a biochemical analyte in the quadruple aneuploidy screen.\",\"Inhibin A is included in the quadruple screen and is often elevated in pregnancies affected by trisomy 21.\"],\"e\":[\"The second-trimester quadruple screen measures AFP, hCG, unconjugated estriol, and inhibin A to estimate aneuploidy risk.\",\"Fetal lung maturity is unrelated to this screening panel and is not one of its biochemical markers.\",\"Marker combinations change because fetal, placental, and maternal hormone production differs with aneuploidy; the panel provides risk estimation rather than diagnosis.\"],\"tk\":\"Know the four analytes in the quadruple screen: AFP, hCG, unconjugated estriol, and inhibin A.\",\"pr\":\"A positive serum screen requires counseling and an appropriate next step such as diagnostic testing or further risk assessment, not a presumption that aneuploidy is present.\",\"tp\":\"Always confirm gestational dating before interpreting serum-screen results because incorrect gestational age can materially alter calculated risk.\",\"cn\":\"The second-trimester quadruple screen measures alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), unconjugated estriol and inhibin A.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F144\"},{\"id\":\"F145\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Routine antenatal booking blood tests\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not routinely included among booking blood tests in an uncomplicated pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Complete blood count\",\"Blood group and rhesus D (RhD) status\",\"Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) screening\",\"Syphilis screening\",\"Liver function tests\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A complete blood count is routinely obtained to detect maternal anemia and hematologic abnormalities early in pregnancy.\",\"ABO group and rhesus D (RhD) status are established at booking to identify transfusion needs and risk of RhD alloimmunization.\",\"Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing is routinely offered because diagnosis permits antiretroviral treatment that markedly reduces vertical transmission.\",\"Syphilis screening is routinely offered because untreated maternal infection can cause fetal loss and congenital syphilis.\",\"Routine liver-function testing is reserved for a clinical indication such as suspected hepatic disease, preeclampsia, or cholestasis, rather than universal booking.\"],\"e\":[\"Initial antenatal testing targets conditions in which early detection changes care, including anemia, blood-group incompatibility, and vertically transmissible infection.\",\"Liver function tests are not part of routine universal booking blood work in an otherwise uncomplicated pregnancy.\",\"Screening policy prioritizes tests with established maternal-fetal benefit, whereas biochemical panels such as liver function tests (LFTs) are used selectively when clinical findings suggest hepatic or hypertensive disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Routine booking blood work includes CBC, blood group/RhD, and infectious-disease screening, while LFTs are indication-driven.\",\"pr\":\"Current National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) antenatal care recommends first-appointment full blood count, blood group, and RhD testing, alongside national infectious-disease screening.\",\"tp\":\"When booking history reveals pruritus, jaundice, hypertension, severe vomiting, or known liver disease, add targeted liver investigations rather than relying on the routine panel.\",\"cn\":\"Routine booking bloods include complete blood count (CBC), blood group, Rhesus status and infection screening; liver function tests are not routine.\",\"sl\":\"Booking investigations: complete blood count (CBC), blood group (BG), urine analysis and culture, hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), rubella IgG and screening for sexually transmitted diseases (STDs).\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F145\"},{\"id\":\"F146\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), which statement is wrong?\",\"o\":[\"Bacterial vaginosis is associated with an increased risk of PID\",\"Deep dyspareunia can occur with PID\",\"Pregnant women with suspected PID can usually be managed as outpatients with oral azithromycin and cefuroxime\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are recognized causes, although many cases are polymicrobial\",\"PID may be subclinical and later present through tubal-factor infertility\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Bacterial vaginosis-associated flora are linked with upper-genital-tract infection and can contribute to the polymicrobial microbiology of PID.\",\"Deep dyspareunia can occur when upper-genital-tract inflammation produces cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness.\",\"Suspected PID in pregnancy warrants hospitalization and intravenous (IV) antimicrobial therapy because maternal morbidity and preterm-delivery risk are increased.\",\"C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae are important sexually transmitted causes, but anaerobes and other vaginal flora can also participate.\",\"PID may be mild or subclinical yet cause tubal scarring and impaired ciliary function, later presenting as infertility or ectopic pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"PID is an ascending infection involving the endometrium, fallopian tubes, ovaries, or contiguous pelvic structures and is frequently polymicrobial.\",\"Pregnancy changes the management threshold: suspected PID in a pregnant patient warrants hospitalization and intravenous antimicrobial therapy rather than routine outpatient treatment.\",\"Pregnancy-associated PID can threaten both mother and fetus because upper-genital infection promotes systemic inflammation, preterm birth, and maternal infectious complications.\"],\"tk\":\"Treat suspected PID in pregnancy as a higher-risk presentation requiring inpatient assessment and parenteral antibiotics.\",\"pr\":\"Even mild or subclinical PID can leave tubal scarring, increasing later risks of infertility and ectopic pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"In any patient with suspected PID, perform pregnancy testing, assess for tubo-ovarian abscess and sepsis, and obtain sexually transmitted infection (STI) testing while starting empiric treatment when clinical criteria are met.\",\"cn\":\"Suspected pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) in pregnancy needs hospital admission and intravenous antibiotics, not outpatient oral treatment.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F146\"},{\"id\":\"F147\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Lactational mastitis treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding mastitis, treatment includes all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"Analgesics\",\"Antibiotics\",\"Moist heat\",\"Steroids\",\"Nursing or manual expression of milk\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Analgesia, commonly with an NSAID when appropriate, reduces pain and inflammation and helps the patient continue breast emptying.\",\"Antibiotics are appropriate when bacterial mastitis is suspected, especially with systemic illness or persistent focal inflammatory symptoms.\",\"Comfort measures such as warmth before feeding may facilitate milk flow, although excessive heat or vigorous massage can worsen inflammation.\",\"Systemic corticosteroids are not routine lactational mastitis therapy because they do not treat milk stasis or common bacterial infection.\",\"Continued breastfeeding or milk expression is generally encouraged because effective drainage helps relieve stasis and maintains lactation.\"],\"e\":[\"Lactational mastitis is usually managed with continued milk removal, analgesia, supportive care, and antibiotics when bacterial infection is likely.\",\"Routine systemic steroids are not part of standard mastitis treatment.\",\"Milk stasis and ductal inflammation can permit bacterial overgrowth, so maintaining physiologic drainage reduces intraductal pressure while antimicrobials treat suspected bacterial disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Core mastitis management includes effective breast emptying and appropriate analgesia, with antibiotics when indicated.\",\"pr\":\"Failure to improve within about 24–48 hours despite appropriate therapy should prompt assessment for breast abscess or resistant organisms.\",\"tp\":\"Inspect for a fluctuant mass and arrange ultrasound if abscess is suspected; drainage plus continued lactation support is usually required.\",\"cn\":\"Lactational mastitis is managed with continued milk removal, analgesia (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) and antibiotics if bacterial; steroids have no role.\",\"sl\":\"Inflammatory mastitis: non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), ice; bacterial mastitis (systemic symptoms or no improvement in 24 h): antibiotics; keep feeding from both breasts as milk remains safe.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F147\"},{\"id\":\"F148\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Clinical signs of placental separation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Signs of placental separation include all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"The uterus rises because the placenta passes down into the lower uterine segment and vagina\",\"The uterus becomes globular and firm\",\"The umbilical cord protrudes further out of the vagina\",\"There is a sudden gush of blood\",\"Maternal heart rate increases\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The uterine fundus can rise in the abdomen as the separated placenta descends into the lower uterine segment.\",\"After separation, the uterus becomes firm and globular as myometrial contraction reduces the placental bed.\",\"Apparent lengthening of the umbilical cord at the introitus occurs as the placenta descends.\",\"A sudden gush of blood can occur as the placenta detaches and retroplacental blood is released through the cervix.\",\"Maternal tachycardia is not a specific placental-separation sign; an increased pulse should prompt assessment for pain, anxiety, hypovolemia, or hemorrhage.\"],\"e\":[\"Classic signs of placental separation are a firm globular uterus, fundal rise, cord lengthening, and a gush of blood.\",\"Maternal tachycardia is not part of this physiologic sign set and should trigger assessment for hemorrhage or hemodynamic stress.\",\"Myometrial contraction shears the placenta from the decidua and constricts uterine vessels, while descent of the detached placenta alters fundal shape and cord position.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize placental separation by uterine shape change, fundal rise, cord lengthening, and a blood gush, not maternal tachycardia.\",\"pr\":\"Controlled cord traction should be performed only when separation is evident and with uterine countertraction to reduce inversion risk.\",\"tp\":\"During the third stage, continue to monitor pulse, blood pressure, uterine tone, and blood loss even when placental-separation signs appear normal.\",\"cn\":\"Signs of placental separation are a gush of blood, cord lengthening, fundal rise and a firm globular uterus, not maternal tachycardia.\",\"sl\":\"Signs of placental separation: fresh show of blood from the vagina, the umbilical cord lengthens outside the vagina, the fundus rises up, the uterus becomes firm and globular.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F148\"},{\"id\":\"F149\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ovarian tumor markers\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"One of the following ovarian tumor and marker matches is wrong:\",\"o\":[\"Serous ovarian tumour - CA125\",\"Granulosa - Inhibin\",\"Endodermal sinus tumor - CA19-9\",\"Choriocarcinoma - B-hCG\",\"Dysgerminoma - lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Serous epithelial ovarian malignancy commonly raises CA-125, but CA-125 lacks specificity for population screening because benign conditions can also elevate it.\",\"Granulosa-cell tumors can secrete inhibin, making inhibin A/B useful for diagnosis and surveillance.\",\"A yolk-sac or endodermal sinus tumor is associated with alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) rather than CA19-9.\",\"Gestational or ovarian choriocarcinoma produces beta-hCG, often at very high concentrations.\",\"Dysgerminoma is classically associated with elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), reflecting high tumor-cell turnover.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovarian tumor markers are chosen according to the likely histologic cell lineage.\",\"The mismatched pair is endodermal sinus tumor with CA19-9; yolk-sac tumors produce AFP.\",\"Tumor-marker secretion reflects cellular differentiation: yolk-sac elements synthesize AFP, trophoblastic tissue produces hCG, and granulosa cells produce inhibin.\"],\"tk\":\"Match ovarian tumor markers to histology, especially AFP for yolk-sac tumor, hCG for choriocarcinoma, inhibin for granulosa tumor, and LDH for dysgerminoma.\",\"pr\":\"CA-125 is useful for selected adnexal-mass assessment and follow-up but is not a population screening test because many benign conditions can elevate it.\",\"tp\":\"In a young patient with an adnexal mass, order tumor markers selectively based on age and imaging phenotype rather than reflexively ordering every marker.\",\"cn\":\"Endodermal sinus (yolk sac) tumour secretes alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), not carbohydrate antigen 19-9 (CA19-9).\",\"sl\":\"Granulosa cell tumour: inhibin. Germ cell tumours (yolk sac, dysgerminoma, embryonal, choriocarcinoma): alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)/human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). High-grade serous carcinoma: raised cancer antigen 125 (CA-125).\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[6,7,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F149\"},{\"id\":\"F150\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal tachycardia causes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the most common cause of fetal tachycardia?\",\"o\":[\"Drug-induced\",\"Thyroid storm\",\"Maternal fever\",\"Cardiac arrhythmia\",\"Fetal anemia\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. Maternal drugs such as beta-agonists or atropine can raise the fetal heart rate, but they are a less frequent cause than maternal fever.\",\"Wrong. Maternal thyrotoxicosis or thyroid storm can cause fetal tachycardia, but it is rare.\",\"Correct. Maternal fever, most often from intrapartum infection such as chorioamnionitis, is the most common cause of fetal tachycardia because it raises fetal metabolic rate and sympathetic activity.\",\"Wrong. Primary fetal tachyarrhythmias such as supraventricular tachycardia are uncommon and usually give rates above 200 beats per minute.\",\"Wrong. Fetal anemia, for example from red cell alloimmunization, can cause tachycardia through raised cardiac output, but it is much less common than maternal fever.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal tachycardia is a sustained elevated baseline and can arise from maternal fever, drugs, fetal infection, anemia, hypoxia, or arrhythmia.\",\"Maternal fever is the commonest listed cause and should prompt assessment for infection, hydration status, and other intrapartum contributors.\",\"Increased maternal temperature raises fetal metabolic demand and sympathetic activity, increasing the fetal sinus rate without necessarily implying a primary cardiac rhythm disorder.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate fetal tachycardia first for common reversible causes, especially maternal fever and medication exposure.\",\"pr\":\"A fetal heart rate persistently above about 200 beats/min is more suggestive of a fetal tachyarrhythmia than uncomplicated maternal fever.\",\"tp\":\"Recheck maternal temperature, pulse, hydration, medications, uterine activity, and the rest of the cardiotocography (CTG) before attributing fetal tachycardia to a primary fetal arrhythmia.\",\"cn\":\"Maternal fever (pyrexia, often from intra-amniotic infection) is the commonest cause of fetal baseline tachycardia.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F150\"},{\"id\":\"F151\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal growth restriction complications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding fetal growth restriction, all of the following statements are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Growth-restricted fetuses have an increased risk of intrapartum meconium passage and aspiration\",\"Growth restriction is associated with increased adult-onset hypertension\",\"Growth restriction is associated with increased later insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes\",\"Growth-restricted fetuses are more likely to develop hyperglycemia than normally grown fetuses\",\"Fetal growth restriction increases the risk of stillbirth and neonatal morbidity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placental insufficiency and chronic hypoxemia increase the chance of fetal compromise and meconium passage during labor.\",\"Low fetal growth is associated through developmental programming with higher rates of adult hypertension and cardiovascular disease.\",\"Growth restriction is associated with later insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes through developmental metabolic programming after chronic fetal undernutrition.\",\"Growth-restricted neonates are prone to hypoglycemia, not hyperglycemia, because glycogen and fat stores are limited and glucose consumption remains high.\",\"Fetal growth restriction (FGR) increases stillbirth, acidemia, hypothermia, hypoglycemia, and neonatal morbidity, particularly when placental Dopplers are abnormal.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal growth restriction reflects failure to reach expected growth potential and carries important perinatal and long-term metabolic consequences.\",\"Growth-restricted newborns are predisposed to hypoglycemia rather than hyperglycemia because endogenous energy reserves are limited.\",\"Reduced placental nutrient delivery limits fetal glycogen and adipose stores, while chronic adaptation alters endocrine and vascular development, linking FGR with neonatal glucose instability and later cardiometabolic disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Growth-restricted newborns require surveillance for hypoglycemia, hypothermia, polycythemia, and respiratory or intrapartum compromise.\",\"pr\":\"An estimated fetal weight below the 10th centile identifies a small fetus, but Doppler findings, growth trajectory, and placental features help distinguish pathologic FGR from a constitutionally small fetus.\",\"tp\":\"After delivery of a growth-restricted infant, arrange early glucose and temperature monitoring and review the antenatal record for evidence of placental insufficiency.\",\"cn\":\"Growth-restricted fetuses and neonates are prone to hypoglycaemia, not hyperglycaemia, because glycogen and fat stores are depleted.\",\"sl\":\"Depleted liver glycogen and fat stores cause a small abdominal circumference (AC) and neonatal hypoglycaemia and hypothermia. Long-term (Barker hypothesis): hypertension, coronary heart disease, type 2 diabetes and insulin resistance.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[24,55,56]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F151\"},{\"id\":\"F152\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":7,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Swyer syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 17-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea. Breast development is minimal, pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) confirms a small uterus with a short but present vagina, and karyotyping shows 46,XY. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome\",\"Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome\",\"Swyer syndrome\",\"Turner syndrome\",\"46,XX gonadal dysgenesis\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Complete androgen insensitivity is 46,XY with normal breast development but absent Müllerian structures because functioning testes produce anti-Müllerian hormone.\",\"Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser (MRKH) syndrome is usually 46,XX with normal ovaries and secondary sexual characteristics but congenital absence or severe hypoplasia of the uterus and upper vagina.\",\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis; absent gonadal hormone production permits a uterus to persist while estrogen deficiency causes poor breast development.\",\"Turner syndrome is classically 45,X and is often accompanied by short stature and other somatic features rather than a 46,XY karyotype.\",\"46,XX gonadal dysgenesis can cause hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with a uterus present, but the 46,XY karyotype excludes it.\"],\"e\":[\"46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis, or Swyer syndrome, produces phenotypically female external genitalia with persistent Müllerian structures and streak gonads.\",\"The combination of 46,XY, a uterus, and poor breast development identifies Swyer syndrome rather than androgen insensitivity.\",\"Dysgenetic gonads fail to produce both testosterone and anti-Müllerian hormone, so virilization does not occur and the uterus persists, while absent ovarian estrogen limits secondary sexual development.\"],\"tk\":\"Use the triad of 46,XY karyotype, uterus present, and poor spontaneous breast development to recognize Swyer syndrome.\",\"pr\":\"Y-chromosome material in dysgenetic gonads carries a substantial gonadoblastoma risk, so prophylactic gonadectomy is recommended after diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"In primary amenorrhea, document breast development, pubic hair, pelvic anatomy, FSH/luteinising hormone (LH), and karyotype; these features rapidly separate Swyer syndrome, complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS), MRKH, and Turner syndrome.\",\"cn\":\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis with female genitalia, a present uterus, absent puberty and high gonadal malignancy risk.\",\"sl\":\"Swyer syndrome: 46 XY gonadal dysgenesis, no testosterone or anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH), female external genitalia, uterus and tubes present, absent pubertal development, 30% risk of dysgerminoma. Absent breasts with high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) needs a karyotype; in Swyer the small uterus can be missed.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[6]],[\"L28_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F152\"},{\"id\":\"F153\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum psychosis risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 28-year-old woman attends the mental health antenatal clinic at 12 weeks for a booking assessment. This is her first baby. Which condition gives her the highest risk of puerperal psychosis?\",\"o\":[\"Anorexia nervosa\",\"Bipolar affective disorder\",\"Moderate depression\",\"Obsessive-compulsive disorder\",\"Recurrent anxiety\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Anorexia nervosa can complicate pregnancy through maternal malnutrition and fetal growth concerns; acute puerperal mania or psychosis is far more strongly associated with bipolar-spectrum illness.\",\"Bipolar disorder, particularly bipolar I disorder, carries a markedly increased risk of severe postpartum relapse including postpartum psychosis.\",\"Unipolar depression raises the risk of postpartum depressive relapse, but acute mania or psychosis is more strongly linked to bipolar-spectrum illness.\",\"Obsessive-compulsive disorder may worsen around childbirth and can cause intrusive thoughts with preserved insight, which differs from loss of reality testing in psychosis.\",\"Anxiety disorders are common perinatally and can impair sleep and functioning, but they do not confer the marked postpartum manic-psychotic relapse risk seen with bipolar disorder.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum psychosis is a psychiatric emergency characterized by rapid-onset mania, psychosis, severe mood disturbance, or confusion, usually early after birth.\",\"A history of bipolar disorder gives the highest risk among these choices and warrants proactive perinatal mental-health planning.\",\"Childbirth is a potent trigger for mood destabilization in susceptible patients, and abrupt changes in sleep, hormones, and medication exposure can precipitate severe bipolar relapse.\"],\"tk\":\"Women with bipolar disorder are at particularly high risk of postpartum psychosis and need antenatal relapse-prevention planning.\",\"pr\":\"Postpartum psychosis most often begins within the first 2 weeks after delivery and can deteriorate rapidly.\",\"tp\":\"Create a written perinatal mental-health plan before delivery, including medication strategy, sleep protection, early-warning symptoms, and emergency contacts for any patient with bipolar disorder.\",\"cn\":\"Bipolar affective disorder carries the highest risk of postpartum (puerperal) psychosis, about 1 in 5 affected women.\",\"sl\":\"Postpartum psychosis occurs in 1-2 per 1000 births and in about 1 in 5 women with bipolar disorder; women with bipolar disorder need prophylaxis planned during pregnancy.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[58]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F153\"},{\"id\":\"F154\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Bleeding disorders in adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which specific test, additional to routine screening, is undertaken in women with menorrhagia in their teenage years requiring blood transfusion?\",\"o\":[\"Full blood count\",\"Thyroid function test\",\"Testing for coagulation disorders\",\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) pelvis\",\"Hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A full blood count quantifies hemoglobin, hematocrit, and platelet count and is part of routine assessment when menstrual blood loss is severe.\",\"Thyroid testing is reserved for patients with symptoms or signs of thyroid disease rather than ordered routinely for every case of heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"Severe adolescent bleeding from menarche, especially when transfusion is required, should prompt testing for an inherited coagulation disorder such as von Willebrand disease.\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is reserved for selected structural questions and does not evaluate the hemostatic defect suggested by severe bleeding from adolescence.\",\"Endometrial biopsy is rarely indicated in an adolescent without major risk factors because endometrial malignancy is exceptionally uncommon at this age.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding beginning near menarche can be the first manifestation of an inherited bleeding disorder, especially von Willebrand disease.\",\"A teenager whose bleeding is severe enough to require transfusion warrants coagulation-disorder evaluation in addition to routine complete blood count (CBC) assessment.\",\"Defects in von Willebrand factor or coagulation impair stable platelet adhesion and clot formation, producing disproportionate mucosal and menstrual bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding severe enough to cause major anemia should trigger assessment for von Willebrand disease or another coagulation disorder.\",\"pr\":\"A history of epistaxis, easy bruising, prolonged bleeding after dental work, or affected relatives strengthens suspicion for an inherited bleeding disorder.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing long-term therapy, ask specifically whether heavy bleeding has been present since menarche and obtain a focused personal and family bleeding history.\",\"cn\":\"Adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) severe enough to need transfusion requires testing for a bleeding disorder such as von Willebrand disease.\",\"sl\":\"16-year-old with heavy periods since menarche: exclude a bleeding disorder (von Willebrand) with full blood count (FBC), ferritin, coagulation studies and haematology referral. Think coagulopathy in adolescents with heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB).\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[19,27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F154\"},{\"id\":\"F155\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Respiratory physiologic changes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding physiological changes in pregnancy, all of the following statements are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Respiratory rate increases in pregnancy\",\"Lung tidal volume increases in pregnancy\",\"Lung residual volume decreases in pregnancy\",\"Lung vital capacity is generally not changed in pregnancy\",\"Minute ventilation increases in pregnancy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Resting respiratory rate is usually unchanged or only minimally changed in normal pregnancy; increased ventilation comes mainly from a larger tidal volume.\",\"Tidal volume rises by roughly 30–40% during pregnancy, driving the increase in minute ventilation despite little change in respiratory rate.\",\"The enlarging uterus elevates the diaphragm and reduces functional residual capacity and residual volume.\",\"Vital capacity remains approximately unchanged despite changes in its component lung volumes.\",\"Minute ventilation increases substantially because tidal volume rises more than respiratory rate.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy produces major changes in lung volumes and ventilation without a major rise in resting respiratory rate.\",\"Normal pregnancy raises minute ventilation mainly through increased tidal volume, while resting respiratory rate usually changes little.\",\"Progesterone increases respiratory-center sensitivity to carbon dioxide, while diaphragmatic elevation lowers resting lung volumes, producing increased ventilation with reduced functional residual capacity.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal pregnancy increases tidal volume and minute ventilation while respiratory rate remains near baseline.\",\"pr\":\"Reduced functional residual capacity means pregnant patients can desaturate more rapidly during apnea, an important anesthetic and resuscitation consideration.\",\"tp\":\"In an acutely unwell pregnant patient, interpret respiratory rate changes as potentially pathologic rather than assuming tachypnea is a normal adaptation.\",\"cn\":\"Respiratory rate does not change in pregnancy; the rise in minute ventilation is due to increased tidal volume.\",\"sl\":\"Tidal volume increases 30-40% with an unchanged respiratory rate, so minute ventilation rises 30-50%; functional residual capacity (FRC) falls about 20% from reduced expiratory reserve and residual volume; vital capacity is unchanged.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[31]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F155\"},{\"id\":\"F156\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Conservative management of stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Conservative measures for stress incontinence include all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic Floor Muscle Training (PFMT)\",\"Biofeedback\",\"Electrical stimulation\",\"Bladder retraining\",\"Vaginal cones\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Supervised pelvic-floor muscle training strengthens levator ani and periurethral support and is first-line conservative therapy for stress leakage.\",\"Biofeedback can help patients correctly recruit pelvic-floor musculature when awareness or technique is poor.\",\"Electrical stimulation may assist selected patients who cannot generate an effective voluntary pelvic-floor contraction.\",\"Bladder retraining targets urgency, frequency, and urge incontinence by scheduled voiding and urge-suppression strategies rather than urethral support failure.\",\"Vaginal cones provide graded resistance during contraction and can augment pelvic-floor strengthening in selected motivated patients.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence results from inadequate urethral support or sphincteric competence during rises in intra-abdominal pressure.\",\"Conservative treatment centers on pelvic-floor strengthening; bladder retraining is principally an urgency/OAB intervention.\",\"Repeated pelvic-floor contraction improves levator and periurethral support so urethral closure pressure rises during coughing, lifting, or exercise, reducing pressure-related leakage.\"],\"tk\":\"First-line conservative therapy for stress urinary incontinence is pelvic floor muscle training, not bladder retraining.\",\"pr\":\"Mixed urinary incontinence may require both pelvic-floor work and bladder training because stress and urgency mechanisms coexist.\",\"tp\":\"Before referral for surgery, confirm the leakage pattern, perform urinalysis, assess pelvic-floor contraction, and document a supervised PFMT trial.\",\"cn\":\"Conservative stress urinary incontinence (SUI) treatment is pelvic floor training, biofeedback, electrical stimulation, vaginal cones; bladder retraining treats overactive bladder.\",\"sl\":\"SUI conservative measures include pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT), biofeedback, electrical stimulation, vaginal cones and urethral devices. Bladder retraining (cure rate 44-90%) is listed under overactive bladder (OAB) conservative management.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[59,76]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F156\"},{\"id\":\"F157\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Uroflowmetry versus filling cystometry\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding uroflowmetry, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"It is a non-invasive measurement of urine flow during voiding\",\"First bladder sensation at 50–100 mL is routinely measured by uroflowmetry\",\"Maximum flow rate is the highest recorded flow during the void\",\"A voided volume of at least about 150 mL improves interpretability\",\"The test plots urinary flow rate against time\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Uroflowmetry is non-invasive and records the rate and pattern of urinary flow during spontaneous voiding.\",\"First bladder sensation is recorded during filling cystometry as bladder volume rises; uroflowmetry evaluates spontaneous urinary flow during voiding.\",\"The maximum flow rate (Qmax) is the highest instantaneous flow recorded and is interpreted in relation to voided volume and curve shape.\",\"A voided volume around 150 mL or more generally makes a flow trace more interpretable because low volumes can artifactually reduce peak flow.\",\"Uroflowmetry produces a flow-rate-versus-time curve, and an unobstructed void often has a smooth bell-shaped pattern.\"],\"e\":[\"Uroflowmetry evaluates voiding flow, whereas cystometry evaluates bladder filling, sensation, compliance, and detrusor activity.\",\"First bladder sensation belongs to filling cystometry, because sensation is recorded while the bladder is progressively filled rather than during spontaneous voiding.\",\"Flow depends on detrusor pressure, urethral resistance, and voided volume, so abnormal uroflow can suggest obstruction or impaired contractility but cannot by itself define the pressure-flow mechanism.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish uroflowmetry, which measures urinary flow, from cystometry, which measures filling sensation and bladder pressure behavior.\",\"pr\":\"A low Qmax is not diagnostic of outlet obstruction by itself because low voided volume or weak detrusor contraction can produce the same pattern.\",\"tp\":\"Check voided volume and post-void residual when interpreting a poor flow curve; repeat the study if the void was too small to be representative.\",\"cn\":\"Uroflowmetry measures voided volume and flow rate during voiding; bladder sensation is assessed by filling cystometry.\",\"sl\":\"Uroflowmetry: simple, non-invasive, measures voided volume and flow rate; normal peak flow >15 mL/sec, voided volume >150 mL, post-void residual volume (PVRV) <100 mL. Filling cystometry: retrograde bladder filling with pressure measurement.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[33,34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F157\"},{\"id\":\"F158\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hematologic and endocrine changes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding physiological changes in pregnancy, all of the following statements are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Hemoglobin level decreases in pregnancy\",\"Leukocyte count increases in pregnancy\",\"Platelet count decreases in pregnancy\",\"Prolactin levels increase in pregnancy\",\"Total thyroxine level decreases in pregnancy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Plasma volume expands more than red-cell mass, producing physiologic hemodilution and a modest fall in hemoglobin concentration.\",\"Pregnancy produces a physiologic leukocytosis, particularly neutrophilia, which becomes more marked during labor.\",\"Platelet counts can fall mildly because of hemodilution and increased consumption, producing gestational thrombocytopenia in many otherwise healthy pregnancies.\",\"Maternal prolactin rises progressively as estrogen stimulates pituitary lactotroph hyperplasia.\",\"Estrogen increases thyroxine-binding globulin, so total T4 rises rather than falls during pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy causes predictable hematologic and endocrine adaptations including hemodilution, leukocytosis, mild platelet decline, and rising prolactin.\",\"Estrogen increases thyroid-binding globulin, so total thyroxine rises rather than falls even though free-hormone physiology remains tightly regulated.\",\"Increased hepatic TBG synthesis and reduced clearance bind more circulating thyroid hormone, shifting total hormone concentrations upward while free hormone is regulated through the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis.\"],\"tk\":\"Pregnancy increases thyroxine-binding globulin and total thyroid hormone concentrations, so thyroid tests require pregnancy-aware interpretation.\",\"pr\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is usually mild and late; a marked platelet fall should prompt evaluation for preeclampsia/haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP), ITP, or other pathology.\",\"tp\":\"Use trimester-specific thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and free-T4 reference ranges when available instead of interpreting pregnancy values against nonpregnant laboratory ranges.\",\"cn\":\"Oestrogen doubles thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), so total thyroxine rises in pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) doubles, so total T4/T3 rise about 50%. Haemoglobin falls (dilution), white cell count rises to about 15 x 10^9/L, platelets fall slightly and prolactin rises about 10-20 fold by term.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[22,25,49,50]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F158\"},{\"id\":\"F159\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome in recurrent pregnancy loss\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"One of the following is wrong regarding antiphospholipid syndrome:\",\"o\":[\"Laboratory criteria needed for the diagnosis should be repeated twice 2 weeks apart\",\"Diagnosis is based on the Sydney criteria\",\"Could be associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\",\"Pregnant women diagnosed with it should be given low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) during pregnancy\",\"It is a cause of recurrent miscarriages\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Persistent antiphospholipid antibodies require confirmation on two occasions at least 12 weeks apart, not merely 2 weeks apart.\",\"The Sydney framework requires an appropriate clinical event plus persistent laboratory evidence of antiphospholipid antibodies, historically forming the standard diagnostic classification approach.\",\"APS may occur as a primary disorder or in association with systemic lupus erythematosus.\",\"Obstetric APS is commonly managed with low-dose aspirin plus prophylactic heparin/LMWH when indicated to improve pregnancy outcomes.\",\"APS is an established acquired thrombophilia associated with recurrent pregnancy loss, fetal death, and placental complications.\"],\"e\":[\"Antiphospholipid syndrome requires both an appropriate clinical event and persistent antiphospholipid antibody positivity.\",\"Diagnostic laboratory positivity must persist on repeat testing at least 12 weeks apart; a 2-week interval is too short to establish persistence.\",\"Persistence matters because transient antibodies can appear after infection or inflammation, whereas sustained lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, or anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies can drive placental thrombosis and complement-mediated injury.\"],\"tk\":\"Confirm APS laboratory positivity on two tests separated by at least 12 weeks rather than diagnosing the syndrome from a single or closely repeated result.\",\"pr\":\"Obstetric APS can occur without prior venous thrombosis; its clinical criteria include characteristic pregnancy morbidity as well as thrombotic events.\",\"tp\":\"When APS is suspected after pregnancy loss, order the defined antibody panel and plan correctly timed repeat testing before labeling the patient with a lifelong thrombophilia diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) laboratory criteria require persistent antibodies on two tests at least 12 weeks apart, not 2 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome is found in 15% of recurrent miscarriage; screen with anticardiolipin antibodies and lupus anticoagulant (dilute Russell viper venom time); treat with low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) and low-dose aspirin from positive fetal heart (live birth rate 70% vs 10% untreated).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[17,18,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F159\"},{\"id\":\"F160\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Rh alloimmunization and critical antibody titers\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Rhesus (Rh) isoimmunization: one statement is false:\",\"o\":[\"Happen after a silent feto-maternal hemorrhage\",\"Hydrops Usually develop at the same time as, or earlier than, in the previous pregnancy\",\"External cephalic version is a known risk factor\",\"Anti-D antibody titers generally provide limited information regarding the severity of fetal hemolysis in Rh disease\",\"In an initially immunized pregnancy, the fetus is not in serious jeopardy if the titer remains at or higher than 1:16\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Occult fetomaternal hemorrhage can expose an RhD-negative mother to RhD-positive fetal erythrocytes even without obvious bleeding or trauma.\",\"Once sensitization is established, affected subsequent pregnancies can develop fetal anemia earlier and more severely because maternal memory responses are rapid.\",\"External cephalic version can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage, making it a recognized sensitizing event for an unsensitized RhD-negative patient.\",\"Maternal anti-D titers are useful for identifying a critical threshold for fetal surveillance but correlate imperfectly with the severity of established fetal hemolysis.\",\"A titer at or above a laboratory's critical threshold, commonly around 1:16, signals meaningful fetal risk and prompts specialized surveillance rather than reassurance.\"],\"e\":[\"Rh alloimmunization occurs when maternal anti-D IgG forms after exposure to RhD-positive fetal erythrocytes.\",\"A maternal anti-D titer at or above a laboratory critical threshold such as 1:16 is concerning and triggers fetal-anemia surveillance rather than reassurance.\",\"Maternal IgG crosses the placenta and destroys fetal red cells, causing anemia, high-output cardiac failure, and potentially hydrops fetalis.\"],\"tk\":\"A critical maternal anti-D titer should trigger specialist fetal surveillance, commonly with middle cerebral artery Doppler assessment.\",\"pr\":\"Peak MCA systolic velocity above 1.5 multiples of the median is a key noninvasive marker of moderate-to-severe fetal anemia.\",\"tp\":\"Check prior pregnancy history before relying on titers; a previously affected fetus can warrant MCA surveillance even when the current titer alone seems modest.\",\"cn\":\"An anti-D titre at or above the critical level (about 1:16) signals fetal risk and triggers middle cerebral artery (MCA) Doppler surveillance.\",\"sl\":\"In a first sensitized pregnancy, follow the titer until it reaches the critical titer, then follow with middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity (MCA-PSV); if PSV is above 1.5 multiples of the median (MoM), cordocentesis for haematocrit, and intrauterine transfusion if haematocrit is below 30. Sensitization can be silent (asymptomatic).\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[6,16,19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F160\"},{\"id\":\"F161\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Twin pregnancy risk factors and outcomes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding twins, all of the following statements are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Twins deliver at a mean gestational age of about 36 weeks\",\"The perinatal mortality and morbidity for multiple gestations exceed that of singleton pregnancies\",\"Family history of twins is a risk factor for monozygotic twins\",\"Monozygotic twins are more likely to have congenital anomalies than dizygotic twins\",\"Preeclampsia and gestational hypertension are almost doubled in twins\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Twin pregnancies deliver earlier on average than singletons, with a mean gestation roughly in the mid- to late-36-week range depending on population and chorionicity.\",\"Multiple gestation increases prematurity, growth restriction, stillbirth, and neonatal morbidity, so perinatal risk exceeds that of singleton pregnancy.\",\"Familial tendency mainly affects dizygotic twinning through inherited variation in multiple ovulation; monozygotic twinning is relatively constant across populations.\",\"Monozygotic twinning carries increased risk of selected anomalies, especially when embryonic division is delayed or placentation is monochorionic.\",\"Twin gestation increases placental mass and maternal cardiovascular demand, raising the incidence of gestational hypertension and preeclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Twin pregnancy risk depends strongly on zygosity and chorionicity, with higher rates of preterm birth and hypertensive disease than singleton pregnancy.\",\"Familial clustering primarily reflects inherited tendencies toward dizygotic hyperovulation, not monozygotic embryo splitting.\",\"Dizygotic twins arise from two ova and therefore track maternal ovulatory biology, whereas monozygotic twins result from post-fertilization embryo division that is largely sporadic.\"],\"tk\":\"Family history and maternal hyperovulation increase dizygotic, not monozygotic, twinning.\",\"pr\":\"Determine chorionicity in the first trimester, because monochorionic twins require closer surveillance for complications such as twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome.\",\"tp\":\"At the booking scan, document chorionicity and amnionicity clearly and schedule surveillance according to that classification rather than simply labeling the pregnancy 'twins.'\",\"cn\":\"Family history increases dizygotic twinning (maternal line); monozygotic twinning is mostly random and not familial.\",\"sl\":\"Family history: dizygotic (DZ) twinning runs in the maternal line. Monozygotic (MZ) twins: mostly random; slight increase after assisted reproduction (ART).\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F161\"},{\"id\":\"F162\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"CTG deceleration mechanisms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The following statements in a cardiotocography (CTG) trace are true, except:\",\"o\":[\"Variable decelerations are due to cord compression\",\"Early decelerations may be a normal feature with the descent of the fetal head\",\"Variable decelerations are due to head compression\",\"A baseline fetal heart rate of 120 bpm is considered reassuring\",\"A deceleration is a transient decrease in fetal heart rate of at least 15 bpm below the baseline that lasts for 15 seconds\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Variable decelerations are most often produced by intermittent umbilical-cord compression, which causes abrupt fetal heart-rate changes.\",\"Early decelerations mirror contractions and are usually benign, reflecting vagal stimulation from fetal head compression during descent.\",\"Head compression produces early, not variable, decelerations; variable decelerations are linked primarily to cord compression.\",\"A baseline fetal heart rate of 120 beats/min lies within the usual normal range.\",\"A standard deceleration involves a fall of at least about 15 beats/min lasting at least 15 seconds, with further classification based on timing and duration.\"],\"e\":[\"CTG interpretation integrates baseline rate, variability, decelerations, accelerations, and uterine activity.\",\"Variable decelerations reflect umbilical-cord compression, whereas early decelerations classically accompany fetal-head compression during descent.\",\"Cord compression produces rapid baroreceptor and chemoreceptor responses that create abrupt heart-rate drops with variable timing and morphology.\"],\"tk\":\"Associate variable decelerations with cord compression and early decelerations with fetal head compression.\",\"pr\":\"Variable decelerations become more concerning when they are prolonged, repetitive, have slow recovery, or accompany rising baseline or reduced variability.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret each deceleration in the context of contraction timing, baseline trend, variability, and evolving clinical risk rather than labeling a trace from one feature alone.\",\"cn\":\"Variable decelerations on cardiotocography (CTG) are caused by umbilical cord compression; early decelerations reflect fetal head compression.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal distress in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM): variable deceleration reflecting umbilical cord compression caused by oligohydramnios.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F162\"},{\"id\":\"F163\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ovulation timing in a long menstrual cycle\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a 35-day menstrual cycle, ovulation is expected to be on which day?\",\"o\":[\"12\",\"13\",\"14\",\"21\",\"28\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Day 12 would place ovulation about 23 days before the next menses, far longer than the usual luteal interval.\",\"Day 13 is typical only for a shorter cycle and would imply an unusually long 22-day luteal phase in a 35-day cycle.\",\"Day 14 is a teaching approximation for a 28-day cycle, not a fixed ovulation day for all cycle lengths.\",\"With a relatively constant luteal phase of about 14 days, a 35-day cycle predicts ovulation near day 21.\",\"Day 28 would leave only about 7 days of luteal phase, which is substantially shorter than the usual interval.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovulation timing varies mainly because the follicular phase changes in length, whereas the luteal phase is comparatively stable at about 14 days.\",\"In a 35-day cycle, subtracting about 14 days places expected ovulation near day 21.\",\"Follicular recruitment and dominant-follicle maturation determine when sustained estradiol triggers the luteinising hormone (LH) surge, while corpus-luteum lifespan makes the post-ovulatory interval less variable.\"],\"tk\":\"Estimate ovulation at roughly cycle length minus 14 days rather than assuming every patient ovulates on day 14.\",\"pr\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone should be timed about 7 days before the expected next period, not automatically on day 21.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing ovulation, first establish usual cycle length and variability; calendar estimates are less reliable when cycles are irregular.\",\"cn\":\"Ovulation occurs about 14 days before the next menses, so in a 35-day cycle it is expected on about day 21.\",\"sl\":\"If pregnancy does not occur, menstruation appears exactly two weeks after ovulation; the progesterone phase is fixed at 14 days and cycle length varies with the follicular phase.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[6,11]],[\"L33_1\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F163\"},{\"id\":\"F164\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum blues\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding postpartum blues, all are true except:\",\"o\":[\"Commonly in the first 2 weeks\",\"Signs and symptoms include Sadness, Crying, and Confusion\",\"Often resolves by postpartum day 10\",\"Medication is not routinely indicated\",\"Need a referral to a psychiatrist\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Transient tearfulness and emotional lability commonly begin within the first few postpartum days and remain confined to the early puerperium.\",\"Emotional lability, tearfulness, irritability, anxiety, and transient confusion or poor concentration can occur while overall functioning is preserved.\",\"Symptoms are self-limited and usually resolve within about 10–14 days without specific psychiatric treatment.\",\"Uncomplicated transient mood symptoms generally improve with reassurance, sleep, practical support, and observation rather than routine medication.\",\"Routine psychiatric referral is unnecessary when symptoms are mild and self-limited; psychosis, suicidality, severe functional impairment, or persistence beyond about 2 weeks requires urgent reassessment.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum blues is a common, short-lived mood disturbance characterized by tearfulness and emotional lability soon after delivery.\",\"Mild, self-limited symptoms generally resolve with supportive care within about 2 weeks; mandatory psychiatric referral is therefore inappropriate in an uncomplicated presentation.\",\"Rapid hormonal shifts, sleep deprivation, physical recovery, and psychosocial adjustment contribute to transient mood symptoms, while persistence or marked functional impairment suggests postpartum depression or another disorder.\"],\"tk\":\"Postpartum blues is self-limited and usually resolves within 2 weeks, unlike postpartum depression or psychosis.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms lasting beyond 2 weeks, suicidality, severe functional impairment, delusions, or mania require evaluation for a more serious postpartum psychiatric disorder.\",\"tp\":\"Before reassuring a patient with presumed blues, ask directly about self-harm thoughts, infant-safety concerns, sleep, psychotic symptoms, and available support.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum blues are self-limiting within 2 weeks and need reassurance and support, not medication or psychiatric referral.\",\"sl\":\"Postpartum blues affect up to 80% of mothers, onset day 2-3, peak day 4-5, resolve within 2 weeks; manage by reassurance and support, no medication needed; escalate only if symptoms last beyond 2 weeks or there is confusion, self-harm thoughts or loss of function.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[43,48,49]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key E (psychiatric referral) is the intended false statement and is correct. However, slides 48-49 list confusion as a feature of postpartum psychosis and a reason to escalate, so option B (confusion as a blues symptom) is also arguably false by the slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F164\"},{\"id\":\"F165\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Salpingectomy versus salpingotomy for ectopic pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Salpingectomy is the preferred surgical treatment for tubal ectopic pregnancy in which of the following situations?\",\"o\":[\"Healthy contralateral tube\",\"Serum β-hCG of 3000 IU/L in a stable patient\",\"Intact unruptured ectopic pregnancy\",\"Ectopic sac smaller than 4 cm\",\"Damaged contralateral tube with a strong desire to preserve fertility\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"When surgery is required and the contralateral tube is healthy, salpingectomy is generally preferred because removing the affected tube does not materially reduce fertility compared with conservative tubal surgery in this setting.\",\"A serum beta-hCG of 3000 IU/L alone does not require surgery; a stable patient may still qualify for medical, expectant, or operative management depending on the full clinical picture.\",\"An intact unruptured ectopic pregnancy can be eligible for nonoperative management when hemodynamic stability and follow-up criteria are satisfied.\",\"A tubal mass under 4 cm may fall within medical-treatment criteria in selected patients; size alone does not mandate salpingectomy.\",\"With contralateral tubal damage and a strong fertility concern, salpingotomy may be considered to preserve tubal tissue, accepting the need for serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) follow-up.\"],\"e\":[\"Operative treatment of tubal ectopic pregnancy is usually by laparoscopic salpingectomy or salpingotomy, chosen partly according to future fertility risk.\",\"With a healthy contralateral tube, salpingectomy is preferred when surgery is indicated; salpingotomy is considered when the other tube is damaged.\",\"Salpingectomy removes the trophoblastic implantation completely, reducing the risk of persistent trophoblast that can follow conservative tubal incision.\"],\"tk\":\"When ectopic-pregnancy surgery is required, choose salpingectomy if the contralateral tube is healthy; consider salpingotomy when fertility risk from contralateral damage is important.\",\"pr\":\"After salpingotomy, up to a meaningful minority of patients need additional treatment for persistent trophoblastic tissue, so serial hCG follow-up is mandatory.\",\"tp\":\"Before choosing surgery, document hemodynamic stability, ultrasound findings, hCG level, contralateral-tube status, and the patient's fertility priorities.\",\"cn\":\"When surgery is needed for tubal ectopic pregnancy and the contralateral tube is healthy, salpingectomy is preferred over salpingotomy.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F165\"},{\"id\":\"F166\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"harmony\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding after tamoxifen\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 52-year-old woman presented to the postmenopausal bleeding clinic. A transvaginal scan showed an endometrial thickness of 15 mm. She has previously used tamoxifen for 5 years for breast cancer. The next step in her management would be:\",\"o\":[\"High-dose oral progestogens\",\"Do nothing as her bleeding was minimal\",\"Hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy\",\"Mirena IUS insertion\",\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Empiric high-dose progestogens should not precede tissue diagnosis in postmenopausal bleeding with a markedly thickened endometrium and tamoxifen exposure.\",\"Any postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation because even small-volume bleeding can be the presenting feature of endometrial malignancy.\",\"Hysteroscopy with endometrial biopsy permits direct cavity assessment and tissue diagnosis in a symptomatic woman with a 15-mm endometrium and prior tamoxifen use.\",\"A levonorgestrel intrauterine system is not inserted as the next step before excluding endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma.\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is mainly used for local staging of selected confirmed gynecologic malignancies and does not provide the tissue diagnosis required for unexplained postmenopausal bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal bleeding requires prompt exclusion of endometrial cancer and hyperplasia, and tamoxifen exposure further increases endometrial pathology risk.\",\"A symptomatic 52-year-old with 15-mm endometrial thickness needs direct endometrial assessment; hysteroscopy and biopsy provide tissue for diagnosis.\",\"Tamoxifen has estrogen-agonist effects on the endometrium, promoting polyps, hyperplasia, and carcinoma, so ultrasound thickness alone cannot substitute for histologic evaluation in a bleeding patient.\"],\"tk\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding with significant endometrial thickening, especially after tamoxifen, requires endometrial sampling rather than empiric hormonal treatment.\",\"pr\":\"Tamoxifen can produce cystic endometrial changes that make transvaginal thickness less specific, increasing the value of direct evaluation when bleeding occurs.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about duration of tamoxifen use, recurrent bleeding, obesity, diabetes, and other endometrial-cancer risks, then ensure histology is obtained before treatment planning.\",\"cn\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding on or after tamoxifen with a thick endometrium needs hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy.\",\"sl\":\"Tamoxifen increases the risk of endometrial cancer. Reliability of endometrial thickness in postmenopausal women is not applicable to patients on tamoxifen. Hysteroscopy gives a direct view of the endometrium and allows directed biopsy.\",\"s\":[[\"L42_0\",[14,21,24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F166\"},{\"id\":\"F167\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placenta previa risk after prior cesareans\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"According to current evidence, approximately how much higher is the risk of placenta previa after three previous cesarean births compared with no previous cesarean birth?\",\"o\":[\"About 2-fold higher\",\"About 4-fold higher\",\"About 6.5-fold higher\",\"About 15-fold higher\",\"About 30-fold higher\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A single prior cesarean scar raises placenta previa risk, but a roughly 2-fold estimate understates the dose-response seen after three previous cesareans.\",\"An approximately 4-fold increase has been reported after fewer prior cesareans in some cohorts; three previous scars carry a larger pooled relative risk.\",\"A meta-analysis cited in the 2026 Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) placenta praevia guideline reports a relative risk of about 6.5 after three previous cesarean births compared with none.\",\"A 15-fold increase is higher than the pooled estimate for three prior cesareans and would overstate the association for this specific exposure.\",\"A 30-fold increase is not the current pooled estimate for exactly three prior cesareans; very high estimates have mainly appeared with four or more scars in older datasets.\"],\"e\":[\"Previous cesarean birth is a major risk factor for placenta previa, with risk increasing as the number of uterine scars increases.\",\"For three prior cesareans, the 2026 RCOG review cites an older pooled relative risk of approximately 6.5, although estimates vary across studies and populations.\",\"Cesarean scars can alter decidualization and implantation in the lower uterine segment, linking repeated cesareans to both placenta previa and placenta accreta spectrum.\"],\"tk\":\"Placenta previa risk rises in a dose-response fashion with prior cesarean births, and current RCOG evidence cites an approximate 6.5-fold relative risk after three previous cesareans.\",\"pr\":\"A placenta that is previa or low-lying over a cesarean scar should also trigger targeted assessment for placenta accreta spectrum.\",\"tp\":\"Document placental location at the anatomy scan and arrange expert follow-up when a low placenta overlies a previous uterine scar.\",\"cn\":\"Placenta previa risk rises with each previous caesarean; pooled data give about 6.5-fold after three, while Dr. Hadeel's slide quotes an older odds ratio of 22.4 after three.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors for placenta praevia: previous caesarean section odds ratio (OR) 2.2, two previous caesarean sections OR 4.1, three previous caesarean sections OR 22.4.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key gives about 6.5-fold, but the doctor's slide (L08 page 38) gives an odds ratio (OR) of 22.4 after three cesarean sections. No option matches exactly; for the doctors' exam the 6.5-fold option contradicts the slide, and about 15-fold is the closest option (30-fold is almost equally close).\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F167\"},{\"id\":\"F168\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"POP-Q stage 2 anterior prolapse\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient undergoes a POPQ examination, and the first row readings are -1, 0, and -7. This indicates:\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) stage 2 anterior vaginal wall prolapse (cystocele)\",\"POP-Q stage 1 anterior vaginal wall prolapse\",\"POP-Q stage 3 anterior vaginal wall prolapse\",\"Complete uterine procidentia\",\"Posterior vaginal wall prolapse (rectocele)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"With Ba at 0 cm, the leading edge lies at the hymen; a most distal point from −1 to +1 cm is POP-Q stage 2.\",\"POP-Q stage 1 requires the most distal prolapse to remain more than 1 cm above the hymen; Ba at 0 cm has reached the hymenal plane.\",\"POP-Q stage 3 extends more than 1 cm beyond the hymen while remaining at least 2 cm short of total vaginal length; Ba at 0 cm is not beyond the hymen.\",\"Procidentia reflects near-complete or complete apical uterine prolapse, whereas C at −7 cm indicates the apex remains well above the hymen.\",\"A rectocele is staged from posterior points Ap and Bp; the abnormal leading measurement here is anterior point Ba.\"],\"e\":[\"The POP-Q system measures vaginal support relative to the hymen, which is defined as 0 cm; negative values are proximal and positive values protrude beyond it.\",\"A Ba measurement of 0 cm places the leading anterior vaginal wall exactly at the hymen, satisfying stage 2 prolapse.\",\"POP-Q staging is determined by the most distal prolapsing point, allowing reproducible communication rather than older imprecise degree terminology.\"],\"tk\":\"POP-Q stage 2 prolapse has a leading edge between 1 cm above and 1 cm below the hymen.\",\"pr\":\"Anterior prolapse can cause incomplete bladder emptying or stress incontinence, particularly when the vaginal wall reaches or extends beyond the hymen.\",\"tp\":\"Record all six POP-Q points during maximal strain; a single descriptive term such as “cystocele” does not capture apical or posterior support.\",\"cn\":\"In pelvic organ prolapse quantification (POP-Q), a leading point within 1 cm of the hymen (Ba = 0) is stage 2.\",\"sl\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse quantification (POP-Q) system (International Continence Society [ICS] 1996, Bump et al.) is site-specific and quantitative. Older grading of cystocele: 1st degree halfway to the hymen, 2nd degree to the hymen, 3rd degree outside the hymen.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[26,41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F168\"},{\"id\":\"F169\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Chronic pelvic pain definition\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Not true about Chronic pelvic pain:\",\"o\":[\"Endometriosis is a recognized cause\",\"Pain may be intermittent or constant\",\"Symptoms can arise from gynecologic, gastrointestinal, urologic, or musculoskeletal disease\",\"Psychosocial factors can influence symptom severity and disability\",\"Pain present for only 3 months fulfills the usual chronic pelvic pain duration definition\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometriosis is a recognized cause of chronic pelvic pain because ectopic endometrial-like tissue can produce persistent or cyclic pain over months.\",\"Chronic pelvic pain may be intermittent or continuous; its temporal pattern does not need to be constant to meet the syndrome definition.\",\"Symptoms may arise from gynecologic, gastrointestinal, urinary, musculoskeletal, or neuropathic sources, so multisystem contributors are common.\",\"Psychological and social factors can amplify pain-related disability and should be assessed as contributors without implying that the pain is nonorganic.\",\"A duration of only 3 months is shorter than the traditional 6-month threshold used in many chronic pelvic pain definitions, so this statement does not meet that criterion.\"],\"e\":[\"Chronic pelvic pain is generally defined as noncyclic or cyclic pelvic pain lasting at least 6 months and severe enough to impair function or prompt medical care.\",\"Endometriosis is one cause, but patients often have overlapping bladder, bowel, pelvic-floor, neuropathic, and musculoskeletal contributors.\",\"Persistent nociceptive input can promote central sensitization, so a multimodal evaluation is more useful than assuming a single pelvic lesion explains every symptom.\"],\"tk\":\"Chronic pelvic pain usually requires symptoms persisting for at least 6 months and often has multifactorial contributors.\",\"pr\":\"Pain severity correlates poorly with endometriosis stage, so mild-appearing disease can still produce substantial symptoms.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about bowel, bladder, intercourse, movement, and menstrual triggers, then examine the pelvic floor and abdominal wall as well as pelvic organs.\",\"cn\":\"Chronic pelvic pain is defined as intermittent or constant pelvic pain lasting at least 6 months, not 3 months.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F169\"},{\"id\":\"F170\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Proteinuria screening in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The best test for screening for proteinuria during pregnancy is:\",\"o\":[\"Automated urine dipstick testing\",\"24-hour urine protein collection\",\"Serum creatinine alone\",\"Urine microscopy for casts\",\"Renal ultrasonography\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends an automated reagent-strip reader for initial dipstick screening; a result of 1+ or more should be quantified with PCR or ACR.\",\"A 24-hour urine collection is not routinely recommended for quantifying proteinuria because spot PCR or ACR is quicker and sufficiently accurate.\",\"Serum creatinine assesses renal function but does not screen directly for urinary protein loss.\",\"Urine microscopy can identify casts or hematuria in intrinsic renal disease; preeclamptic proteinuria is quantified with PCR or ACR after a positive screening dipstick.\",\"Renal ultrasound evaluates anatomy or obstruction and has no role as the initial proteinuria screen in hypertensive pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Proteinuria in hypertensive pregnancy should be interpreted alongside the full clinical picture rather than in isolation.\",\"NICE recommends automated dipstick screening, followed by protein:creatinine ratio or albumin:creatinine ratio when the dipstick is 1+ or greater.\",\"Spot ratios estimate renal protein loss without the delay and collection error associated with a 24-hour urine specimen.\"],\"tk\":\"Use automated dipstick testing to screen for proteinuria, then quantify a positive result with PCR or ACR.\",\"pr\":\"A PCR of 30 mg/mmol or more is the NICE threshold used to support significant proteinuria in preeclampsia assessment.\",\"tp\":\"When a dipstick is positive, send a spot PCR or ACR and continue maternal-organ and fetal assessment rather than waiting for a 24-hour collection.\",\"cn\":\"Proteinuria in pregnancy is screened with an automated reagent-strip (dipstick) reader, then quantified by protein:creatinine ratio (PCR) or albumin:creatinine ratio (ACR).\",\"sl\":\"Use an automated reagent-strip reading device for dipstick screening for proteinuria; if 1+ or more, quantify with spot protein:creatinine ratio (PCR) or albumin:creatinine ratio (ACR). The 24-hour urine collection is the gold standard to quantify but is inconvenient and not routine.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[29]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F170\"},{\"id\":\"F171\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Post-term pregnancy epidemiology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"False about post-term pregnancy:\",\"o\":[\"Post-term pregnancy is defined as gestation reaching 42+0 weeks or beyond\",\"Accurate first-trimester dating reduces the apparent incidence of post-term pregnancy\",\"With modern ultrasound dating, 15–20% of pregnancies still progress beyond 42 weeks\",\"Advancing gestation beyond term increases risks such as macrosomia and oligohydramnios\",\"Induction is commonly discussed before 42 weeks to reduce risks of prolonged pregnancy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Post-term pregnancy is traditionally defined as pregnancy continuing to 42+0 weeks or beyond.\",\"Reliable first-trimester dating reduces apparent post-term pregnancy by correcting errors from uncertain last-menstrual-period dating.\",\"True post-term pregnancy is far less common than 15–20% when early ultrasonographic dating is used; that percentage substantially overstates contemporary incidence.\",\"Prolonged pregnancy increases risks such as macrosomia, oligohydramnios, meconium passage, and stillbirth, especially as gestation advances.\",\"Planned birth is generally offered before 42 weeks because perinatal risk rises with advancing gestation.\"],\"e\":[\"Post-term pregnancy is defined as 42+0 weeks or later, and its measured incidence falls when gestation is dated accurately by early ultrasound.\",\"An estimate of 15–20% is not appropriate for modern well-dated pregnancies; many historical “post-term” cases reflected inaccurate menstrual dating.\",\"As placental reserve declines, oligohydramnios and fetal compromise become more likely, which is why induction is generally offered before 42 weeks.\"],\"tk\":\"Reliable early ultrasound dating reduces false classification of post-term pregnancy, which begins at 42+0 weeks.\",\"pr\":\"A first-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate ultrasound parameter for establishing gestational age.\",\"tp\":\"Before labeling a pregnancy post-term, verify the best obstetric estimate and review any first-trimester scan rather than recalculating from a late ultrasound.\",\"cn\":\"With accurate first-trimester ultrasound dating, only about 2-5% of pregnancies go beyond 42 weeks, not 15-20%.\",\"sl\":\"Prolonged pregnancy continues beyond 42 weeks, dated by first-trimester ultrasound; incidence 5-10%. Routine ultrasound dating reduced the overall rate from 10-15% to approximately 2-5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[2,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F171\"},{\"id\":\"F172\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Assessment before induction of labour\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"False about Induction of labor (IOL):\",\"o\":[\"Assess the fetal presentation and stability of the presenting part\",\"Record the Bishop score\",\"Confirm a normal fetal heart-rate pattern\",\"Confirm that significant uterine contractions are already present\",\"Review the maternal indication and contraindications to induction\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Assessment of fetal presentation is required before induction because malpresentation can alter the safety or mode of planned birth.\",\"The Bishop score estimates cervical favorability and guides whether cervical ripening or amniotomy/oxytocin is appropriate.\",\"A reassuring fetal heart-rate assessment should be documented before pharmacologic or mechanical induction methods are started.\",\"Established significant uterine contractions indicate that labor may already be underway; induction is intended to initiate labor when it has not begun.\",\"The indication, contraindications, gestational age, and maternal-fetal status should be reviewed before an induction plan is undertaken.\"],\"e\":[\"Before induction, clinicians should confirm that the maternal and fetal status are suitable for the planned method.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) specifically requires assessment of presentation, Bishop score, fetal heart-rate pattern, and absence of significant uterine contractions.\",\"These checks reduce preventable complications such as cord prolapse after amniotomy, inappropriate induction during established labour, or use of a ripening method in an unsuitable cervix.\"],\"tk\":\"Assessment before induction includes presentation, Bishop score, fetal wellbeing, and confirmation that significant labour contractions are absent.\",\"pr\":\"A Bishop score greater than 6 favors amniotomy plus intravenous oxytocin under NICE guidance.\",\"tp\":\"Palpate the presenting part before amniotomy; if the head is high or unstable, reassess because cord prolapse risk rises after membrane rupture.\",\"cn\":\"Before induction of labour, confirm presentation, Bishop score, normal fetal heart rate and the absence of significant contractions.\",\"sl\":\"Assessment before induction: ensure the baby's position and the woman's condition are suitable, record the Bishop score, confirm a normal fetal heart rate pattern on cardiotocography (CTG), and confirm the absence of significant uterine contractions.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F172\"},{\"id\":\"F173\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Cesarean wound dehiscence risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are associated with incision dehiscence except:\",\"o\":[\"Adequate hydration and tissue perfusion\",\"Poorly controlled diabetes mellitus\",\"Obesity\",\"Cigarette smoking\",\"Malnutrition\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maintaining adequate perfusion and hydration supports tissue oxygenation and wound healing; it is not a classic predisposing factor for dehiscence.\",\"Diabetes mellitus impairs leukocyte function and collagen remodeling, increasing wound infection and impaired healing.\",\"Obesity increases wound tension, dead space, and surgical-site complications after abdominal surgery.\",\"Smoking causes vasoconstriction and tissue hypoxia, which impair collagen deposition and wound healing.\",\"Malnutrition, especially protein deficiency, limits collagen synthesis and tissue repair and increases dehiscence risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Wound dehiscence reflects failure of fascial healing and is promoted by infection, poor tissue oxygenation, excessive tension, and impaired collagen formation.\",\"Obesity, diabetes, smoking, and malnutrition all impair one or more of these processes, whereas adequate hydration and perfusion support healing.\",\"Fascial strength initially depends on sutures; later recovery requires fibroblast activity and collagen deposition, so systemic factors that impair repair increase separation risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Wound dehiscence is promoted by infection, poor perfusion, obesity, diabetes, smoking, and malnutrition rather than adequate hydration.\",\"pr\":\"Sudden serosanguineous wound drainage in the early postoperative period can herald fascial separation even before bowel becomes visible.\",\"tp\":\"If fascial dehiscence is suspected, cover exposed viscera with sterile saline-moistened dressings and obtain urgent surgical review.\",\"cn\":\"Wound infection and dehiscence are promoted by obesity, diabetes, smoking and malnutrition; adequate perfusion protects healing.\",\"sl\":\"Caesarean wound: surgical site infection is higher with obesity, diabetes and emergency caesarean section (CS); treat cellulitis with antibiotics and drain collections.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F173\"},{\"id\":\"F174\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cardiac risk predictors in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Not a risk index of cardiac complications on fetus or mother:\",\"o\":[\"Ejection fraction below 40%\",\"Cyanosis\",\"Left-sided heart obstruction\",\"History of a previous child with congenital heart disease\",\"Previous cardiac event\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Reduced left-ventricular systolic function, classically an ejection fraction below about 40%, predicts maternal cardiac complications during pregnancy.\",\"Maternal cyanosis reflects severe underlying heart disease and is associated with substantial maternal and fetal risk.\",\"Left-heart obstruction, such as severe mitral or aortic stenosis, limits the ability to increase cardiac output during pregnancy.\",\"A prior child with congenital heart disease changes fetal recurrence counseling and may prompt targeted fetal echocardiography, but it does not measure the pregnant woman's cardiac functional reserve.\",\"A previous cardiac event, including heart failure or significant arrhythmia, is a major predictor of recurrence with the hemodynamic stress of pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases plasma volume, heart rate, and cardiac output, which can unmask limited cardiovascular reserve.\",\"Risk tools such as CARPREG emphasize maternal findings including prior cardiac events, cyanosis/poor functional status, left-heart obstruction, and ventricular dysfunction.\",\"A previous child with congenital heart disease affects fetal recurrence counseling, but it does not measure the mother's hemodynamic capacity to tolerate pregnancy.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal cardiac risk in pregnancy is driven by the woman’s functional and structural heart disease, not by a previous child’s cardiac diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"Severe mitral stenosis can decompensate as blood volume and heart rate rise because tachycardia shortens diastolic filling time.\",\"tp\":\"Women with significant structural heart disease should have preconception or early-pregnancy multidisciplinary risk assessment rather than relying on a single score.\",\"cn\":\"The cardiac risk index uses prior cardiac event, cyanosis or poor functional class, left heart obstruction and ejection fraction (EF) below 40%.\",\"sl\":\"A risk index of four factors predicts adverse cardiac or neonatal complications: prior cardiac event; cyanosis or poor New York Heart Association (NYHA) class III-IV; left heart obstruction; systemic ventricular dysfunction (ejection fraction [EF] below 40%). Scores 0, 1, more than 1 give 5%, 27%, 75% risk.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F174\"},{\"id\":\"F175\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease microbiology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 27-year-old sexually active patient presents with pelvic pain and is suspected of having pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). Which of the following is the most common cause of PID?\",\"o\":[\"Trichomonas vaginalis\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis\",\"Candida albicans\",\"Gardnerella vaginalis\",\"Herpes simplex virus type 2\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Trichomonas vaginalis causes vaginitis with discharge and may coexist with upper-genital-tract infection; classic PID epidemiology instead centers on ascending chlamydial or gonococcal infection.\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis is a major and often asymptomatic cause of PID; ascending infection can produce endometritis, salpingitis, infertility, and ectopic pregnancy.\",\"Candida albicans causes vulvovaginal candidiasis and does not typically ascend to cause salpingitis in immunocompetent women.\",\"Gardnerella-associated bacterial vaginosis is linked to altered vaginal flora and PID risk, but bacterial vaginosis (BV) is polymicrobial rather than the classic sexually transmitted pathogen requested.\",\"HSV-2 causes genital ulcer disease and neonatal infection risk but is not a common cause of salpingitis or PID.\"],\"e\":[\"PID is an ascending polymicrobial infection of the upper genital tract, and Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae remain important sexually transmitted causes.\",\"In a young sexually active woman with pelvic pain, cervical motion or adnexal tenderness, chlamydial infection is particularly important because it may be minimally symptomatic before tubal damage occurs.\",\"Tubal inflammation can destroy ciliated epithelium and produce adhesions, explaining later infertility, chronic pelvic pain, and ectopic pregnancy.\"],\"tk\":\"Chlamydia trachomatis is a major sexually transmitted cause of PID and can produce silent tubal injury.\",\"pr\":\"Negative cervical nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) testing does not fully exclude upper-genital-tract infection, so treatment of clinically suspected PID should not be delayed when minimum criteria are met.\",\"tp\":\"Obtain pregnancy testing and STI nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), but start empiric broad-spectrum PID treatment when the clinical threshold is met rather than waiting for every laboratory result.\",\"cn\":\"Chlamydia trachomatis is the commonest cause of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) in young sexually active women.\",\"sl\":\"Chlamydia is the most commonly reported curable bacterial sexually transmitted infection (STI); its complications include pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), endometritis, salpingitis, tubal infertility and ectopic pregnancy.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[17,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F056\"},{\"id\":\"F176\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Bacterial vaginosis clue cells\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient presented with vaginal discharge, and her microbiology and culture revealed clue cells, which of the following is the most likely cause?\",\"o\":[\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"Trichomoniasis\",\"Desquamative inflammatory vaginitis\",\"Bacterial vaginosis\",\"Genital herpes\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis typically causes pruritus with budding yeast or pseudohyphae on microscopy rather than clue cells.\",\"Trichomoniasis may cause frothy discharge, vaginal inflammation, and motile trichomonads on wet preparation.\",\"Desquamative inflammatory vaginitis is associated with purulent discharge, elevated pH, parabasal cells, and abundant leukocytes rather than epithelial cells coated with coccobacilli.\",\"Clue cells are vaginal epithelial cells densely coated with adherent bacteria and are a defining Amsel finding for bacterial vaginosis.\",\"Genital herpes classically produces painful vesicles or ulcers; diagnosis relies on lesion nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) rather than vaginal clue cells.\"],\"e\":[\"Bacterial vaginosis is a vaginal dysbiosis characterized by loss of lactobacilli and overgrowth of anaerobic organisms.\",\"Clue cells are squamous epithelial cells coated with bacteria and form one of the Amsel diagnostic criteria, along with thin discharge, elevated pH, and an amine odor.\",\"The altered microbiome raises vaginal pH above 4.5 and increases amine production, creating the characteristic odor and microscopy appearance.\"],\"tk\":\"Clue cells are characteristic of bacterial vaginosis, not candidiasis or trichomoniasis.\",\"pr\":\"Amsel diagnosis requires at least 3 of 4 criteria: homogeneous discharge, pH >4.5, positive whiff test, and clue cells.\",\"tp\":\"When microscopy is available, correlate clue cells with vaginal pH and symptoms; culture of Gardnerella alone is not a useful diagnostic test because it lacks specificity.\",\"cn\":\"Clue cells on microscopy (one of Amsel's criteria) indicate bacterial vaginosis (BV).\",\"sl\":\"Bacterial vaginosis: most common cause of discharge in women of child-bearing age, overgrowth of anaerobes (Gardnerella vaginalis), decreased lactobacilli, pH 4.5-7, fishy discharge. Diagnosis by Amsel's criteria, 3 out of 4.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[3,4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F176\"},{\"id\":\"F177\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":9,\"L\":44,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PCOS and atypical endometrial hyperplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Atypical hyperplasia of endometrium related to:\",\"o\":[\"Subserosal uterine leiomyoma\",\"Combined oral contraceptive use\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"Copper intrauterine device use\",\"Prior tubal ligation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":4,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. A subserosal uterine leiomyoma (fibroid) lies on the outer surface of the uterus and does not expose the endometrium to unopposed estrogen.\",\"Wrong. Combined oral contraceptives contain a progestin that opposes estrogen, and long-term use actually lowers the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.\",\"Correct. Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) causes chronic anovulation, so the endometrium is exposed to estrogen without cyclic progesterone, which drives atypical hyperplasia and endometrioid carcinoma.\",\"Wrong. A copper intrauterine device (IUD) has no hormonal effect on the endometrium and does not cause atypical hyperplasia.\",\"Wrong. Prior tubal ligation blocks the tubes mechanically and does not alter ovulation or create an unopposed estrogen state.\"],\"e\":[\"Atypical endometrial hyperplasia/endometrial intraepithelial neoplasia is linked to prolonged unopposed estrogen stimulation.\",\"In PCOS, chronic anovulation means regular luteal progesterone exposure is absent, allowing persistent endometrial proliferation.\",\"Repeated proliferation without progesterone-mediated differentiation increases glandular crowding and atypia, creating a precursor pathway to endometrioid endometrial carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Chronic anovulation in PCOS increases atypical endometrial hyperplasia risk through unopposed estrogen exposure.\",\"pr\":\"Regular progestin exposure, whether cyclic or via a levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS), protects the endometrium in women with prolonged anovulation.\",\"tp\":\"Ask patients with PCOS about prolonged amenorrhea and abnormal bleeding; persistent or high-risk bleeding requires endometrial evaluation rather than reassurance alone.\",\"cn\":\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) causes chronic anovulation and unopposed oestrogen, leading to endometrial hyperplasia, including atypical hyperplasia.\",\"sl\":\"Anovulatory cycles: unopposed oestrogen gives a thick, fragile endometrium; common at menarche, perimenopause and in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS); long-term risk is endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. Long-term effects of PCOS include endometrial cancer.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[6]],[\"L30_0\",[14]]],\"f\":\"c\",\"n\":\"The QBank marked E (prior tubal ligation), but its own explanation describes polycystic ovary syndrome: chronic anovulation gives unopposed oestrogen, which causes atypical endometrial hyperplasia (Dr. Fida's abnormal uterine bleeding [AUB] discussion, slide 6; PCOS lecture, slide 14). Tubal ligation has no hormonal effect.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F177\"},{\"id\":\"F178\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hypergonadotropic primary amenorrhea\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are associated with high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and absent breast in a pt with primary amenorrhea except:\",\"o\":[\"46,XX pure gonadal dysgenesis\",\"Turner syndrome\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis (Swyer syndrome)\",\"Primary ovarian insufficiency occurring before puberty\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"46,XX gonadal dysgenesis causes hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with elevated FSH and absent or poor breast development.\",\"Turner syndrome produces streak ovaries, low estrogen, and elevated FSH, so absent breast development is expected without treatment.\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome usually has preserved estrogen production and normal breast development; markedly elevated FSH with absent breasts is atypical.\",\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis with a uterus present, high gonadotropins, and absent spontaneous breast development.\",\"Primary ovarian insufficiency before puberty can produce high FSH and failure of spontaneous breast development because ovarian estrogen production is deficient.\"],\"e\":[\"High FSH with absent breast development indicates hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, meaning the gonads are failing to produce adequate estrogen despite pituitary stimulation.\",\"Turner syndrome, Swyer syndrome, 46,XX gonadal dysgenesis, and prepubertal ovarian failure can all create this pattern; PCOS generally does not.\",\"Loss of ovarian negative feedback raises FSH and luteinising hormone (LH), whereas PCOS is characterized by chronic anovulation with ongoing estrogen exposure rather than gonadal failure.\"],\"tk\":\"High FSH plus absent breast development indicates gonadal failure, whereas PCOS usually preserves estrogenization.\",\"pr\":\"In Swyer syndrome, a uterus is present because there is no functional testicular anti-Müllerian hormone production, despite a 46,XY karyotype.\",\"tp\":\"With primary amenorrhea, document breast development and uterine presence before interpreting gonadotropins; those two findings rapidly narrow the differential.\",\"cn\":\"High follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) with absent breasts means gonadal failure (Turner, Swyer, 46,XX dysgenesis); polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) does not cause this.\",\"sl\":\"Absent breast development and high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH): the defect lies in the gonad; karyotype separates Turner syndrome (45,XO), premature ovarian failure (POF, 46,XX) and Swyer syndrome (46,XY). High gonadotropins mean failing gonads.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[5,6]],[\"L28_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F178\"},{\"id\":\"F179\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"When to defer stress incontinence surgery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which one of these is not an indication of conservative management in stress incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Current pregnancy\",\"A remote previous cesarean delivery with complete recovery\",\"Actively attempting conception\",\"Early postpartum recovery\",\"Uncertain completion of childbearing\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"During pregnancy, pelvic floor loading and hormonal changes can alter continence symptoms, so conservative management is preferred and elective continence surgery is deferred.\",\"A healed previous cesarean delivery alone does not create a specific reason to postpone future stress-incontinence surgery when the woman is otherwise an appropriate surgical candidate.\",\"If a woman is trying to conceive, future pregnancy and birth may affect continence outcomes, so pelvic floor therapy is preferred while reproductive plans remain active.\",\"During the early puerperium, pelvic tissues are still recovering and continence may improve spontaneously, making immediate elective surgery inappropriate.\",\"When future childbearing is uncertain, conservative treatment allows symptom control without exposing a repair to the mechanical stresses of a later pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence is initially managed with supervised pelvic floor muscle training, and surgery is considered when symptoms remain bothersome after conservative treatment.\",\"Pregnancy, the early postpartum period, and active fertility plans are reasonable settings to defer elective continence surgery because pelvic support and reproductive plans may change.\",\"A remote cesarean scar does not itself alter the urethral-support mechanism responsible for SUI and is not a standalone indication to postpone treatment.\"],\"tk\":\"Future pregnancy or early postpartum recovery favors conservative SUI management, whereas a remote cesarean history alone does not mandate surgical delay.\",\"pr\":\"Pregnancy after continence surgery can be followed by recurrent stress leakage, so reproductive goals should be part of preoperative counseling.\",\"tp\":\"Before offering surgery, confirm demonstrable stress-predominant leakage, assess postvoid residual when indicated, and document the patient’s future pregnancy plans.\",\"cn\":\"Conservative stress urinary incontinence (SUI) care suits women not finished childbearing or under six months postpartum; remote caesarean is irrelevant.\",\"sl\":\"SUI conservative management is effective, has few complications and does not compromise further surgery; useful in women who are unfit for surgery, have not completed their family, are breastfeeding, or are less than six months postpartum.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[59]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F179\"},{\"id\":\"F180\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Conservative treatment of stress incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are conservative management for stress urinary incontinence (SUI) except:\",\"o\":[\"Increasing caffeine intake\",\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training\",\"Weight reduction when body mass index (BMI) is above 30 kg/m²\",\"Optimizing excessive or inadequate fluid intake\",\"Smoking cessation and treatment of chronic cough\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Higher caffeine intake may worsen lower urinary tract symptoms; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends caffeine reduction for overactive bladder rather than increasing intake as a continence strategy.\",\"At least 3 months of supervised pelvic floor muscle training is first-line treatment for stress or mixed urinary incontinence.\",\"For women with body mass index (BMI) above 30 kg/m², weight reduction can decrease chronic intra-abdominal pressure and improve urinary-incontinence symptoms.\",\"Adjusting very high or very low fluid intake can reduce bladder symptoms without deliberately dehydrating the patient.\",\"Treating chronic cough and stopping smoking reduce repeated rises in intra-abdominal pressure and have broader pelvic-floor and perioperative benefits.\"],\"e\":[\"Conservative management is the first step for most women with stress urinary incontinence.\",\"NICE recommends supervised pelvic floor muscle training for at least 3 months; weight management and sensible fluid habits can also address modifiable contributors.\",\"Caffeine is a bladder stimulant and may worsen urgency/frequency, so increasing caffeine has no therapeutic role in continence care.\"],\"tk\":\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training is first-line treatment for stress urinary incontinence; increasing caffeine is not therapeutic.\",\"pr\":\"A typical PFMT program includes at least 8 contractions three times daily, with supervision helping confirm correct muscle recruitment.\",\"tp\":\"Ask the patient to demonstrate a pelvic floor contraction during examination before prescribing exercises; many women initially recruit abdominal or gluteal muscles instead.\",\"cn\":\"Lifestyle measures for urinary incontinence include caffeine reduction, fluid modification, weight reduction and stopping smoking, plus pelvic floor training.\",\"sl\":\"Lifestyle interventions: caffeine reduction, modify fluid intake, weight reduction, stop smoking; plus pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) of at least 3 months.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[55,56,57]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F180\"},{\"id\":\"F181\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"External cephalic version failure\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"For a term breech fetus undergoing external cephalic version, which outcome is most common?\",\"o\":[\"Emergency cesarean delivery for sustained fetal compromise\",\"Placental abruption\",\"Fetomaternal hemorrhage requiring immediate delivery\",\"Umbilical cord prolapse\",\"Failure to achieve a cephalic presentation\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Sustained fetal compromise requiring emergency cesarean is uncommon when ECV is performed with appropriate selection and monitoring.\",\"Placental abruption is a recognized but rare complication of external manipulation.\",\"Small-volume fetomaternal hemorrhage can occur after external manipulation, which is why anti-D prophylaxis is considered for unsensitized RhD-negative women; clinically significant hemorrhage remains uncommon.\",\"Cord prolapse is uncommon during a planned intact-membrane ECV and is not the usual reason the procedure fails.\",\"The most common unfavorable outcome is simply failed version; success rates are commonly around one-half, varying with parity, placental location, fluid volume, and fetal engagement.\"],\"e\":[\"External cephalic version attempts to rotate a breech fetus to cephalic presentation near term and reduces the likelihood of non-cephalic birth.\",\"Serious complications such as abruption or persistent fetal compromise are uncommon; the most frequent adverse outcome is failure of the version itself.\",\"Success depends on the ability to mobilize the fetus, so multiparity, adequate amniotic fluid, and an unengaged presenting part generally improve the chance of successful rotation.\"],\"tk\":\"Failure to achieve cephalic presentation is the most common unfavorable outcome of ECV; serious complications are uncommon.\",\"pr\":\"Transient fetal heart-rate abnormalities can occur after ECV, which is why fetal assessment before and after the procedure is standard.\",\"tp\":\"Perform ECV where ultrasound, fetal monitoring, and emergency cesarean capability are available, and reassess fetal status after the attempt.\",\"cn\":\"Failed version is the commonest adverse outcome of external cephalic version (ECV); serious complications are uncommon.\",\"sl\":\"The most common risk of external cephalic version (ECV) is failed version; others are compromised umbilical flow, abruption, fetal distress, injury, rupture of membranes and fetomaternal bleeding. Success is 60% in multiparous and 40% ";